window.LEARN_DATA = {"config":{"label":"Exam 4","key":"e4-learn-v1","map":"Exam 4 Map.html","test":"Exam 4 Map.html#test","mhPrefix":"e4-","scope":"The Exam 4 study guide follows your assigned list: Chapters 1, 2, 3 and 6; Chapter 34; and Peplau’s model and Communication Skills for Nurses from Chapter 9. Everything also retains the additional authored lessons.","weightNote":"No professor question-count blueprint is encoded here; recommendations balance chapter coverage.","page":"Exam 4 Learn.html","chapters":["1","2","3","4","5","6","8","9","10","13","14","15","16","25","34"]},"weights":{"1":1,"2":1,"3":1,"4":1,"5":1,"6":1,"8":1,"9":1,"10":1,"13":1,"14":1,"15":1,"16":1,"25":1,"34":1},"lessons":[{"id":"t-intro","ch":"1","chapters":["1"],"title":"Mental health and mental illness","notes":["t-intro"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 1; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Start with the person’s experience and functioning. A diagnosis describes an illness; it does not tell you everything about relationships, strengths, culture or ability to cope today.","Stress, biology, experience and access to support interact. Ask what changed, how the person understands it, and what helps. A cultural difference alone is not evidence of a disorder.","Recovery can mean a meaningful, self-directed life while treatment continues. Nursing care combines safety assessment, practical support, respectful language and goals chosen with the person."],"checks":["Mental illness requires what two things?","Incidence vs prevalence?","What is recovery in mental health?","The most common class of mental disorders?"],"recallAnswers":["A disturbance in thinking, emotion or behavior that causes distress or impaired functioning.","Incidence = new cases over a period. Prevalence = all existing cases at a point in time.","A process toward a self-directed, meaningful life, with hope, even if symptoms remain.","Anxiety disorders."],"requires":[],"terms":["Mental health continuum","Resilience","Recovery","Stigma","psychotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{"Mental health continuum":"The same person can be thriving, struggling, unwell, then recovering over a lifetime. Health and illness are not two boxes: stress, supports, biology and coping shift where someone sits at any moment. You assess where the patient is now and what moves them toward health, rather than labeling them by a diagnosis.","Resilience":"Someone who goes through a divorce or job loss, grieves, and then adapts and rebuilds. The ability to bounce back from adversity, supported by relationships, optimism, problem-solving and a sense of purpose. A protective factor you can build: point out strengths, connect patients to support, teach coping skills.","Recovery":"A person with schizophrenia who lives on their own, works part-time and manages symptoms. Recovery is a process of change in which people improve their health, live self-directed lives and reach their potential, even if some symptoms remain. The modern goal of care: hope, person-centered choices and peer support, not just symptom control.","Stigma":"“He’s crazy.” Avoiding a coworker after learning about their diagnosis. Not seeking help out of shame. Negative stereotypes about mental illness lead to discrimination (public stigma) and to shame that people turn against themselves (self-stigma). A major barrier to treatment. Use person-first language (“a person with bipolar disorder,” not “a bipolar”)."},"questions":[{"id":"q-intro-1","sourceId":"q-intro-1","q":"Which statement best describes mental health?","o":["The absence of a psychiatric diagnosis","A state of well-being in which a person copes with normal stress, works productively and contributes to the community","Feeling happy most of the time","Behavior that matches the majority of people in one’s community"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Mental health is a state of well-being and functioning, not just the absence of illness or constant happiness. Cultural norms matter, but “being like the majority” isn’t the definition.","Supported. Mental health is a state of well-being and functioning, not just the absence of illness or constant happiness. Cultural norms matter, but “being like the majority” isn’t the definition.","Not supported for this question. Mental health is a state of well-being and functioning, not just the absence of illness or constant happiness. Cultural norms matter, but “being like the majority” isn’t the definition.","Not supported for this question. Mental health is a state of well-being and functioning, not just the absence of illness or constant happiness. Cultural norms matter, but “being like the majority” isn’t the definition."],"phys":"Mental health is a state of well-being and functioning, not just the absence of illness or constant happiness. Cultural norms matter, but “being like the majority” isn’t the definition.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-intro-2","sourceId":"q-intro-2","q":"A nurse says, “Recovery means the symptoms are gone for good.” Which response by the charge nurse is most accurate?","o":["“Yes, recovery is the same as cure.”","“Recovery is a process of living a self-directed, meaningful life, even if some symptoms remain.”","“Recovery only applies to substance use.”","“Recovery happens only after hospitalization.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Recovery is a process focused on hope, self-direction and reaching potential; symptoms may persist.","Supported. Recovery is a process focused on hope, self-direction and reaching potential; symptoms may persist.","Not supported for this question. Recovery is a process focused on hope, self-direction and reaching potential; symptoms may persist.","Not supported for this question. Recovery is a process focused on hope, self-direction and reaching potential; symptoms may persist."],"phys":"Recovery is a process focused on hope, self-direction and reaching potential; symptoms may persist.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-intro","active":true,"patient":"A patient receiving treatment for depression is back at work and wants to rebuild friendships. Some symptoms remain.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-culture","ch":"5","chapters":["5"],"title":"Culture and mental health","notes":["t-culture"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 5; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Culture decides what counts as normal, how distress is felt and described, who makes decisions, and what treatment people trust. Psychiatric nursing grew out of Western ideas, so the nurse checks their own assumptions, assesses each patient’s beliefs, and builds a plan that fits them: culturally congruent care.","Three worldviews (broad patterns, not rules): Western: the individual is the source of identity; autonomy; mind and body separate; disease has a measurable cause; time is linear; plan for the future.; Eastern: the family is the source of identity; group decisions; mind–body–spirit unity; illness = imbalance (yin–yang, hot–cold); time is circular.; Indigenous: the community and nature; illness = loss of harmony with others, nature or the spirit world; time is the present.","Beliefs that differ from Western care: Decisions: made by the patient (Western) vs by the family or its head.; Telling the truth: full disclosure vs protecting the patient from bad news to preserve hope.; Sick role: be independent and get moving vs family cares for the sick person, rest helps recovery.; Treatment: prescribed medicines and technology vs balance-restoring remedies and traditional healers.; Pain: rated on a scale vs expressed openly or not at all; felt globally."],"checks":["Who should interpret for a patient with limited English in a psychiatric interview?","A grieving patient reports only chest and stomach pain. Term?","Believing your culture’s way is the only right way?","Campinha-Bacote’s five constructs?"],"recallAnswers":["A professional medical interpreter, never family or friends.","Somatization.","Ethnocentrism.","Awareness, knowledge, encounters, skill, desire."],"requires":[],"terms":["Culture vs ethnicity vs race","Ethnocentrism","Stereotyping","Somatization","Acculturation and assimilation"],"termDefinitions":{"Culture vs ethnicity vs race":"Same race, different cultures: an African American family in the US for generations and a family just arrived from Nigeria. Culture = shared beliefs, values and practices that guide a group. Ethnicity = a shared heritage and history. Race = a social category (now self-identified, often multiple). Don’t assume beliefs from appearance or a census box; ask.","Ethnocentrism":"“I’m sure our treatment will fix this.” “Her family is too involved.” The universal tendency to believe one’s own culture’s way is the only right and natural way. Leads to judging patients and to care they won’t accept. The fix is cultural awareness of your own norms.","Stereotyping":"“All Asian patients are quiet about pain.” Believing every member of a group is the same. Reduced by many cultural encounters: individuals express their culture in their own ways.","Somatization":"A woman grieving her son describes only stomach pain, back pain and fatigue, and denies sadness. Psychological distress is felt and expressed as physical symptoms, common where mind and body are seen as one or where mental illness carries strong stigma. A common cause of missed or wrong diagnoses. Assess for depression behind physical complaints.","Acculturation and assimilation":"Teenagers adopt American norms quickly; grandparents keep traditional ways; conflict at home. Acculturation = learning a new culture’s norms. Assimilation = fully adopting them. Enculturation = learning your own culture growing up. Some people become bicultural; some have culture shock. Children acculturate faster → intergenerational conflict. Assess the stage of acculturation."},"questions":[{"id":"q-cult-2","sourceId":"q-cult-2","q":"A woman from Southeast Asia reports stomach pain and chest discomfort a month after her son died. The nurse suspects:","o":["Vulnerability","Acid reflux","Somatization","Transference"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Somatization: emotional distress expressed as physical symptoms; screen for grief and depression.","Not supported for this question. Somatization: emotional distress expressed as physical symptoms; screen for grief and depression.","Supported. Somatization: emotional distress expressed as physical symptoms; screen for grief and depression.","Not supported for this question. Somatization: emotional distress expressed as physical symptoms; screen for grief and depression."],"phys":"Somatization: emotional distress expressed as physical symptoms; screen for grief and depression.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-cult-3","sourceId":"q-cult-3","q":"Which statements by the nurse support a culturally sensitive assessment? Select all that apply.","o":["“What do you call this illness?”","“What do you think is causing it?”","“How do you think it should be treated?”","“Do you consider this a serious problem?”","“Our medicine works better than home remedies.”"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. Kleinman’s questions explore the patient’s own explanation. The last statement is ethnocentric.","Supported. Kleinman’s questions explore the patient’s own explanation. The last statement is ethnocentric.","Supported. Kleinman’s questions explore the patient’s own explanation. The last statement is ethnocentric.","Supported. Kleinman’s questions explore the patient’s own explanation. The last statement is ethnocentric.","Not supported for this question. Kleinman’s questions explore the patient’s own explanation. The last statement is ethnocentric."],"phys":"Kleinman’s questions explore the patient’s own explanation. The last statement is ethnocentric.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-cult-4","sourceId":"q-cult-4","q":"Intergenerational conflict in immigrant families often happens because acculturation is:","o":["Ignored due to cultural beliefs","Filled with traumatic experiences","Easier for children","A function of assimilation"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Children acculturate faster than older adults, so values clash.","Not supported for this question. Children acculturate faster than older adults, so values clash.","Supported. Children acculturate faster than older adults, so values clash.","Not supported for this question. Children acculturate faster than older adults, so values clash."],"phys":"Children acculturate faster than older adults, so values clash.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-cult-5","sourceId":"q-cult-5","q":"Which nursing actions demonstrate cultural competence? Select all that apply.","o":["Planning mealtimes around the patient’s prayer schedule","Helping the patient visit with the chaplain","Finding out which foods a lacto-ovo vegetarian patient eats","Allowing time for the patient’s spiritual healer to visit","Ordering standard meal trays three times daily"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. Each one adapts care to the patient’s beliefs; a standard tray ignores them.","Supported. Each one adapts care to the patient’s beliefs; a standard tray ignores them.","Supported. Each one adapts care to the patient’s beliefs; a standard tray ignores them.","Supported. Each one adapts care to the patient’s beliefs; a standard tray ignores them.","Not supported for this question. Each one adapts care to the patient’s beliefs; a standard tray ignores them."],"phys":"Each one adapts care to the patient’s beliefs; a standard tray ignores them.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-cult-6","sourceId":"q-cult-6","q":"In Campinha-Bacote’s model, which construct reduces stereotyping through direct interaction with patients of diverse backgrounds?","o":["Cultural awareness","Cultural encounters","Cultural desire","Cultural knowledge"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Many cultural encounters show that individuals express their culture in individual ways.","Supported. Many cultural encounters show that individuals express their culture in individual ways.","Not supported for this question. Many cultural encounters show that individuals express their culture in individual ways.","Not supported for this question. Many cultural encounters show that individuals express their culture in individual ways."],"phys":"Many cultural encounters show that individuals express their culture in individual ways.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-culture"},{"id":"t-dx","ch":"1","chapters":["1","13","14","15","16"],"title":"Diagnostic tools and assessment","notes":["t-dx"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 1 and 13–16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Psychiatric diagnosis rests on criteria, not a lab test. The DSM-5 lists the criteria; the mental status exam is the nurse’s systematic snapshot of how the patient looks, thinks and feels right now; rating scales measure severity and track change. Always ask what medical problem or substance could explain the symptoms.","A “new psychiatric illness” that is really hypothyroidism or delirium will be treated wrongly, and the cause will be missed.","Mental status exam, in order: Appearance: grooming, dress, hygiene, posture, eye contact.; Behavior and motor activity: agitation, retardation, tremors, tics, cooperation.; Speech: rate, volume, quantity (pressured, poverty of speech).; Mood and affect.; Thought process (how they think: logical, tangential, flight of ideas, loose) and thought content (what they think: delusions, obsessions, suicidal or homicidal ideation).; Perception: hallucinations (hearing, seeing, smelling, tasting, feeling things that aren’t there), illusions.; Cognition: orientation, attention, memory, abstract thinking.; Insight and judgment.","Psychosocial assessment: Reason for seeking care, in the patient’s own words.; Past psychiatric history, hospitalizations, suicide attempts, medications.; Substance use; trauma and abuse history.; Family history; relationships, supports, work, finances, housing.; Culture, spirituality, strengths and coping.; Use open-ended questions; a calm, private setting; and tell the patient why you’re asking."],"checks":["Who writes the DSM-5?","Mood or affect: “The patient is smiling while describing her father’s death.”","Thought process vs thought content?","Which labs often rule out a medical cause of depression or anxiety?"],"recallAnswers":["The American Psychiatric Association.","Affect (observed), and it is incongruent with the topic.","Process = how thoughts connect (flight of ideas, tangential). Content = what they are about (delusions, suicidal ideas).","TSH, CBC, BMP, glucose, urine drug screen, B12."],"requires":[],"terms":["DSM-5","Mental status exam (MSE)","Mood vs affect","Insight and judgment"],"termDefinitions":{"DSM-5":"“Major depressive disorder: 5 of 9 symptoms for 2 weeks, causing distress or impairment.” The American Psychiatric Association’s manual of diagnostic criteria, so clinicians everywhere mean the same thing by a diagnosis. Gives the medical diagnosis. Codes match the ICD-10-CM for billing. The nurse writes nursing diagnoses (responses to illness), not DSM diagnoses.","Mental status exam (MSE)":"Appearance, behavior, speech, mood and affect, thought process and content, perception, cognition, insight and judgment. Like a physical assessment for the mind: a structured way to describe what you observe and hear. Document specific observations and quotes (“states ‘the TV is talking to me’”) rather than labels (“psychotic”).","Mood vs affect":"Mood: “I feel empty.” Affect: flat face, monotone voice. Mood is the sustained emotion the patient reports; affect is the emotion the nurse observes. Note whether affect is congruent with mood, and its range: full, constricted, blunted, flat; or labile (rapid shifts).","Insight and judgment":"“There’s nothing wrong with me; my family is the problem.” Insight = awareness of one’s illness and its effects. Judgment = ability to make sound decisions (“What would you do if you found a stamped, addressed envelope?”). Poor insight predicts nonadherence; poor judgment predicts safety risks."},"questions":[{"id":"q-dx-2","sourceId":"q-dx-2","q":"A patient newly diagnosed with depression has fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance and dry skin. Which lab should the nurse expect first?","o":["Serum lithium","TSH","Troponin","Serum ammonia"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. These are signs of hypothyroidism, which can mimic depression. A TSH rules out a medical cause.","Supported. These are signs of hypothyroidism, which can mimic depression. A TSH rules out a medical cause.","Not supported for this question. These are signs of hypothyroidism, which can mimic depression. A TSH rules out a medical cause.","Not supported for this question. These are signs of hypothyroidism, which can mimic depression. A TSH rules out a medical cause."],"phys":"These are signs of hypothyroidism, which can mimic depression. A TSH rules out a medical cause.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-dx-3","sourceId":"q-dx-3","q":"Which parts belong in the mental status exam? Select all that apply.","o":["Appearance and behavior","Mood and affect","Thought process and thought content","Insurance status","Insight and judgment"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. The MSE covers appearance, behavior, speech, mood and affect, thought, perception, cognition, insight and judgment. Insurance is not part of it.","Supported. The MSE covers appearance, behavior, speech, mood and affect, thought, perception, cognition, insight and judgment. Insurance is not part of it.","Supported. The MSE covers appearance, behavior, speech, mood and affect, thought, perception, cognition, insight and judgment. Insurance is not part of it.","Not supported for this question. The MSE covers appearance, behavior, speech, mood and affect, thought, perception, cognition, insight and judgment. Insurance is not part of it.","Supported. The MSE covers appearance, behavior, speech, mood and affect, thought, perception, cognition, insight and judgment. Insurance is not part of it."],"phys":"The MSE covers appearance, behavior, speech, mood and affect, thought, perception, cognition, insight and judgment. Insurance is not part of it.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-e4-dsm-purpose","sourceId":"q-e4-dsm-purpose","q":"A student asks what the DSM contributes to mental health care. Which explanation is most accurate?","o":["A shared set of criteria for classifying mental disorders","A complete nursing care plan for every person with the same diagnosis","A laboratory test that confirms every mental disorder"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. The DSM supplies diagnostic criteria and shared terminology. A diagnostic category does not replace assessment or provide an individualized nursing care plan, and it is not a laboratory test.","Not supported for this question. The DSM supplies diagnostic criteria and shared terminology. A diagnostic category does not replace assessment or provide an individualized nursing care plan, and it is not a laboratory test.","Not supported for this question. The DSM supplies diagnostic criteria and shared terminology. A diagnostic category does not replace assessment or provide an individualized nursing care plan, and it is not a laboratory test."],"phys":"The DSM supplies diagnostic criteria and shared terminology. A diagnostic category does not replace assessment or provide an individualized nursing care plan, and it is not a laboratory test.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-e4-nursing-response","sourceId":"q-e4-nursing-response","q":"The team has identified a mental disorder using diagnostic criteria. When developing the nursing plan, what should the nurse identify in addition to that label?","o":["The individual patient’s responses, needs, risks and measurable outcomes","The same interventions for everyone who has that diagnosis","A diagnosis label that makes further assessment unnecessary"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Nursing care addresses the individual’s responses and needs and uses measurable patient-centered outcomes. A shared diagnosis can support communication, but it does not make every person’s assessment and interventions identical.","Not supported for this question. Nursing care addresses the individual’s responses and needs and uses measurable patient-centered outcomes. A shared diagnosis can support communication, but it does not make every person’s assessment and interventions identical.","Not supported for this question. Nursing care addresses the individual’s responses and needs and uses measurable patient-centered outcomes. A shared diagnosis can support communication, but it does not make every person’s assessment and interventions identical."],"phys":"Nursing care addresses the individual’s responses and needs and uses measurable patient-centered outcomes. A shared diagnosis can support communication, but it does not make every person’s assessment and interventions identical.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-dx"},{"id":"t-e4-diagnostics","ch":"1","chapters":["1"],"title":"Diagnostic tools: criteria and nursing care","notes":["t-dx"],"source":"Chapter 1 diagnostic foundations: focused selection from the existing site-authored DSM and nursing-care material. The broader assessment topic remains available separately.","why":["The DSM is the American Psychiatric Association’s diagnostic classification. Shared criteria help clinicians describe disorders consistently. A category is not a laboratory confirmation or a complete care plan.","Nursing assessment identifies this person’s responses and needs: sleep, coping, daily activity, nutrition, relationships and immediate risks. Two people with the same medical diagnosis can need different nursing plans.","Write an outcome that can be checked. Describe the patient’s action or change, a measurable target and a time frame. Evaluate what actually happened, then revise the plan with the patient."],"checks":["Who writes the DSM-5?","What does nursing care add to the diagnostic label?"],"recallAnswers":["The American Psychiatric Association.","Assessment of the individual’s responses, needs and risks, with patient-centered outcomes."],"requires":[],"terms":["DSM-5"],"termDefinitions":{"DSM-5":"The American Psychiatric Association’s manual of diagnostic criteria, so clinicians everywhere mean the same thing by a diagnosis."},"questions":[{"id":"q-e4-dsm-purpose","sourceId":"q-e4-dsm-purpose","q":"A student asks what the DSM contributes to mental health care. Which explanation is most accurate?","o":["A shared set of criteria for classifying mental disorders","A complete nursing care plan for every person with the same diagnosis","A laboratory test that confirms every mental disorder"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. The DSM supplies diagnostic criteria and shared terminology. A diagnostic category does not replace assessment or provide an individualized nursing care plan, and it is not a laboratory test.","Not supported for this question. The DSM supplies diagnostic criteria and shared terminology. A diagnostic category does not replace assessment or provide an individualized nursing care plan, and it is not a laboratory test.","Not supported for this question. The DSM supplies diagnostic criteria and shared terminology. A diagnostic category does not replace assessment or provide an individualized nursing care plan, and it is not a laboratory test."],"phys":"The DSM supplies diagnostic criteria and shared terminology. A diagnostic category does not replace assessment or provide an individualized nursing care plan, and it is not a laboratory test.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-e4-nursing-response","sourceId":"q-e4-nursing-response","q":"The team has identified a mental disorder using diagnostic criteria. When developing the nursing plan, what should the nurse identify in addition to that label?","o":["The individual patient’s responses, needs, risks and measurable outcomes","The same interventions for everyone who has that diagnosis","A diagnosis label that makes further assessment unnecessary"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Nursing care addresses the individual’s responses and needs and uses measurable patient-centered outcomes. A shared diagnosis can support communication, but it does not make every person’s assessment and interventions identical.","Not supported for this question. Nursing care addresses the individual’s responses and needs and uses measurable patient-centered outcomes. A shared diagnosis can support communication, but it does not make every person’s assessment and interventions identical.","Not supported for this question. Nursing care addresses the individual’s responses and needs and uses measurable patient-centered outcomes. A shared diagnosis can support communication, but it does not make every person’s assessment and interventions identical."],"phys":"Nursing care addresses the individual’s responses and needs and uses measurable patient-centered outcomes. A shared diagnosis can support communication, but it does not make every person’s assessment and interventions identical.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-e4-diagnostics","active":true,"patient":"Two patients have the same diagnostic label. One cannot sleep; the other sleeps adequately but has stopped eating regular meals.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-life","ch":"2","chapters":["2"],"title":"Development across the lifespan","notes":["t-life"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 2; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Developmental theories give you questions to explore; they do not diagnose a person from age alone. Ask about abilities, relationships, experience and what the person is trying to accomplish.","Erikson emphasizes a psychosocial task: trust, autonomy, initiative, industry, identity, intimacy, generativity or integrity. Support a meaningful opportunity to practise the task rather than shaming a difficulty.","Piaget describes forms of thinking, while Kohlberg and Gilligan address moral reasoning from different perspectives. Adapt explanations to demonstrated understanding. The full chapter reference keeps all stages and the additional theories together."],"checks":["A 16-year-old keeps changing friend groups and says, “I don’t know who I am.” Erikson stage?","A 72-year-old says, “I wasted my life.” Which unresolved crisis?","Middle adulthood crisis?","Piaget stage of a 9-year-old?"],"recallAnswers":["Identity vs role confusion.","Integrity vs despair (despair).","Generativity vs self-absorption (stagnation).","Concrete operational: logical about concrete things, not abstract ones yet."],"requires":[],"terms":["psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q-life-1","sourceId":"q-life-1","q":"A 74-year-old patient says, “I made so many mistakes. My life was a waste.” According to Erikson, this reflects:","o":["Generativity vs self-absorption","Integrity vs despair, unresolved","Intimacy vs isolation","Identity vs role confusion"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Later life: integrity vs despair. Regret and hopelessness reflect despair. Explore life review and screen for depression.","Supported. Later life: integrity vs despair. Regret and hopelessness reflect despair. Explore life review and screen for depression.","Not supported for this question. Later life: integrity vs despair. Regret and hopelessness reflect despair. Explore life review and screen for depression.","Not supported for this question. Later life: integrity vs despair. Regret and hopelessness reflect despair. Explore life review and screen for depression."],"phys":"Later life: integrity vs despair. Regret and hopelessness reflect despair. Explore life review and screen for depression.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-life-2","sourceId":"q-life-2","q":"Which developmental task does the nurse expect of a 45-year-old?","o":["Establishing a sense of identity","Developing close, committed relationships","Guiding the next generation and contributing to society","Accepting one’s life and approaching death with dignity"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Middle adulthood (35–65): generativity vs self-absorption (stagnation).","Not supported for this question. Middle adulthood (35–65): generativity vs self-absorption (stagnation).","Supported. Middle adulthood (35–65): generativity vs self-absorption (stagnation).","Not supported for this question. Middle adulthood (35–65): generativity vs self-absorption (stagnation)."],"phys":"Middle adulthood (35–65): generativity vs self-absorption (stagnation).","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-life","active":true,"patient":"A school-age child receiving treatment says, “Everyone else can do their work. I’m bad at everything.”","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-settings","ch":"4","chapters":["4","6"],"title":"History and treatment settings","notes":["t-settings"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 4 and 6; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Care moved from large state hospitals to the community after the first antipsychotics (1950s) and the Community Mental Health Centers Act (1963). Today most people are treated as outpatients; inpatient stays are short and reserved for people who are dangerous to themselves or others or can’t care for themselves. Patients move along a continuum of care to the least restrictive setting that keeps them safe.","Structure lowers anxiety and gives people a safe place to try new behavior.","Milestones: 1950s: chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, makes community living possible.; 1963: Community Mental Health Centers Act → deinstitutionalization.; Unintended results: too few community services, so many people with serious mental illness ended up homeless or incarcerated.; 2008 Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act (in effect 2010) and the Affordable Care Act: insurance must cover mental health and substance use care like physical care.","Outpatient and community settings: Primary care: where most people first get mental health care.; Community mental health centers: therapy, medication management, case management.; Partial hospitalization (PHP): treatment most of the day, home at night; intensive outpatient (IOP): several hours a few days a week.; Assertive community treatment (ACT): a mobile team that brings care to people with serious mental illness wherever they are, on call 24 hours a day.; Psychiatric home care, telehealth, crisis lines (988), peer support, clubhouses, supported housing and employment."],"checks":["Which law started deinstitutionalization?","What is ACT?","What is milieu therapy?","What does mental health parity mean?"],"recallAnswers":["The Community Mental Health Centers Act of 1963.","Assertive community treatment: a mobile, 24/7 team that brings care to people with serious mental illness in the community.","Using the structured, safe unit environment and its routines and relationships as treatment.","Insurance must cover mental health and substance use care on equal terms with physical care."],"requires":[],"terms":["psychotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q-set-1","sourceId":"q-set-1","q":"Which describes assertive community treatment (ACT)?","o":["A locked inpatient unit for long stays","A mobile team that provides care to people with serious mental illness in the community, available 24 hours","A single weekly outpatient therapy session","A self-help group run by peers"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. ACT teams bring intensive, 24/7 care to people with serious mental illness wherever they live.","Supported. ACT teams bring intensive, 24/7 care to people with serious mental illness wherever they live.","Not supported for this question. ACT teams bring intensive, 24/7 care to people with serious mental illness wherever they live.","Not supported for this question. ACT teams bring intensive, 24/7 care to people with serious mental illness wherever they live."],"phys":"ACT teams bring intensive, 24/7 care to people with serious mental illness wherever they live.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-set-2","sourceId":"q-set-2","q":"Which event led to deinstitutionalization in the United States?","o":["The HIPAA Privacy Rule","The Community Mental Health Centers Act of 1963","The Tarasoff ruling","The Mental Health Parity Act"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. The 1963 Act moved care from state hospitals to community mental health centers (helped by the first antipsychotics in the 1950s).","Supported. The 1963 Act moved care from state hospitals to community mental health centers (helped by the first antipsychotics in the 1950s).","Not supported for this question. The 1963 Act moved care from state hospitals to community mental health centers (helped by the first antipsychotics in the 1950s).","Not supported for this question. The 1963 Act moved care from state hospitals to community mental health centers (helped by the first antipsychotics in the 1950s)."],"phys":"The 1963 Act moved care from state hospitals to community mental health centers (helped by the first antipsychotics in the 1950s).","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-settings"},{"id":"t-legal","ch":"6","chapters":["6"],"title":"Legal and ethical care","notes":["t-legal"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 6; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Begin with rights, current risk and the least restrictive effective response. Admission status does not by itself remove every right. Consent, capacity, emergency authority and discharge processes depend on the applicable law and policy.","Restraint or seclusion is a safety intervention with strict requirements, never a shortcut for convenience, punishment or ordinary disagreement. Assess alternatives, obtain required authorization, monitor and end the restriction as soon as it is no longer needed.","For U.S. hospital restraint or seclusion for violent or self-destructive behavior, federal order-renewal limits distinguish adults and children. These limits are not permission to continue until the clock expires. Nonviolent restraint rules and stricter state requirements must be checked separately. Use the reference for the chapter’s legal terms and ethics comparisons."],"checks":["A voluntary patient asks to leave. First action?","Restraint order limit for an adult?","Can an involuntarily committed patient refuse medication?","Telling a patient “Take this or I’ll get the staff to hold you down” is which tort?"],"recallAnswers":["Notify the provider for reevaluation; the patient has the right to request discharge (staff may start a hold only if the patient is dangerous).","4 hours (2 for ages 9–17, 1 for under 9), renewable up to 24 hours.","Yes, except in an emergency or after a court finds them incompetent.","Assault (a threat). Actually forcing it without grounds = battery."],"requires":[],"terms":["Least restrictive alternative","Competency vs capacity","Duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff)"],"termDefinitions":{"Least restrictive alternative":"Treating depression as an outpatient rather than in the hospital; verbal de-escalation before medication, and medication before restraint. Patients have a right to treatment that limits their freedom only as much as safety requires. Always try and document less-restrictive measures first, unless the emergency is too acute.","Competency vs capacity":"A judge declares a person incompetent and appoints a guardian; a psychiatrist judges that a patient can’t weigh the risks of ECT today. Competency is a legal status decided by a court; capacity is a clinical judgment about a specific decision, and it can change hour to hour. Patients are presumed competent. Involuntary admission does not by itself make someone incompetent; they keep the right to consent to or refuse treatment.","Duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff)":"A patient says he will kill his ex-girlfriend when he gets out. The Tarasoff rulings (California, 1974 and 1976): when a patient makes a serious threat against an identifiable person, the duty to protect that person outweighs confidentiality. Report the threat to the treatment team and provider immediately; the clinician warns the victim or police per state law."},"questions":[{"id":"q-legal-2","sourceId":"q-legal-2","q":"An adult patient is placed in restraints for violent behavior. Which actions are correct? Select all that apply.","o":["Obtain a provider order, which lasts up to 4 hours for an adult","Write the order as PRN for future episodes","Observe the patient continuously","Offer fluids and toileting and check circulation at regular intervals","Release as soon as the patient can safely be released"],"a":[0,2,3,4],"r":["Supported. Orders are never PRN or standing. Adult orders last 4 hours (renewable up to 24). Continuous observation, regular checks and the earliest safe release are required.","Not supported for this question. Orders are never PRN or standing. Adult orders last 4 hours (renewable up to 24). Continuous observation, regular checks and the earliest safe release are required.","Supported. Orders are never PRN or standing. Adult orders last 4 hours (renewable up to 24). Continuous observation, regular checks and the earliest safe release are required.","Supported. Orders are never PRN or standing. Adult orders last 4 hours (renewable up to 24). Continuous observation, regular checks and the earliest safe release are required.","Supported. Orders are never PRN or standing. Adult orders last 4 hours (renewable up to 24). Continuous observation, regular checks and the earliest safe release are required."],"phys":"Orders are never PRN or standing. Adult orders last 4 hours (renewable up to 24). Continuous observation, regular checks and the earliest safe release are required.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-legal-3","sourceId":"q-legal-3","q":"A voluntarily admitted patient demands to be discharged. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Tell the patient he must complete treatment first","Notify the provider so the patient can be evaluated","Place the patient in seclusion","Call security to escort him off the unit"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Voluntary patients may request release. The provider evaluates; a hold is possible only if he is dangerous. Holding him without grounds = false imprisonment.","Supported. Voluntary patients may request release. The provider evaluates; a hold is possible only if he is dangerous. Holding him without grounds = false imprisonment.","Not supported for this question. Voluntary patients may request release. The provider evaluates; a hold is possible only if he is dangerous. Holding him without grounds = false imprisonment.","Not supported for this question. Voluntary patients may request release. The provider evaluates; a hold is possible only if he is dangerous. Holding him without grounds = false imprisonment."],"phys":"Voluntary patients may request release. The provider evaluates; a hold is possible only if he is dangerous. Holding him without grounds = false imprisonment.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-legal-4","sourceId":"q-legal-4","q":"A patient tells the nurse he plans to kill his neighbor when discharged. The priority action is to:","o":["Keep the information confidential","Report it to the treatment team and provider immediately","Document it and reassess tomorrow","Ask the patient to promise not to do it"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. A serious threat against an identifiable person triggers the duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff), an exception to confidentiality.","Supported. A serious threat against an identifiable person triggers the duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff), an exception to confidentiality.","Not supported for this question. A serious threat against an identifiable person triggers the duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff), an exception to confidentiality.","Not supported for this question. A serious threat against an identifiable person triggers the duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff), an exception to confidentiality."],"phys":"A serious threat against an identifiable person triggers the duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff), an exception to confidentiality.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-legal-5","sourceId":"q-legal-5","q":"Which situation is false imprisonment?","o":["Restraining a patient who is attacking staff, with an order","Keeping a voluntary patient on a locked unit after he asks to leave, with no legal hold","Placing a patient on 1:1 observation for suicide risk","Asking a patient to stay in the dayroom during room checks"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Confining someone without legal grounds is false imprisonment.","Supported. Confining someone without legal grounds is false imprisonment.","Not supported for this question. Confining someone without legal grounds is false imprisonment.","Not supported for this question. Confining someone without legal grounds is false imprisonment."],"phys":"Confining someone without legal grounds is false imprisonment.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-legal-6","sourceId":"q-legal-6","q":"The nurse explains the side effects of a new antidepressant honestly, including sexual dysfunction. Which ethical principle is the nurse demonstrating?","o":["Justice","Fidelity","Veracity","Beneficence"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Veracity = truthfulness.","Not supported for this question. Veracity = truthfulness.","Supported. Veracity = truthfulness.","Not supported for this question. Veracity = truthfulness."],"phys":"Veracity = truthfulness.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-legal","active":true,"patient":"An adult voluntary patient asks to leave. The nurse needs to assess the request, the patient’s condition and the applicable discharge process.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-theories","ch":"2","chapters":["2"],"title":"Theories and therapies","notes":["t-theories"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 2; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["A theory is a lens for understanding a problem. Psychodynamic approaches explore unconscious patterns; interpersonal approaches examine relationships; behavioral approaches focus on learning; cognitive approaches examine interpretations.","Match the intervention to the lens. Practising a skill or reinforcing a behavior differs from examining the thought that precedes it. DBT combines acceptance and change with skills such as distress tolerance and emotion regulation.","Physiological needs and immediate safety can change what is useful now. A patient who is exhausted or medically unstable needs that problem addressed before a demanding insight exercise. Theories support assessment; they do not replace it."],"checks":["Which part of personality is the conscience?","Peplau’s main contribution?","Systematic desensitization vs flooding?","Which therapy for self-harm and emotion dysregulation?"],"recallAnswers":["The superego.","The nurse–patient relationship as therapy, with its phases, and the levels of anxiety.","Desensitization = gradual exposure with relaxation; flooding = intense, all-at-once exposure.","DBT."],"requires":[],"terms":["disulfiram"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q-theory-1","sourceId":"q-theory-1","q":"A patient with a fear of elevators first looks at pictures of elevators, then stands near one, then rides one floor, using relaxation at each step. This is:","o":["Flooding","Aversion therapy","Systematic desensitization","Psychoanalysis"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Graded exposure paired with relaxation = systematic desensitization. Flooding is intense, all-at-once exposure.","Not supported for this question. Graded exposure paired with relaxation = systematic desensitization. Flooding is intense, all-at-once exposure.","Supported. Graded exposure paired with relaxation = systematic desensitization. Flooding is intense, all-at-once exposure.","Not supported for this question. Graded exposure paired with relaxation = systematic desensitization. Flooding is intense, all-at-once exposure."],"phys":"Graded exposure paired with relaxation = systematic desensitization. Flooding is intense, all-at-once exposure.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-theory-2","sourceId":"q-theory-2","q":"Using Maslow’s hierarchy, which need should the nurse address first in a newly admitted patient with acute mania?","o":["Self-esteem","Belonging in group therapy","Nutrition, fluids and rest","Insight into the illness"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Physiological needs come first; a manic patient who hasn’t eaten or slept is at physical risk.","Not supported for this question. Physiological needs come first; a manic patient who hasn’t eaten or slept is at physical risk.","Supported. Physiological needs come first; a manic patient who hasn’t eaten or slept is at physical risk.","Not supported for this question. Physiological needs come first; a manic patient who hasn’t eaten or slept is at physical risk."],"phys":"Physiological needs come first; a manic patient who hasn’t eaten or slept is at physical risk.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-theories","active":true,"patient":"A patient avoids a common room because one awkward conversation led to the thought, “Everyone will reject me.”","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-neuro","ch":"3","chapters":["3"],"title":"The brain and psych meds","notes":["t-neuro"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 3; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Neurons communicate through chemical signals acting at receptors. Drugs can change signaling in several ways. An agonist activates a receptor; an antagonist blocks its activation. A partial agonist is not the same as a pure blocker.","A receptor’s location helps explain an effect. Blocking histamine H1 can cause sedation; muscarinic blockade can produce dry mouth and constipation; alpha-1 blockade can contribute to orthostatic symptoms. Actual risks vary by drug and dose.","Connect the mechanism to assessment and teaching, then use the specific medication’s precautions. Transmitter charts are simplified learning models, not tests for a diagnosis or proof that one chemical alone causes an illness. The chapter reference retains the brain areas, drug classes, monitoring and interactions."],"checks":["Benzodiazepines enhance which neurotransmitter?","Blocking which receptor causes orthostatic hypotension?","Which dopamine pathway causes EPS when blocked?","Which antidepressant is contraindicated with a seizure disorder?"],"recallAnswers":["GABA.","Alpha-1.","Nigrostriatal.","Bupropion."],"requires":[],"terms":["Agonist vs antagonist","Reuptake","Pharmacokinetics vs pharmacodynamics","Narrow therapeutic index","Pharmacogenetics (CYP450)","clozapine","olanzapine","aripiprazole","sertraline","citalopram","venlafaxine","bupropion","lithium","valproate","carbamazepine","lamotrigine","methylphenidate","atomoxetine","donepezil","rivastigmine","memantine","diazepam","lorazepam","clonazepam","alprazolam","haloperidol","fluoxetine","paroxetine","duloxetine","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Agonist vs antagonist":"A benzodiazepine boosts GABA; an antipsychotic blocks dopamine D2 receptors. An agonist binds and stimulates a receptor (a partial agonist stimulates it less). An antagonist binds and blocks it so the natural neurotransmitter can’t act. Tells you the direction of the effect, and so the side effects: blocking a receptor removes everything that receptor normally does.","Reuptake":"SSRIs, SNRIs, TCAs and bupropion are all “reuptake inhibitors.” After signaling, monoamines (serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine) are pulled back into the sending neuron, where monoamine oxidase (MAO) breaks them down. Blocking reuptake, or blocking MAO, leaves more neurotransmitter in the synapse. Why stacking two serotonin-raising drugs (e.g., an SSRI + an MAOI) causes serotonin syndrome.","Pharmacokinetics vs pharmacodynamics":"Lithium rises when the kidneys slow down; an SSRI causes nausea through 5-HT3 receptors. Pharmacokinetics = what the body does to the drug: Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion (ADME). Pharmacodynamics = what the drug does to the body: its action and responses. Kidney or liver disease, age and drug interactions change kinetics, so the level and the toxicity risk change.","Narrow therapeutic index":"Lithium: a therapeutic level and a toxic level are close together. The therapeutic index is the ratio of the lethal (or toxic) dose to the effective dose. Narrow = little room for error. Needs blood level monitoring; anything that changes excretion (dehydration, NSAIDs, thiazides, ACE inhibitors) can push it into toxicity.","Pharmacogenetics (CYP450)":"Two patients on the same dose: one has heavy side effects, the other no benefit. Genetic variations in liver cytochrome P450 enzymes make people poor metabolizers (high levels, more side effects) or rapid metabolizers (low levels, less effect). Consider it when a drug doesn’t work as expected. HLA-B1502 (more common in people of Asian ancestry) → test before carbamazepine (SJS/TEN risk)."},"questions":[{"id":"q-neuro-2","sourceId":"q-neuro-2","q":"A patient with a seizure disorder is starting an antidepressant. Which one is contraindicated?","o":["Bupropion","Mirtazapine","Paroxetine","Venlafaxine"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold (also avoid it in eating disorders and abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal).","Not supported for this question. Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold (also avoid it in eating disorders and abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal).","Not supported for this question. Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold (also avoid it in eating disorders and abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal).","Not supported for this question. Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold (also avoid it in eating disorders and abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal)."],"phys":"Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold (also avoid it in eating disorders and abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal).","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-3","sourceId":"q-neuro-3","q":"A patient taking trazodone gets dizzy when standing. Blockade of which receptor explains this?","o":["Alpha-1","Dopamine-2","Histamine-1","Muscarinic-1"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Alpha-1 blockade dilates blood vessels → orthostatic hypotension. Teach to rise slowly.","Not supported for this question. Alpha-1 blockade dilates blood vessels → orthostatic hypotension. Teach to rise slowly.","Not supported for this question. Alpha-1 blockade dilates blood vessels → orthostatic hypotension. Teach to rise slowly.","Not supported for this question. Alpha-1 blockade dilates blood vessels → orthostatic hypotension. Teach to rise slowly."],"phys":"Alpha-1 blockade dilates blood vessels → orthostatic hypotension. Teach to rise slowly.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-4","sourceId":"q-neuro-4","q":"Which mood stabilizer most commonly causes hypothyroidism with long-term use?","o":["Carbamazepine","Lamotrigine","Lithium","Valproate"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Lithium interferes with thyroid hormone; check TSH at baseline and periodically.","Not supported for this question. Lithium interferes with thyroid hormone; check TSH at baseline and periodically.","Supported. Lithium interferes with thyroid hormone; check TSH at baseline and periodically.","Not supported for this question. Lithium interferes with thyroid hormone; check TSH at baseline and periodically."],"phys":"Lithium interferes with thyroid hormone; check TSH at baseline and periodically.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-5","sourceId":"q-neuro-5","q":"A patient on lamotrigine is started on another medication. Which one increases lamotrigine levels and the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","o":["Carbamazepine","Lithium","Quetiapine","Valproate"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Valproate raises lamotrigine levels, so the lamotrigine dose is halved.","Not supported for this question. Valproate raises lamotrigine levels, so the lamotrigine dose is halved.","Not supported for this question. Valproate raises lamotrigine levels, so the lamotrigine dose is halved.","Supported. Valproate raises lamotrigine levels, so the lamotrigine dose is halved."],"phys":"Valproate raises lamotrigine levels, so the lamotrigine dose is halved.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-6","sourceId":"q-neuro-6","q":"Extrapyramidal symptoms from antipsychotics come from dopamine blockade in which pathway?","o":["Mesocortical","Mesolimbic","Nigrostriatal","Tuberoinfundibular"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Nigrostriatal = movement → EPS. Mesolimbic = the antipsychotic effect; tuberoinfundibular = prolactin.","Not supported for this question. Nigrostriatal = movement → EPS. Mesolimbic = the antipsychotic effect; tuberoinfundibular = prolactin.","Supported. Nigrostriatal = movement → EPS. Mesolimbic = the antipsychotic effect; tuberoinfundibular = prolactin.","Not supported for this question. Nigrostriatal = movement → EPS. Mesolimbic = the antipsychotic effect; tuberoinfundibular = prolactin."],"phys":"Nigrostriatal = movement → EPS. Mesolimbic = the antipsychotic effect; tuberoinfundibular = prolactin.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-7","sourceId":"q-neuro-7","q":"Which second-generation antipsychotic requires routine absolute neutrophil count (ANC) monitoring?","o":["Brexpiprazole","Clozapine","Risperidone","Ziprasidone"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Clozapine can cause life-threatening neutropenia: ANC weekly for 6 months, every 2 weeks for 6 months, then monthly.","Supported. Clozapine can cause life-threatening neutropenia: ANC weekly for 6 months, every 2 weeks for 6 months, then monthly.","Not supported for this question. Clozapine can cause life-threatening neutropenia: ANC weekly for 6 months, every 2 weeks for 6 months, then monthly.","Not supported for this question. Clozapine can cause life-threatening neutropenia: ANC weekly for 6 months, every 2 weeks for 6 months, then monthly."],"phys":"Clozapine can cause life-threatening neutropenia: ANC weekly for 6 months, every 2 weeks for 6 months, then monthly.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-8","sourceId":"q-neuro-8","q":"Which drug is FDA approved specifically for postpartum depression?","o":["Brexanolone","Esketamine","Fluoxetine","Levomilnacipran"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Brexanolone: a 60-hour IV infusion with continuous pulse oximetry; the mother is accompanied while with her child.","Not supported for this question. Brexanolone: a 60-hour IV infusion with continuous pulse oximetry; the mother is accompanied while with her child.","Not supported for this question. Brexanolone: a 60-hour IV infusion with continuous pulse oximetry; the mother is accompanied while with her child.","Not supported for this question. Brexanolone: a 60-hour IV infusion with continuous pulse oximetry; the mother is accompanied while with her child."],"phys":"Brexanolone: a 60-hour IV infusion with continuous pulse oximetry; the mother is accompanied while with her child.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-9","sourceId":"q-neuro-9","q":"Which part of the brain coordinates voluntary motor movement and balance?","o":["Brainstem","Cerebellum","Cerebrum","Hypothalamus"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. The cerebellum smooths and coordinates movement and balance (why sedatives cause ataxia).","Supported. The cerebellum smooths and coordinates movement and balance (why sedatives cause ataxia).","Not supported for this question. The cerebellum smooths and coordinates movement and balance (why sedatives cause ataxia).","Not supported for this question. The cerebellum smooths and coordinates movement and balance (why sedatives cause ataxia)."],"phys":"The cerebellum smooths and coordinates movement and balance (why sedatives cause ataxia).","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-10","sourceId":"q-neuro-10","q":"A patient on a first-generation antipsychotic has dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation and urinary hesitancy. These effects come from blocking which receptors?","o":["Histamine-1","Muscarinic (acetylcholine)","Alpha-1","Serotonin 2A"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Muscarinic (M1) blockade = anticholinergic effects. Tolerance to these usually does not develop.","Supported. Muscarinic (M1) blockade = anticholinergic effects. Tolerance to these usually does not develop.","Not supported for this question. Muscarinic (M1) blockade = anticholinergic effects. Tolerance to these usually does not develop.","Not supported for this question. Muscarinic (M1) blockade = anticholinergic effects. Tolerance to these usually does not develop."],"phys":"Muscarinic (M1) blockade = anticholinergic effects. Tolerance to these usually does not develop.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-11","sourceId":"q-neuro-11","q":"Which teaching points apply to a patient taking lithium? Select all that apply.","o":["Ibuprofen and hydrochlorothiazide can raise your lithium level","Large amounts of caffeine can lower your lithium level","Lithium has a wide margin of safety","Report vomiting or diarrhea to your provider","Your thyroid and kidney function will be checked"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Supported. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index. NSAIDs, thiazides and ACE inhibitors raise it; caffeine lowers it; fluid loss raises it.","Supported. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index. NSAIDs, thiazides and ACE inhibitors raise it; caffeine lowers it; fluid loss raises it.","Not supported for this question. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index. NSAIDs, thiazides and ACE inhibitors raise it; caffeine lowers it; fluid loss raises it.","Supported. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index. NSAIDs, thiazides and ACE inhibitors raise it; caffeine lowers it; fluid loss raises it.","Supported. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index. NSAIDs, thiazides and ACE inhibitors raise it; caffeine lowers it; fluid loss raises it."],"phys":"Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index. NSAIDs, thiazides and ACE inhibitors raise it; caffeine lowers it; fluid loss raises it.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-neuro-12","sourceId":"q-neuro-12","q":"Pharmacokinetics describes:","o":["What the drug does to the body","How the body absorbs, distributes, metabolizes and excretes the drug","The drug’s receptor binding","The patient’s genetic risk of illness"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Kinetics = ADME (what the body does to the drug). Dynamics = what the drug does to the body.","Supported. Kinetics = ADME (what the body does to the drug). Dynamics = what the drug does to the body.","Not supported for this question. Kinetics = ADME (what the body does to the drug). Dynamics = what the drug does to the body.","Not supported for this question. Kinetics = ADME (what the body does to the drug). Dynamics = what the drug does to the body."],"phys":"Kinetics = ADME (what the body does to the drug). Dynamics = what the drug does to the body.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-neuro","active":true,"patient":"After starting a prescribed psychiatric medicine, a patient reports dry mouth and dizziness when standing.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-relationship","ch":"8","chapters":["8"],"title":"The therapeutic relationship","notes":["t-relationship"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 8; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["A therapeutic relationship exists for the patient’s needs, has a goal and an end, and the nurse uses self-awareness to keep it that way. It differs from a social relationship, where both people’s needs are met and advice and personal stories flow both ways.","Termination starts in orientation: telling the patient the end date up front keeps the ending from feeling like abandonment.","Peplau’s phases: Preorientation: before meeting. Gather data, and do a self-assessment of your own feelings and biases.; Orientation: introductions; set the contract: purpose, time, place, how often, confidentiality and its limits, and when the relationship ends. Build trust; the patient may test you (lateness, limit testing).; Working: explore thoughts, feelings and stressors; problem-solve; practice new coping; transference and resistance often appear.; Termination: review progress and goals, summarize, plan follow-up; talk about feelings of loss. The patient may regress or be angry. The end date was planned from the start.","Factors that help the relationship: Consistency and reliability (be on time, keep promises).; Genuineness, empathy, positive regard (accepting the person even when not accepting the behavior).; Active listening, attention to nonverbal cues, cultural humility.; Self-awareness: the Johari window (open, hidden, blind and unknown parts of the self)."],"checks":["In which phase is the termination date set?","A nurse feels protective of a patient who looks like her daughter. Term?","Who is responsible for keeping professional boundaries?","The working phase focuses on what?"],"recallAnswers":["The orientation phase (as part of the contract).","Countertransference.","Always the nurse.","Exploring stressors and feelings, problem-solving, and practicing new coping behaviors."],"requires":[],"terms":["Transference","Countertransference","Boundary crossing","Empathy vs sympathy"],"termDefinitions":{"Transference":"A patient treats a nurse with anger because the nurse “acts just like my mother.” The patient unconsciously moves feelings about someone from their past onto the nurse. Expected. Don’t take it personally; explore it, and keep limits clear.","Countertransference":"A nurse feels unusual irritation with a patient who reminds her of her ex, or tries to rescue a patient who reminds her of her son. The nurse’s own unconscious feelings about someone from their past get moved onto the patient. A nurse’s problem to recognize and manage: reflect, talk to a supervisor, and switch assignments if needed.","Boundary crossing":"Sharing a lot of personal information, accepting gifts, spending far more time with one patient, keeping secrets, meeting outside work. The relationship slowly shifts toward the nurse’s needs, or toward a social relationship. Ask: “Whose needs am I meeting?” Bring it to your supervisor; it is always the nurse’s responsibility to keep boundaries.","Empathy vs sympathy":"Empathy: “It sounds like you feel trapped.” Sympathy: “I feel so sorry for you; I cried when my dad died too.” Empathy is understanding the patient’s feelings from their point of view while staying separate; sympathy is sharing the feelings, which shifts the focus to the nurse. Use empathy: it keeps the focus on the patient and keeps you objective."},"questions":[{"id":"q-rel-2","sourceId":"q-rel-2","q":"A nurse notices she is spending much more time with one patient than with others and has shared details of her own divorce. What is happening?","o":["Transference","Boundary crossing","Termination","Empathy"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Extra time and excessive self-disclosure are signs of boundary crossing: the relationship is drifting toward the nurse’s needs. The nurse is responsible for refocusing and seeking supervision.","Supported. Extra time and excessive self-disclosure are signs of boundary crossing: the relationship is drifting toward the nurse’s needs. The nurse is responsible for refocusing and seeking supervision.","Not supported for this question. Extra time and excessive self-disclosure are signs of boundary crossing: the relationship is drifting toward the nurse’s needs. The nurse is responsible for refocusing and seeking supervision.","Not supported for this question. Extra time and excessive self-disclosure are signs of boundary crossing: the relationship is drifting toward the nurse’s needs. The nurse is responsible for refocusing and seeking supervision."],"phys":"Extra time and excessive self-disclosure are signs of boundary crossing: the relationship is drifting toward the nurse’s needs. The nurse is responsible for refocusing and seeking supervision.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-rel-3","sourceId":"q-rel-3","q":"A patient becomes angry at a nurse, saying, “You’re bossy just like my mother.” The nurse recognizes this as:","o":["Countertransference","Transference","Projection by the nurse","Splitting"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. The patient transfers feelings about someone from the past onto the nurse = transference.","Supported. The patient transfers feelings about someone from the past onto the nurse = transference.","Not supported for this question. The patient transfers feelings about someone from the past onto the nurse = transference.","Not supported for this question. The patient transfers feelings about someone from the past onto the nurse = transference."],"phys":"The patient transfers feelings about someone from the past onto the nurse = transference.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-e4-peplau-working","sourceId":"q-e4-peplau-working","q":"The nurse and patient identify situations that trigger distress and rehearse a coping skill together. Which phase of their therapeutic relationship is this?","o":["Preorientation","Orientation","Working","Termination"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient.","Not supported for this question. The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient.","Supported. The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient.","Not supported for this question. The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient."],"phys":"The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-relationship"},{"id":"t-e4-peplau","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Peplau’s Model of the Nurse-Patient Relationship","notes":["t-relationship"],"source":"Assigned Chapter 9 section: Peplau’s model. Uses the existing site-authored phase card; complete relationship notes are stored under Chapter 8.","why":["Prepare before meeting: review relevant information and notice your own assumptions. In orientation, introduce the role and agree on purpose, boundaries, time and confidentiality limits. Establish the planned ending early.","Working is the active middle of the relationship. Explore concerns, connect patterns, practise coping and work toward the patient’s goals. The nurse uses a professional relationship to support the patient’s work.","Termination reviews progress, discusses feelings about ending and plans the next support. Mentioning the end date during orientation is preparation for termination, not evidence that the relationship is already ending."],"checks":["In which phase is the termination date set?","The working phase focuses on what?","What do you do before meeting the patient?","What belongs in termination?"],"recallAnswers":["The orientation phase (as part of the contract).","Exploring stressors and feelings, problem-solving, and practicing new coping behaviors.","Review available information and examine your own feelings and assumptions.","Review progress and goals, discuss the ending, and plan follow-up."],"requires":[],"terms":[],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q-rel-1","sourceId":"q-rel-1","q":"In which phase of the nurse–patient relationship are the terms of the contract, including when the relationship will end, established?","o":["Preorientation","Orientation","Working","Termination"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date.","Supported. The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date.","Not supported for this question. The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date.","Not supported for this question. The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date."],"phys":"The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-e4-peplau-working","sourceId":"q-e4-peplau-working","q":"The nurse and patient identify situations that trigger distress and rehearse a coping skill together. Which phase of their therapeutic relationship is this?","o":["Preorientation","Orientation","Working","Termination"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient.","Not supported for this question. The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient.","Supported. The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient.","Not supported for this question. The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient."],"phys":"The working phase uses the established relationship to explore problems and practise coping. Orientation establishes the relationship and its agreement; termination reviews progress and closes it. Preorientation is preparation before meeting the patient.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-e4-peplau","active":true,"patient":"You will meet with a patient during this admission, practise a coping skill together and end your work when the placement finishes.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-comm","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Communication Skills for Nurses","notes":["t-comm"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 9; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Listen for what the patient is communicating, then choose a response that fits the task. Open invitations, silence, observations, clarification and reflection can help the person explain an experience in their own words.","False reassurance, rapid questioning, advice and judgment can close exploration. Avoid assuming a question is therapeutic just because it begins with an open word; consider its tone, timing and effect.","A safety cue changes the task. A direct question about harm can be more appropriate than a broad invitation when risk needs clarification. With a delusion or hallucination, acknowledge distress without endorsing the belief, and assess the actual safety concern."],"checks":["Patient: “I’m worried about my surgery.” Nurse: “Don’t worry, you have a great surgeon.” Block?","“What do you think you should do?” is which technique?","“You seem upset today” is which technique?","Better than “Why didn’t you take your pills?”"],"recallAnswers":["False reassurance (and defending).","Reflecting (the question back to the patient).","Making observations.","“Tell me what happened with your medications.” (Open-ended, no blame.)"],"requires":[],"terms":["psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q-comm-2","sourceId":"q-comm-2","q":"Which nurse statements are nontherapeutic? Select all that apply.","o":["“Why did you stop taking your medication?”","“Tell me more about what happened.”","“Don’t worry, everything will be fine.”","“If I were you, I would call your sister.”","“You seem upset.”"],"a":[0,2,3],"r":["Supported. A “why” question, false reassurance and advice are blocks. Exploring and making observations are therapeutic.","Not supported for this question. A “why” question, false reassurance and advice are blocks. Exploring and making observations are therapeutic.","Supported. A “why” question, false reassurance and advice are blocks. Exploring and making observations are therapeutic.","Supported. A “why” question, false reassurance and advice are blocks. Exploring and making observations are therapeutic.","Not supported for this question. A “why” question, false reassurance and advice are blocks. Exploring and making observations are therapeutic."],"phys":"A “why” question, false reassurance and advice are blocks. Exploring and making observations are therapeutic.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-comm-3","sourceId":"q-comm-3","q":"A patient says, “The CIA is listening through the TV.” The best response is:","o":["“That’s impossible. The TV is just a TV.”","“What is the CIA saying to you?”","“That must be frightening. I don’t see any evidence of that, but I’ll stay with you.”","“I’ll unplug the TV so they can’t hear you.”"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Acknowledge the feeling, present reality without arguing, and offer support. Don’t argue, explore the delusion’s content, or act as if it’s real.","Not supported for this question. Acknowledge the feeling, present reality without arguing, and offer support. Don’t argue, explore the delusion’s content, or act as if it’s real.","Supported. Acknowledge the feeling, present reality without arguing, and offer support. Don’t argue, explore the delusion’s content, or act as if it’s real.","Not supported for this question. Acknowledge the feeling, present reality without arguing, and offer support. Don’t argue, explore the delusion’s content, or act as if it’s real."],"phys":"Acknowledge the feeling, present reality without arguing, and offer support. Don’t argue, explore the delusion’s content, or act as if it’s real.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-comm","active":true,"patient":"A patient looks down and says, “No one really listens to me.” There is no stated immediate danger.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-stress","ch":"10","chapters":["10","15"],"title":"Stress, coping and defense mechanisms","notes":["t-stress"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 10 and 15; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Stress is the body’s response to any demand. Short-term, the stress response protects us; chronic stress damages the heart, immune system, mood and memory. Coping is what we consciously do to manage stress; defense mechanisms are mostly unconscious ways the mind protects itself from anxiety. Either can be adaptive or harmful.","On the exam, match the quote to the mechanism. Key pairs: suppression is conscious, repression is not; projection blames others, displacement redirects.","Defense mechanisms (with examples): Denial: refusing to accept reality. “The biopsy is wrong; I don’t have cancer.”; Projection: putting your own unacceptable feelings on someone else. A man attracted to a coworker accuses his wife of cheating.; Displacement: moving feelings to a safer target. Yelled at by the boss, he yells at his kids.; Rationalization: a logical-sounding excuse. “I failed because the test was unfair.”; Reaction formation: acting the opposite of what you feel. Being overly sweet to someone you dislike.; Regression: returning to an earlier stage. An adult patient sulks or throws a tantrum; a 4-year-old with a new sibling wets the bed.; Repression: unconscious forgetting of something painful.; Suppression: conscious decision to put it aside for now. “I’ll worry about the rent after my exam.” (The only conscious one, and healthy.); Sublimation: channeling an unacceptable impulse into something constructive. Anger into running or kickboxing. (Always healthy.); Compensation: making up for a weakness by excelling elsewhere.; Intellectualization: using facts and logic to avoid feelings. Reciting survival statistics instead of grieving.; Undoing: an act to cancel out a previous one. Buying flowers after an argument.; Identification: taking on traits of an admired person.; Conversion: anxiety becomes a physical symptom with no organic cause (sudden blindness).; Splitting: seeing people as all good or all bad (common in borderline personality disorder).; Dissociation: disconnecting from memory, identity or surroundings to escape distress.; Altruism: meeting your own needs by helping others.","Levels of anxiety: Mild: alert, perceptual field heightened, can learn; restless.; Moderate: field narrowed, selective inattention; can problem-solve with help.; Severe: field greatly reduced; can’t problem-solve; physical symptoms; needs direction.; Panic: disorganized, can’t attend to the environment, possible loss of reality; needs safety."],"checks":["The only defense mechanism that is conscious?","“I didn’t get the job because the interviewer was biased.”","After a fight with her husband, she kicks the dog.","Selye’s three stages?"],"recallAnswers":["Suppression.","Rationalization.","Displacement.","Alarm → resistance → exhaustion."],"requires":[],"terms":["Fight-or-flight response","General adaptation syndrome (Selye)","Eustress vs distress","Coping"],"termDefinitions":{"Fight-or-flight response":"Racing heart, fast breathing, dilated pupils, sweating, tense muscles, dry mouth. The sympathetic nervous system and the adrenal medulla release epinephrine and norepinephrine, sending blood and glucose to the muscles and brain. The same signs show up with anxiety and panic. Relaxation techniques activate the parasympathetic system and reverse them.","General adaptation syndrome (Selye)":"Alarm at a crisis → coping for weeks → getting sick and worn out. Alarm (fight-or-flight) → resistance (the HPA axis raises cortisol to sustain energy) → exhaustion (resources run out; illness follows). Chronic stress and high cortisol suppress immunity and raise BP and glucose. Ask about stressors in every health history.","Eustress vs distress":"Starting a new job you wanted vs losing one. Both trigger the stress response; eustress is positive and motivating, distress is negative and harmful. Even positive life changes (marriage, a new baby) count as stressors.","Coping":"Making a study plan (problem-focused) or talking to a friend (emotion-focused). Problem-focused coping changes the situation; emotion-focused coping changes how you feel about it. Both are useful at different times. Ask, “What has helped you get through hard times before?” and build on those strengths."},"questions":[{"id":"q-stress-2","sourceId":"q-stress-2","q":"A man who is angry with his supervisor goes home and yells at his dog. Which defense mechanism is this?","o":["Projection","Displacement","Sublimation","Rationalization"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Displacement: feelings move from a threatening target to a safer one.","Supported. Displacement: feelings move from a threatening target to a safer one.","Not supported for this question. Displacement: feelings move from a threatening target to a safer one.","Not supported for this question. Displacement: feelings move from a threatening target to a safer one."],"phys":"Displacement: feelings move from a threatening target to a safer one.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-stress-3","sourceId":"q-stress-3","q":"Which finding is expected during the alarm stage of the stress response?","o":["Bradycardia and pupil constriction","Tachycardia, increased blood glucose and dilated pupils","Decreased cortisol","Increased digestion"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Alarm = sympathetic fight-or-flight: epinephrine raises heart rate and glucose and dilates pupils; digestion slows.","Supported. Alarm = sympathetic fight-or-flight: epinephrine raises heart rate and glucose and dilates pupils; digestion slows.","Not supported for this question. Alarm = sympathetic fight-or-flight: epinephrine raises heart rate and glucose and dilates pupils; digestion slows.","Not supported for this question. Alarm = sympathetic fight-or-flight: epinephrine raises heart rate and glucose and dilates pupils; digestion slows."],"phys":"Alarm = sympathetic fight-or-flight: epinephrine raises heart rate and glucose and dilates pupils; digestion slows.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-stress-4","sourceId":"q-stress-4","q":"A patient with moderate anxiety is missing parts of the teaching. What should the nurse do?","o":["Stop teaching until tomorrow","Give brief, focused information and help the patient refocus","Give a PRN benzodiazepine and restrain","Teach faster to cover everything"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. At moderate anxiety the perceptual field narrows but the patient can still learn and problem-solve with help.","Supported. At moderate anxiety the perceptual field narrows but the patient can still learn and problem-solve with help.","Not supported for this question. At moderate anxiety the perceptual field narrows but the patient can still learn and problem-solve with help.","Not supported for this question. At moderate anxiety the perceptual field narrows but the patient can still learn and problem-solve with help."],"phys":"At moderate anxiety the perceptual field narrows but the patient can still learn and problem-solve with help.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-stress"},{"id":"t-trauma","ch":"10","chapters":["10","16"],"title":"Trauma and trauma-informed care","notes":["t-trauma"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 10 and 16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Trauma is an event, or a series of events, that is physically or emotionally harmful or life-threatening and has lasting effects on functioning and well-being. Trauma-informed care assumes anyone could have a trauma history and changes the question from “What’s wrong with you?” to “What happened to you?” The disorders themselves (PTSD, acute stress, adjustment, dissociative) are under Think it through.","Restraint, being undressed, loud voices or being touched without warning can retraumatize people. Choices and explanations return a sense of control.","Principles of trauma-informed care: Safety: physical and emotional.; Trustworthiness and transparency: explain what you’ll do before doing it.; Peer support.; Collaboration and mutuality: decisions made with the patient.; Empowerment, voice and choice: offer choices whenever possible.; Attention to cultural, historical and gender issues.","What trauma does: Changes the brain’s alarm system (amygdala), memory (hippocampus) and calming circuits (prefrontal cortex).; Shows up in the body: chronic pain, GI symptoms, headaches, sleep problems.; Leads to hypervigilance, avoidance, numbing, dissociation, substance use and self-harm as ways of coping.; Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing, then reconnection."],"checks":["The trauma-informed question?","How long does a crisis usually last?","First priority in crisis intervention?","A grounding technique?"],"recallAnswers":["“What happened to you?” instead of “What’s wrong with you?”","About 4–6 weeks.","Safety: assess risk to self and others.","5-4-3-2-1, feet on the floor, holding ice, orienting to time and place."],"requires":[],"terms":[],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q-trauma-1","sourceId":"q-trauma-1","q":"Which approaches reflect trauma-informed care? Select all that apply.","o":["Explaining each procedure before doing it","Offering choices whenever possible","Asking “What happened to you?” rather than “What’s wrong with you?”","Using restraint early to prevent escalation","Asking before touching the patient"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Trauma-informed care emphasizes safety, transparency, choice and collaboration. Early restraint can retraumatize.","Supported. Trauma-informed care emphasizes safety, transparency, choice and collaboration. Early restraint can retraumatize.","Supported. Trauma-informed care emphasizes safety, transparency, choice and collaboration. Early restraint can retraumatize.","Not supported for this question. Trauma-informed care emphasizes safety, transparency, choice and collaboration. Early restraint can retraumatize.","Supported. Trauma-informed care emphasizes safety, transparency, choice and collaboration. Early restraint can retraumatize."],"phys":"Trauma-informed care emphasizes safety, transparency, choice and collaboration. Early restraint can retraumatize.","ex":[]},{"id":"learn|trauma-choice","q":"A trauma survivor becomes tense when the nurse approaches for an assessment. Which approach supports trauma-informed care?","o":["Explain the examination, ask permission, offer choices and agree on a pause signal","Proceed without explanation to finish quickly","Require disclosure of the traumatic event before care"],"a":[0],"r":["Safety, consent, predictability and choice support control during care.","Unexpected touch can increase distress and erode trust.","Disclosure is not a condition for receiving respectful care."],"phys":"","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-trauma"},{"id":"t-groups-foundations","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Therapeutic groups: benefits and Yalom’s routes to change","notes":["t-groups"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["A group can offer something a lecture alone cannot: members hear peers, receive feedback and try a new way of relating. Shared time also creates privacy, fit and participation limits that the leader must consider.","Name a therapeutic factor by what the interaction does. Universality reduces the sense of being alone; hope makes improvement seem possible; altruism lets someone benefit through helping; cohesiveness supports belonging.","The other factors describe learning, expression and meaning: information, imitation, social skills, interpersonal learning, family-pattern work, catharsis and existential concerns. Several can occur together. Use the complete reference to compare all eleven."],"checks":["Why choose a therapeutic group, and what are its limitations?","Distinguish hope, universality, altruism, information and cohesiveness.","Explain the remaining six Yalom factors."],"recallAnswers":["It offers peer experience, feedback, supported practice and belonging while reaching several people. Shared time, privacy concerns, disruptive behavior and pressure to conform require attention to fit.","Hope builds belief that improvement is possible; universality recognizes a shared experience; altruism helps the helper through contributing; information supplies knowledge; cohesiveness supports acceptance and connection.","Family-pattern recapitulation works through old relational patterns; socializing techniques develop interaction skills; imitation learns by observing; interpersonal learning examines an interpersonal effect through feedback and trying a new response; catharsis expresses and understands feeling; existential work addresses limits, choice and meaning."],"requires":[],"terms":["Group","Therapeutic group","Group work","Group psychotherapy","Therapeutic factors"],"termDefinitions":{"Group":"Several people work toward an agreed purpose and affect each other’s experience. Membership creates interdependence; one person’s response can change the next person’s behavior. Assess the relationships as well as each person’s needs.","Therapeutic group":"Members practise a useful skill, gain support or explore a difficulty together. A shared activity becomes therapeutic when it supports psychological development or growth. Choose a purpose and measure a relevant benefit; attendance alone is insufficient.","Group work":"The nurse plans a session, attends to participation and checks what members gained. Useful change depends on both preparation and what happens between members. Plan, facilitate and evaluate instead of merely gathering people in a room.","Group psychotherapy":"A trained clinician helps members examine recurring relationship patterns and psychiatric symptoms. The relationships within the session become part of a specialized treatment. Distinguish psychotherapy from RN teaching or activity facilitation; verify preparation and scope.","Therapeutic factors":"A member gains hope, feels understood or tries a new way of relating. Yalom described several routes through which the group experience can support change. Match the helpful factor to the group’s purpose; one mechanism does not explain every benefit."},"questions":[{"id":"q-g34-02","sourceId":"q-g34-02","q":"A nurse explains why a group may be useful while discussing its limits. Which statements are accurate? Select all that apply.","o":["Members can practise interaction and receive peer feedback.","Membership automatically makes every disclosure confidential.","Several patients can receive help within the same session.","A useful group must contain people with identical backgrounds.","Members can learn from experiences beyond those of the leader."],"a":[0,2,4],"r":["Supported. Supported practice, several-person delivery and mutual learning are potential benefits. Privacy cannot be guaranteed, and useful groups may be heterogeneous. Similarity can affect fit, but identical backgrounds are not a requirement.","Not supported for this question. Supported practice, several-person delivery and mutual learning are potential benefits. Privacy cannot be guaranteed, and useful groups may be heterogeneous. Similarity can affect fit, but identical backgrounds are not a requirement.","Supported. Supported practice, several-person delivery and mutual learning are potential benefits. Privacy cannot be guaranteed, and useful groups may be heterogeneous. Similarity can affect fit, but identical backgrounds are not a requirement.","Not supported for this question. Supported practice, several-person delivery and mutual learning are potential benefits. Privacy cannot be guaranteed, and useful groups may be heterogeneous. Similarity can affect fit, but identical backgrounds are not a requirement.","Supported. Supported practice, several-person delivery and mutual learning are potential benefits. Privacy cannot be guaranteed, and useful groups may be heterogeneous. Similarity can affect fit, but identical backgrounds are not a requirement."],"phys":"Supported practice, several-person delivery and mutual learning are potential benefits. Privacy cannot be guaranteed, and useful groups may be heterogeneous. Similarity can affect fit, but identical backgrounds are not a requirement.","ex":[]},{"id":"learn|g34-family","q":"A member notices they expect the leader to dismiss a request just as a parent once did. With feedback, they try stating the request directly. Which group mechanism best fits?","o":["Imparting information alone","Corrective recapitulation of the primary family group","A guarantee that past family losses can be undone"],"a":[1],"r":["No factual lesson is described; the focus is a familiar relational pattern.","The old family pattern is being recognized and worked through in the present group.","The group can support a different response, not erase the person’s history."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-family"}],"studio":"t-groups-foundations","active":true,"patient":"In a coping group, a new member says, “I thought I was the only person struggling like this.”","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-groups-design","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Plan the group: process, membership and setting","notes":["t-groups"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Plan the purpose, membership, setting and leader preparation before recruitment. A medication-education group and a psychotherapy group require different tasks and competencies.","Content is the subject being discussed. Process is how members interact while discussing it. Norms, speaking time, alliances and feedback can affect whether the content is useful.","Match structure to the setting. Changing inpatient membership may need brief, clear sessions; a continuing community group may build on earlier work. Open or closed membership and shared or varied member characteristics each bring benefits and limits."],"checks":["Distinguish content, process, norms, themes and feedback.","What do you decide before recruitment?","How do membership and setting change preparation?"],"recallAnswers":["Content is the spoken subject; process is how members interact. Norms are shared expectations, themes recur across stories, and feedback describes interpersonal effects. Respectfully resolved conflict can support the work.","Define the name, purpose, outcomes, participants and responsibilities; set a schedule, duration, suitable room and seating; decide how to evaluate benefit and maintain consistency if staff rotate.","Open/closed describes whether newcomers join; homogeneous/heterogeneous describes shared traits or differences. Notice subgroups. Inpatient acuity, interruptions and turnover often need added structure; outpatient groups may support greater continuity and member direction."],"requires":[],"terms":["Group content","Group process","Group norms","Feedback","Open and closed membership","Homogeneous, heterogeneous and subgroup","Group themes and conflict"],"termDefinitions":{"Group content":"Participants discuss how to ask for help after discharge. Content is the subject matter and spoken material; a transcript captures this layer. Record the topic without mistaking it for an account of the interaction.","Group process":"People interrupt one speaker but listen closely to another discussing the same topic. Process concerns how members relate, including tone, gestures, silence and patterns of participation. Notice who responds to whom and intervene when the interaction blocks the work.","Group norms":"Members expect a turn to speak and arrive before the session begins. Shared expectations can support safety or quietly pressure members to agree. Clarify helpful expectations and address rules that silence disagreement.","Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone.","Open and closed membership":"An admission group welcomes new people; a fixed course keeps its original membership. Open groups admit replacements. Closed groups do not add members when someone leaves. Revisit orientation with newcomers and plan continuity for changing membership.","Homogeneous, heterogeneous and subgroup":"Members may share a central characteristic, have varied backgrounds, or form a small alliance. Similarity and diversity affect fit. A subgroup is a smaller cluster whose loyalty may compete with the whole group. Assess the actual mix and include quieter or less-connected members; these terms do not rank people’s value.","Group themes and conflict":"Different stories keep returning to trust; two members disagree about asking for help. A theme is a recurring meaning. Conflict is an expressed difference that may expose useful work. Name a possible theme tentatively and support respectful resolution rather than suppressing every disagreement."},"questions":[{"id":"learn|g34-fit","q":"An attentive outpatient has few social contacts and limited income. What should determine whether a proposed therapeutic group is a good fit?","o":["Exclude anyone with few friends.","Require the person to speak as quickly as the most assertive member.","Assess goals, functioning, readiness and the support available in this particular group."],"a":[2],"r":["Few contacts may be a reason to offer appropriate support, not an automatic exclusion.","Response speed does not establish ability to benefit.","Fit depends on the person, the purpose and the group’s capacity to support participation."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-fit"},{"id":"learn|g34-room","q":"A nurse is preparing an inpatient skills group with rotating membership. Which arrangement best supports the work?","o":["An oversized public passageway with frequent interruptions","A suitable private room, visible nonhierarchical seating and a clear, repeatable session structure","A setting with no agenda because all hospital groups have stable membership"],"a":[1],"r":["Noise, exposure and excess distance can inhibit trust and participation.","The environment and structure address privacy, visibility, acuity and changing membership.","Inpatient turnover often calls for more structure; stability should not be assumed."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-room"}],"studio":"t-groups-design","active":true,"patient":"You are planning a short inpatient medication-education group where admissions and discharges change membership.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-groups-development","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Lead through orientation, working and termination","notes":["t-groups"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["At orientation, members need a purpose, agreements and enough safety to begin participating. Early caution and testing do not mean the group has failed.","In working, members explore issues, receive feedback and practise change. Conflict can be part of development when the leader helps members address it respectfully. Storming, norming and performing describe changes within the group’s development.","Termination makes the ending explicit. Review what changed, discuss feelings about leaving and plan how members will use the learning afterward. An ending is more than a final attendance check."],"checks":["Match the leader’s task with each group phase.","Where do storming, norming and performing fit?","What should happen at ending and follow-up?"],"recallAnswers":["Planning designs the group before recruitment. Orientation introduces purpose, members and agreements. Working facilitates problem solving and constructive conflict. Termination reviews gains, reactions to ending and future goals.","After forming, working can include clashes and challenges, then cooperation and settled roles, then task-focused performance. Conflict or membership change can require a return to earlier work.","Discuss loss or anger, collect positive and negative feedback, review accomplishments and new insight, plan next goals or referrals, and compare outcomes during and after the course."],"requires":[],"terms":["Group phases","Feedback","Group norms"],"termDefinitions":{"Group phases":"A new group needs introductions; a mature group can work through disagreement. A group’s needs change as it forms, works and ends. Conflict can move it back to earlier tasks. Adapt the amount of structure and the leadership task to the phase.","Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone.","Group norms":"Members expect a turn to speak and arrive before the session begins. Shared expectations can support safety or quietly pressure members to agree. Clarify helpful expectations and address rules that silence disagreement."},"questions":[{"id":"q-g34-04","sourceId":"q-g34-04","q":"Members challenge a facilitator’s authority during a disagreement. No one is threatening harm. Which interpretation best guides the response?","o":["Any conflict proves the group should be ended.","Storming can be part of working; help members address the disagreement safely.","The group has completed termination.","The leader should withdraw all structure immediately."],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Storming can include clashes and challenges during working. Constructive resolution supports development. Automatically ending the group avoids the task, and either calling it termination or abandoning structure misreads the need.","Supported. Storming can include clashes and challenges during working. Constructive resolution supports development. Automatically ending the group avoids the task, and either calling it termination or abandoning structure misreads the need.","Not supported for this question. Storming can include clashes and challenges during working. Constructive resolution supports development. Automatically ending the group avoids the task, and either calling it termination or abandoning structure misreads the need.","Not supported for this question. Storming can include clashes and challenges during working. Constructive resolution supports development. Automatically ending the group avoids the task, and either calling it termination or abandoning structure misreads the need."],"phys":"Storming can include clashes and challenges during working. Constructive resolution supports development. Automatically ending the group avoids the task, and either calling it termination or abandoning structure misreads the need.","ex":[]},{"id":"learn|g34-ending","q":"An established group is completing its planned course. One person is disappointed and another is angry about the ending. Which response supports termination?","o":["Treat the anger as proof the entire course was useless.","Ignore reactions and use the final session only for a new lecture.","Discuss reactions, review individual gains and identify realistic next goals and follow-up."],"a":[2],"r":["Reactions to ending can occur even when the group has helped.","Avoiding the ending misses an important therapeutic task.","Termination includes both consolidating progress and addressing the feelings and needs associated with separation."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-ending"}],"studio":"t-groups-development","active":true,"patient":"A new group is cautious at first. Later, members disagree over speaking time and then develop a workable shared agreement.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-groups-roles","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Recognize what a member’s behavior does for the group","notes":["t-groups"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Name a role by the function of a contribution, not by labeling the person. Task roles help the group do its work; maintenance roles make working together possible.","An information seeker asks for useful facts; an elaborator develops an idea; an orienter brings the group back to its purpose. A gatekeeper opens participation; an encourager supports contributions; a harmonizer helps members work through tension.","An individual-role pattern can displace shared work through attacking, blocking or repeatedly seeking personal attention. Describe the behavior and effect, explore its context and redirect without humiliation. The chapter reference keeps every named role available."],"checks":["Name task roles and their purpose.","Name maintenance roles and their purpose.","Which individual roles may displace the work, and how do you respond?"],"recallAnswers":["Elaboration, energizing, information giving/seeking, opinion giving and orienting help the group understand and complete its work. Identify the function, not simply who talks most.","Gatekeeping, encouraging, harmonizing, compromising, standard setting and following sustain inclusion and relationships. Maintenance makes productive work possible without suppressing every disagreement.","Aggression, blocking, repeated sympathy seeking, recognition seeking and off-purpose self-confession can displace the shared task. Describe behavior, explore the need and support alternatives. Reflect on these functions in your own learning groups."],"requires":[],"terms":["Task roles","Maintenance roles","Individual roles","Feedback"],"termDefinitions":{"Task roles":"A participant asks for evidence or helps the group decide its next step. These behaviors move the group toward its stated work. Reinforce useful clarification, information and goal-directed action.","Maintenance roles":"A member notices an overlooked speaker and invites their contribution. These behaviors sustain inclusion and the relationships needed for the work. Support balanced participation and repair tension without abandoning the purpose.","Individual roles":"One person repeatedly seeks applause while the agreed topic is left behind. Personal needs can displace the shared task. This describes behavior, not a permanent identity. Describe the effect, set respectful limits and explore the underlying need.","Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone."},"questions":[{"id":"learn|g34-blocker","q":"One member rejects every proposed next step to prevent the group from reaching its agreed decision. Which functional role is most directly shown?","o":["Individual role: blocker","Maintenance role: harmonizer","Task role: orienter"],"a":[0],"r":["Blocking repeatedly obstructs the shared task; respond to its function rather than labeling the person permanently.","A harmonizer supports resolving differences so work can continue.","An orienter reconnects the discussion with its goal."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-blocker"},{"id":"learn|g34-roles","q":"A student is reviewing their own behavior in a clinical learning group. Which observation best identifies a maintenance contribution?","o":["I introduced an unrelated personal achievement whenever the topic changed.","I helped a disagreement become respectful enough for both viewpoints to be heard.","I supplied a verified fact needed for the clinical problem."],"a":[1],"r":["An unrelated bid for recognition can displace the group’s purpose.","Helping members resolve tension sustains the relationships needed for the work.","Providing relevant information is useful, but primarily a task contribution."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-roles"}],"studio":"t-groups-roles","active":true,"patient":"During planning, one member asks for needed details while another notices that a quiet peer has not had a turn.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-groups-modalities","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Match group purpose, theory and evidence","notes":["t-groups"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Start with the intended change. An education group teaches knowledge or skills; a support group shares experience; psychotherapy uses a therapeutic approach and requires suitable preparation and scope.","A theoretical framework guides what the leader notices and does. Cognitive-behavioral work examines thoughts and actions; psychodynamic work explores recurring relational patterns; humanistic and other approaches bring different emphases.","Match the program to the person’s needs, available evidence and leader competence. A historical treatment example or a published study protocol does not establish that a specific intervention is effective or suitable today. The reference retains all named frameworks, group types and resources."],"checks":["How do the five theoretical frameworks guide intervention?","Which group purposes and leader preparations are described?","How should you use the chapter’s resources and treatment evidence?"],"recallAnswers":["Humanistic work explores experience and growth; CBT uses goals, skills and monitoring; psychodynamic work explores underlying conflicts and insight; educational work builds knowledge and coping; systems work examines relationships and social context.","Education, medication teaching, dual-diagnosis, stress/mindfulness, community meetings, activity, task and support may fall within trained RN facilitation. Specialized psychotherapy requires appropriate advanced preparation and authority. Peer self-help has a different role. Categories can overlap.","Know relevant support and mutual-help options, including NAMI and the named twelve-step and consumer resources. Match treatment examples to their population and distinguish trials, reviews, pilot work and protocols. A cited association or historical table is not an individualized treatment order."],"requires":[],"terms":["Therapeutic group","Group psychotherapy","Group work"],"termDefinitions":{"Therapeutic group":"Members practise a useful skill, gain support or explore a difficulty together. A shared activity becomes therapeutic when it supports psychological development or growth. Choose a purpose and measure a relevant benefit; attendance alone is insufficient.","Group psychotherapy":"A trained clinician helps members examine recurring relationship patterns and psychiatric symptoms. The relationships within the session become part of a specialized treatment. Distinguish psychotherapy from RN teaching or activity facilitation; verify preparation and scope.","Group work":"The nurse plans a session, attends to participation and checks what members gained. Useful change depends on both preparation and what happens between members. Plan, facilitate and evaluate instead of merely gathering people in a room."},"questions":[{"id":"learn|g34-theory","q":"A group explores how family expectations, housing barriers and unequal access to resources affect members’ options. Which framework most directly fits that focus?","o":["A framework concerned only with memorizing medication facts","A claim that the environment has no effect on mental health","A systems framework examining the person in relational and social context"],"a":[2],"r":["Education can be useful but does not best describe the central focus here.","The scenario explicitly connects the person with their environment.","Systems approaches examine interactions with family, community and social conditions."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-theory"},{"id":"learn|g34-evidence","q":"A facilitator reads that a virtual group intervention has a published study protocol. What can be concluded from the protocol alone?","o":["It sets out how the intervention will be studied; outcome evidence still needs review.","It proves the intervention has already reduced every participant’s symptoms.","It makes a pilot study identical in certainty to a large controlled trial."],"a":[0],"r":["A study plan does not establish a treatment effect; evaluate the actual results and study design.","The protocol alone does not provide those outcomes or a universal guarantee.","Different designs and stages of research have different evidentiary limits."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-evidence"}],"studio":"t-groups-modalities","active":true,"patient":"A unit wants patients to demonstrate what their prescribed medicine is for and which adverse effects require help.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-groups-leadership","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Choose a leadership style and use supervision","notes":["t-groups"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Choose a leadership style for the task, phase and current needs. Democratic leadership invites shared problem solving while maintaining the frame. More directive structure can be needed for a focused task; little direction is a poor fit when containment is needed.","Use active listening, clarification, reflection, respectful challenge and summaries to support members’ work. Keep feedback specific to observed behavior and its effect rather than claiming to know everyone’s feelings.","Supervision and coleadership help examine the leader’s reactions, boundaries and decisions. Agree on roles, debrief differences and notice cultural assumptions and barriers to participation. Inclusion does not require forced personal disclosure."],"checks":["Contrast the three leadership styles.","Which communication moves support the work?","Why use supervision, coleadership and cultural reflection?"],"recallAnswers":["Autocratic leadership controls direction; democratic leadership supports active shared problem solving; laissez-faire gives substantial freedom and little direction. Match style to task, phase and safety rather than personal preference.","Listen, ask focused questions, clarify and give accurate information. Use respectful confrontation, empathy, reflection, specific support and summaries. The chapter’s antidepressant teaching example notes that benefit can take four weeks or longer.","Examine skill, boundaries, transference/countertransference and difficult reactions. Coleaders can model, train and debrief, but their relationship needs openness. Address marginalization, including LGBTQ inclusion, and offer identity sharing without forcing disclosure."],"requires":[],"terms":["Group work","Feedback","Group norms"],"termDefinitions":{"Group work":"The nurse plans a session, attends to participation and checks what members gained. Useful change depends on both preparation and what happens between members. Plan, facilitate and evaluate instead of merely gathering people in a room.","Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone.","Group norms":"Members expect a turn to speak and arrive before the session begins. Shared expectations can support safety or quietly pressure members to agree. Clarify helpful expectations and address rules that silence disagreement."},"questions":[{"id":"learn|g34-supervision","q":"After a difficult group, the nurse notices unusually strong personal anger toward a member. Which professional action is most helpful?","o":["Describe the member as the problem in front of the group.","Use confidential clinical supervision to examine the reaction, boundaries and facilitation.","Keep practising beyond competence because asking for support shows failure."],"a":[1],"r":["Public blame can harm the relationship and ignores the leader’s contribution.","Supervision helps examine countertransference and identify a safer, more effective response.","Seeking appropriate support is a professional development and safety action."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-supervision"},{"id":"learn|g34-style","q":"Members can safely choose their own approach in an open-ended creative activity. The leader intentionally provides very little direction. Which style is most evident?","o":["Autocratic","Democratic shared decision-making with active facilitation","Laissez-faire"],"a":[2],"r":["Autocratic leadership retains control over the direction.","Democratic leadership actively supports joint problem solving; this example emphasizes minimal direction.","Laissez-faire permits substantial member freedom. The nurse still retains safety responsibilities."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|g34-style"}],"studio":"t-groups-leadership","active":true,"patient":"A group is ready to solve a shared problem, but the leader has begun answering every question before members can respond.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-groups-safety","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Protect consent, confidentiality and immediate safety","notes":["t-groups"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Before participation, explain the purpose, expectations and confidentiality limits. The nurse has professional duties; an agreement cannot guarantee that every peer will protect a disclosure.","Online groups need attention to private space, recording, unseen listeners, each member’s location, emergency contacts and a connection-failure plan. A video connection does not remove licensure or local-response requirements.","A safety concern can interrupt the planned group task. Assess a new statement about self-harm, obtain appropriate support and protect members from an immediate threat. Base restrictions on actual behavior and assessed risk, not on quietness, diagnosis or limited resources alone."],"checks":["What can the nurse promise about group confidentiality?","What requires attention in virtual group care?","When do assessment, backup or stabilization take priority?"],"recallAnswers":["Explain professional duties and exceptions, and ask members to protect privacy, but do not guarantee peers will do so. Discuss risks, benefits, leaving, outside contact, devices and any authorized recording. Legal protections vary.","Screen for fit, protect privacy and security, verify participant location and authorized practice, plan for unseen listeners, agree on emergency and disconnection responses, and adapt to reduced nonverbal cues.","Act on actual threats, violence or suicide risk without waiting for the agenda. Seek experienced support for participation problems related to active paranoia or acute mania. Quietness, few friends and limited finances alone do not justify exclusion. Removal is generally a last resort for non-dangerous persistent difficulties."],"requires":[],"terms":["Group psychotherapy","Group norms","Group process"],"termDefinitions":{"Group psychotherapy":"A trained clinician helps members examine recurring relationship patterns and psychiatric symptoms. The relationships within the session become part of a specialized treatment. Distinguish psychotherapy from RN teaching or activity facilitation; verify preparation and scope.","Group norms":"Members expect a turn to speak and arrive before the session begins. Shared expectations can support safety or quietly pressure members to agree. Clarify helpful expectations and address rules that silence disagreement.","Group process":"People interrupt one speaker but listen closely to another discussing the same topic. Process concerns how members relate, including tone, gestures, silence and patterns of participation. Notice who responds to whom and intervene when the interaction blocks the work."},"questions":[{"id":"q-g34-08","sourceId":"q-g34-08","q":"Before an in-person group begins, which actions support informed participation and privacy? Select all that apply.","o":["Explain likely benefits, risks and confidentiality limits.","Guarantee that peers have the same professional duties as the nurse.","Discuss expectations about leaving and contact outside the group.","Address devices, photographs and recording.","Describe disclosure as risk-free once everyone signs an agreement."],"a":[0,2,3],"r":["Supported. Consent includes risks, boundaries and confidentiality limits, with clear expectations about devices and outside contact. Peers are not governed by the nurse’s professional code, and an agreement does not remove every possibility of disclosure. Never promise absolute confidentiality.","Not supported for this question. Consent includes risks, boundaries and confidentiality limits, with clear expectations about devices and outside contact. Peers are not governed by the nurse’s professional code, and an agreement does not remove every possibility of disclosure. Never promise absolute confidentiality.","Supported. Consent includes risks, boundaries and confidentiality limits, with clear expectations about devices and outside contact. Peers are not governed by the nurse’s professional code, and an agreement does not remove every possibility of disclosure. Never promise absolute confidentiality.","Supported. Consent includes risks, boundaries and confidentiality limits, with clear expectations about devices and outside contact. Peers are not governed by the nurse’s professional code, and an agreement does not remove every possibility of disclosure. Never promise absolute confidentiality.","Not supported for this question. Consent includes risks, boundaries and confidentiality limits, with clear expectations about devices and outside contact. Peers are not governed by the nurse’s professional code, and an agreement does not remove every possibility of disclosure. Never promise absolute confidentiality."],"phys":"Consent includes risks, boundaries and confidentiality limits, with clear expectations about devices and outside contact. Peers are not governed by the nurse’s professional code, and an agreement does not remove every possibility of disclosure. Never promise absolute confidentiality.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-g34-11","sourceId":"q-g34-11","q":"A novice facilitator is reviewing group fit and backup needs. Which findings call for assessment and experienced clinical support rather than routine unsupervised group management? Select all that apply.","o":["Active paranoid beliefs are interfering with trust in the session.","A quiet member reports thoughts of acting on suicide tonight.","A member has limited income but is attentive and follows the discussion.","Acute mania prevents a member from attending to the group or respecting basic boundaries.","A member listens carefully and asks for thinking time."],"a":[0,1,3],"r":["Supported. Assess the actual safety and participation needs associated with paranoia, possible imminent suicide risk and acute mania. Seek supervision, backup or individual stabilization as appropriate. Quiet behavior can coexist with serious risk; poverty and thoughtful silence alone are not exclusion criteria. These findings do not justify permanent diagnosis-based bans.","Supported. Assess the actual safety and participation needs associated with paranoia, possible imminent suicide risk and acute mania. Seek supervision, backup or individual stabilization as appropriate. Quiet behavior can coexist with serious risk; poverty and thoughtful silence alone are not exclusion criteria. These findings do not justify permanent diagnosis-based bans.","Not supported for this question. Assess the actual safety and participation needs associated with paranoia, possible imminent suicide risk and acute mania. Seek supervision, backup or individual stabilization as appropriate. Quiet behavior can coexist with serious risk; poverty and thoughtful silence alone are not exclusion criteria. These findings do not justify permanent diagnosis-based bans.","Supported. Assess the actual safety and participation needs associated with paranoia, possible imminent suicide risk and acute mania. Seek supervision, backup or individual stabilization as appropriate. Quiet behavior can coexist with serious risk; poverty and thoughtful silence alone are not exclusion criteria. These findings do not justify permanent diagnosis-based bans.","Not supported for this question. Assess the actual safety and participation needs associated with paranoia, possible imminent suicide risk and acute mania. Seek supervision, backup or individual stabilization as appropriate. Quiet behavior can coexist with serious risk; poverty and thoughtful silence alone are not exclusion criteria. These findings do not justify permanent diagnosis-based bans."],"phys":"Assess the actual safety and participation needs associated with paranoia, possible imminent suicide risk and acute mania. Seek supervision, backup or individual stabilization as appropriate. Quiet behavior can coexist with serious risk; poverty and thoughtful silence alone are not exclusion criteria. These findings do not justify permanent diagnosis-based bans.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-groups-safety","active":true,"patient":"Before an online group, a participant asks whether it is safe to discuss something they do not want other members to repeat.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"t-groups-participation","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Respond to difficult participation and evaluate benefit","notes":["t-groups"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Protect room for participation without humiliating a member. When one person dominates, describe the pattern, remind the group of shared time and invite other voices. Explore the need behind the pattern without assuming a motive.","Silence can reflect thought, uncertainty or concerns about safety. Offer a manageable invitation and time to respond; do not force sensitive disclosure. With anger, acknowledge feeling while maintaining behavioral limits.","Evaluate the intended change. Medication education needs demonstrated understanding; therapy goals may concern coping, symptoms or relationships. Attendance and a pleasant session can be useful observations, but they do not establish the goal was met."],"checks":["How do you respond to monopolizing, angry or help-rejecting patterns?","How do you support someone who remains silent?","What demonstrates an appropriate outcome?"],"recallAnswers":["Share airtime, invite others and use specific owned feedback; follow up privately about needs and an agreed limit when needed. Combine empathy with behavioral boundaries for anger, explore the advice cycle instead of escalating advice, and use supervision. Immediate danger requires the safety response.","Consider safety, processing time and confidence. Offer thinking time, return with a manageable invitation or use a planned round; explore barriers without forcing disclosure. Listening alone does not prove refusal.","Medication education needs knowledge of purpose, three significant adverse effects, prescribed dose/timing and relevant interactions; the review example includes two interactions. Therapy outcomes concern relevant insight, behavior and symptoms. Use teach-back, suitable measures and follow-up rather than attendance alone."],"requires":[],"terms":["Feedback","Group process","Individual roles"],"termDefinitions":{"Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone.","Group process":"People interrupt one speaker but listen closely to another discussing the same topic. Process concerns how members relate, including tone, gestures, silence and patterns of participation. Notice who responds to whom and intervene when the interaction blocks the work.","Individual roles":"One person repeatedly seeks applause while the agreed topic is left behind. Personal needs can displace the shared task. This describes behavior, not a permanent identity. Describe the effect, set respectful limits and explore the underlying need."},"questions":[{"id":"q-g34-10","sourceId":"q-g34-10","q":"A quiet member says they are still organizing their thoughts. Which next response best supports participation?","o":["Demand an immediate account of their most painful experience.","Offer a little time and agree to return to the member with the prompt.","Explain that silence proves they are refusing treatment.","Permanently exclude the member because others speak faster."],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Time and a predictable invitation can help a member formulate a contribution. Silence may reflect thoughtfulness or uncertainty about safety. Forced disclosure, labeling and exclusion ignore those possibilities.","Supported. Time and a predictable invitation can help a member formulate a contribution. Silence may reflect thoughtfulness or uncertainty about safety. Forced disclosure, labeling and exclusion ignore those possibilities.","Not supported for this question. Time and a predictable invitation can help a member formulate a contribution. Silence may reflect thoughtfulness or uncertainty about safety. Forced disclosure, labeling and exclusion ignore those possibilities.","Not supported for this question. Time and a predictable invitation can help a member formulate a contribution. Silence may reflect thoughtfulness or uncertainty about safety. Forced disclosure, labeling and exclusion ignore those possibilities."],"phys":"Time and a predictable invitation can help a member formulate a contribution. Silence may reflect thoughtfulness or uncertainty about safety. Forced disclosure, labeling and exclusion ignore those possibilities.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-g34-12","sourceId":"q-g34-12","q":"A medication-education group is being evaluated. Which observation gives the strongest evidence that its learning goal was met?","o":["Every participant attended for the full hour.","The group agreed that medication costs should never be questioned.","Members can use their prescribed plans to explain purpose, dose/timing, important adverse effects and relevant interactions.","The facilitator spoke continuously without interruption."],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Demonstrated medication knowledge matches the education outcome. Attendance and a smooth lecture do not show understanding, and accepting cost is not a knowledge outcome or a prerequisite for care. Use teach-back and address any practical barriers.","Not supported for this question. Demonstrated medication knowledge matches the education outcome. Attendance and a smooth lecture do not show understanding, and accepting cost is not a knowledge outcome or a prerequisite for care. Use teach-back and address any practical barriers.","Supported. Demonstrated medication knowledge matches the education outcome. Attendance and a smooth lecture do not show understanding, and accepting cost is not a knowledge outcome or a prerequisite for care. Use teach-back and address any practical barriers.","Not supported for this question. Demonstrated medication knowledge matches the education outcome. Attendance and a smooth lecture do not show understanding, and accepting cost is not a knowledge outcome or a prerequisite for care. Use teach-back and address any practical barriers."],"phys":"Demonstrated medication knowledge matches the education outcome. Attendance and a smooth lecture do not show understanding, and accepting cost is not a knowledge outcome or a prerequisite for care. Use teach-back and address any practical barriers.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-groups-participation","active":true,"patient":"A medication group includes one member who answers every prompt and another who has not spoken yet.","clinicalUpdate":""},{"id":"c-anxiety","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Panic attack and generalized anxiety","patient":"31-year-old in the ED for the third time this month with sudden chest tightness, pounding heart, shaking, numb fingers and a sense she is about to die. Troponin and ECG are normal. Between attacks she worries constantly about work, money and her kids, sleeps poorly and has stopped driving on the highway.","notes":["c-anxiety"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 15; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: With the cardiac work-up negative, the cues fit panic disorder (recurrent unexpected attacks plus worry and avoidance) with generalized anxiety between attacks. Hyperventilation explains the tingling. She is at the panic level now and will come down to moderate with calm support.","Priority: During the attack the priority is safety and physical stability: rule out the dangerous cause, stay with her, and bring her anxiety down from panic. After the attack: ineffective coping, avoidance and sleep. Teaching waits until her anxiety is mild to moderate.","Act: Stay with her, keep a calm manner and a low, slow voice, move to a quiet space, and use short, simple statements with repetition. Guide slow breathing. Give a PRN as ordered. Once calmer: name the anxiety, explore triggers, teach relaxation and CBT skills, and explain the medications.","Reassess: Check vital signs and her ability to follow directions as the attack subsides. Over time: fewer attacks and ED visits, use of breathing skills when an attack begins, return to avoided places (highway driving), better sleep, and safe medication use (no mixing benzodiazepines with alcohol)."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["First, rule out a physical cause (MI, PE, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia, stimulants, withdrawal). Then place the anxiety on the mild → moderate → severe → panic scale, using her perceptual field, her ability to problem-solve and her physical signs. Look for the pattern: panic attacks, anticipatory worry and avoidance. With the cardiac work-up negative, the cues fit panic disorder (recurrent unexpected attacks plus worry and avoidance) with generalized anxiety between attacks. Hyperventilation explains the tingling. She is at the panic level now and will come down to moderate with calm support.","During the attack the priority is safety and physical stability: rule out the dangerous cause, stay with her, and bring her anxiety down from panic. After the attack: ineffective coping, avoidance and sleep. Teaching waits until her anxiety is mild to moderate. Stay with her, keep a calm manner and a low, slow voice, move to a quiet space, and use short, simple statements with repetition. Guide slow breathing. Give a PRN as ordered. Once calmer: name the anxiety, explore triggers, teach relaxation and CBT skills, and explain the medications.","Check vital signs and her ability to follow directions as the attack subsides. Over time: fewer attacks and ED visits, use of breathing skills when an attack begins, return to avoided places (highway driving), better sleep, and safe medication use (no mixing benzodiazepines with alcohol)."],"requires":[],"terms":["Panic attack","Hyperventilation and tingling","Narrowed perceptual field","Anticipatory anxiety and avoidance","Excessive worry (GAD)","sertraline","escitalopram","venlafaxine","diazepam","lorazepam","clonazepam","alprazolam","paroxetine","duloxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Panic attack":"Sudden surge of terror that peaks within minutes: pounding heart, chest pain, shortness of breath, choking, trembling, sweating, dizziness, tingling, fear of dying or “going crazy.” A false alarm of the fight-or-flight system: the amygdala fires and the sympathetic nervous system floods the body with adrenaline. Usually peaks in about 10 minutes and resolves in under 30. Stay with her, speak calmly and simply, and guide her breathing. Never assume a panic attack until a cardiac cause is ruled out.","Hyperventilation and tingling":"Fast, shallow breathing; numb, tingling fingers and lips; lightheadedness. Overbreathing blows off CO₂ → respiratory alkalosis. Alkalosis lowers ionized calcium, so nerves fire more easily (tingling) and blood vessels in the brain constrict (dizziness). Coach slow breathing (in through the nose, longer breath out). The symptoms ease as CO₂ normalizes.","Narrowed perceptual field":"She can only focus on her heartbeat and can’t follow your instructions about the ECG. As anxiety rises, attention narrows onto the threat and filters out everything else. Tells you the level. At severe or panic anxiety, she can’t problem-solve or learn, so give short, simple directions and save the teaching for later.","Anticipatory anxiety and avoidance":"Has stopped driving on the highway in case an attack happens there. The fear of the next attack becomes its own problem; avoiding places brings relief, which reinforces the avoidance. A sign of panic disorder, and of agoraphobia if avoidance spreads to places where escape would be hard.","Excessive worry (GAD)":"Worries about many things most days, can’t stop, with muscle tension, fatigue, irritability and poor sleep. Chronic overactivation of the stress response; worry is used (unsuccessfully) to feel in control. Generalized anxiety disorder: worry most days for at least 6 months, with at least 3 physical or cognitive symptoms."},"questions":[{"id":"q-anx-1","sourceId":"q-anx-1","q":"A patient is having a panic attack. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","o":["Stay with the patient","Use short, simple statements in a calm voice","Move the patient to a quieter area","Teach the patient about the causes of panic disorder","Guide slow breathing"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. At panic level the patient cannot learn; teaching waits until anxiety is mild to moderate.","Supported. At panic level the patient cannot learn; teaching waits until anxiety is mild to moderate.","Supported. At panic level the patient cannot learn; teaching waits until anxiety is mild to moderate.","Not supported for this question. At panic level the patient cannot learn; teaching waits until anxiety is mild to moderate.","Supported. At panic level the patient cannot learn; teaching waits until anxiety is mild to moderate."],"phys":"At panic level the patient cannot learn; teaching waits until anxiety is mild to moderate.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-anx-3","sourceId":"q-anx-3","q":"A patient taking lorazepam asks about drinking wine at dinner. The nurse’s best response is:","o":["“One glass is fine.”","“Avoid alcohol; together they can dangerously slow your breathing.”","“Take the lorazepam after dinner instead.”","“Alcohol makes lorazepam less effective.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Benzodiazepines + alcohol = additive CNS and respiratory depression.","Supported. Benzodiazepines + alcohol = additive CNS and respiratory depression.","Not supported for this question. Benzodiazepines + alcohol = additive CNS and respiratory depression.","Not supported for this question. Benzodiazepines + alcohol = additive CNS and respiratory depression."],"phys":"Benzodiazepines + alcohol = additive CNS and respiratory depression.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-anxiety"},{"id":"c-ocd","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","patient":"22-year-old college student admitted because handwashing now takes 6 hours a day. Her hands are red, cracked and bleeding. She knows the fear of germs is excessive but says, “If I don’t wash, something terrible will happen to my family.” She has missed her classes for a month and rarely eats in the dining hall.","notes":["c-ocd"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 15; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: The cues fit OCD with contamination obsessions and washing compulsions: more than an hour a day, causing distress and impairment, with good insight. The washing has caused skin breakdown with possible infection, and the time spent on rituals is crowding out eating, sleep and school.","Priority: Priorities: impaired skin integrity and infection risk, then nutrition, sleep and elimination (rituals crowd them out), then anxiety and ineffective coping, then role performance (school, relationships).","Act: Don’t interrupt rituals abruptly or punish them — at first, build time for them into a structured daily schedule. Then gradually limit them with her agreement. Meet physical needs, protect her skin, use a nonjudgmental approach, teach relaxation, and support exposure and response prevention and the SSRI.","Reassess: Measure time spent on rituals each day, the condition of her skin, intake and weight, sleep, attendance at therapy and classes, and her anxiety level when rituals are delayed."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Identify obsessions (unwanted, intrusive thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety) and compulsions (repetitive behaviors or mental acts done to reduce that anxiety), how much time they take (more than 1 hour a day), how they impair her function, and the physical damage they cause (skin, nutrition, sleep, elimination). The cues fit OCD with contamination obsessions and washing compulsions: more than an hour a day, causing distress and impairment, with good insight. The washing has caused skin breakdown with possible infection, and the time spent on rituals is crowding out eating, sleep and school.","Priorities: impaired skin integrity and infection risk, then nutrition, sleep and elimination (rituals crowd them out), then anxiety and ineffective coping, then role performance (school, relationships). Don’t interrupt rituals abruptly or punish them — at first, build time for them into a structured daily schedule. Then gradually limit them with her agreement. Meet physical needs, protect her skin, use a nonjudgmental approach, teach relaxation, and support exposure and response prevention and the SSRI.","Measure time spent on rituals each day, the condition of her skin, intake and weight, sleep, attendance at therapy and classes, and her anxiety level when rituals are delayed."],"requires":[],"terms":["Obsession","Compulsion","Fissured, bleeding skin","Insight","sertraline","fluoxetine","paroxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Obsession":"A constant, unwanted fear that germs on her hands will make her family sick. An intrusive thought the person recognizes as coming from their own mind (unlike a delusion), but can’t stop, and it creates intense anxiety. Usually ego-dystonic: the person knows it is excessive and is distressed by it. Ask about the content without judgment.","Compulsion":"Washing hands in a fixed sequence 60 times a day; checking the stove 20 times. The ritual relieves the anxiety briefly. Relief is negative reinforcement, so the ritual gets stronger each time. Interrupting it abruptly sends anxiety soaring, so you don’t stop the ritual cold. Limits are set gradually, with her involved.","Fissured, bleeding skin":"Cracked, raw hands; redness and warmth over the knuckles. Repeated washing strips the skin’s protective oils, so it cracks, and bacteria can get in. A physical priority: skin integrity and infection. Lotion, gloves at night and a provider assessment for cellulitis.","Insight":"“I know it sounds crazy, but I can’t stop.” Most people with OCD have good or fair insight; some have poor insight or none. Good insight helps her engage in therapy; use it to set goals together."},"questions":[{"id":"q-ocd-1","sourceId":"q-ocd-1","q":"A newly admitted patient with OCD washes her hands for hours before breakfast. Which initial intervention is best?","o":["Lock the bathroom","Build time for the ritual into her schedule, then gradually limit it","Tell her the ritual is unnecessary","Skip breakfast until the ritual stops"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Stopping a ritual abruptly sends anxiety soaring. Allow time at first, then limit it gradually with her.","Supported. Stopping a ritual abruptly sends anxiety soaring. Allow time at first, then limit it gradually with her.","Not supported for this question. Stopping a ritual abruptly sends anxiety soaring. Allow time at first, then limit it gradually with her.","Not supported for this question. Stopping a ritual abruptly sends anxiety soaring. Allow time at first, then limit it gradually with her."],"phys":"Stopping a ritual abruptly sends anxiety soaring. Allow time at first, then limit it gradually with her.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-ocd-2","sourceId":"q-ocd-2","q":"Which treatment is first-line behavioral therapy for OCD?","o":["Exposure and response prevention","Flooding with no therapist present","Aversion therapy","Hypnosis"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. ERP: exposure to the trigger while preventing the ritual, so the anxiety rises and falls on its own.","Not supported for this question. ERP: exposure to the trigger while preventing the ritual, so the anxiety rises and falls on its own.","Not supported for this question. ERP: exposure to the trigger while preventing the ritual, so the anxiety rises and falls on its own.","Not supported for this question. ERP: exposure to the trigger while preventing the ritual, so the anxiety rises and falls on its own."],"phys":"ERP: exposure to the trigger while preventing the ritual, so the anxiety rises and falls on its own.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-ocd"},{"id":"c-bipolar","ch":"13","chapters":["13"],"title":"Bipolar I — acute mania","patient":"28-year-old brought in by police after weaving on the highway. Hasn’t slept in 4 nights, talking nonstop, says she is “on the way to fame and fortune,” gave her paycheck to strangers. Sister says she stopped her lithium about 5 weeks ago. Weight down 3 kg this week.","notes":["c-bipolar"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 13; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Connect the cues. This is mania (the mood plus the energy, lasting a week, with impairment), triggered by stopping lithium. The danger comes from the physical cost: no sleep and no intake lead to dehydration, exhaustion and cardiac strain. Rule out what can mimic mania: stimulants, steroids, hyperthyroidism and alcohol.","Priority: The top priority is injury and physical exhaustion: hydration, nutrition, sleep and protection from her own impulsive or aggressive acts. Then come impaired judgment (money, sexuality, safety of others), then medication adherence. Remember that suicide risk is real in bipolar disorder, especially in mixed states and on the way down from mania.","Act: Use a firm, calm, consistent approach and short, concrete statements. Keep stimulation low and redirect energy into solitary physical activity. Give high-calorie finger foods and fluids she can take on the move, and track intake and output and weight. Set clear limits with consequences. Lock up valuables. Give medication as ordered and watch for lithium toxicity. Seclusion or restraint is only a last resort, for imminent danger.","Reassess: Reassess: sleep hours, intake and output and weight, urine specific gravity, vital signs, lithium level and any toxicity signs, how long redirection lasts, and incidents of aggression. Ready for discharge when she sleeps, eats, controls her behavior, can name her early warning signs (less sleep, racing thoughts, spending) and has a plan for lithium levels and follow-up."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Look for an elevated, expansive or irritable mood lasting at least a week, with increased energy: less need for sleep, pressured speech, flight of ideas, grandiosity, distractibility and risky behavior. Then look at the body: no sleep, no food, dehydration and exhaustion are what make mania dangerous. Connect the cues. This is mania (the mood plus the energy, lasting a week, with impairment), triggered by stopping lithium. The danger comes from the physical cost: no sleep and no intake lead to dehydration, exhaustion and cardiac strain. Rule out what can mimic mania: stimulants, steroids, hyperthyroidism and alcohol.","The top priority is injury and physical exhaustion: hydration, nutrition, sleep and protection from her own impulsive or aggressive acts. Then come impaired judgment (money, sexuality, safety of others), then medication adherence. Remember that suicide risk is real in bipolar disorder, especially in mixed states and on the way down from mania. Use a firm, calm, consistent approach and short, concrete statements. Keep stimulation low and redirect energy into solitary physical activity. Give high-calorie finger foods and fluids she can take on the move, and track intake and output and weight. Set clear limits with consequences. Lock up valuables. Give medication as ordered and watch for lithium toxicity. Seclusion or restraint is only a last resort, for imminent danger.","Reassess: sleep hours, intake and output and weight, urine specific gravity, vital signs, lithium level and any toxicity signs, how long redirection lasts, and incidents of aggression. Ready for discharge when she sleeps, eats, controls her behavior, can name her early warning signs (less sleep, racing thoughts, spending) and has a plan for lithium levels and follow-up."],"requires":[],"terms":["Decreased need for sleep","Pressured speech","Flight of ideas","Grandiosity","Distractibility","Poor judgment and risk-taking","Psychomotor agitation","High urine specific gravity (above 1.030)","Tachycardia and rising BP","Medication nonadherence","olanzapine","quetiapine","aripiprazole","lithium","valproate","carbamazepine","lamotrigine","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Decreased need for sleep":"Up all night cleaning, calling people or writing plans, and says she feels rested after 2 hours. Mania is a state of overdriven arousal (high dopamine and norepinephrine signaling). The brain no longer sends the “tired” signal, so the body runs without recovery. The single most reliable early sign of a manic relapse. Without sleep, exhaustion, dehydration and cardiovascular collapse can follow, so rest is a nursing priority, not a comfort measure.","Pressured speech":"Loud, rapid, hard to interrupt; she keeps talking over you and doesn’t wait for answers. Thoughts are being generated faster than they can be spoken, so speech is pushed out with urgency. Keep your statements short and concrete, because a long explanation will not be taken in. Document examples word for word.","Flight of ideas":"“I’m going home… home sweet home… home is where the heart is… heart of the matter is I want out.” Rapid jumps from topic to topic, linked by sounds, puns or loose associations; the listener can usually follow the links. A thought-process finding that tells you the severity. Don’t try to follow every topic; redirect to one simple task.","Grandiosity":"“I’m going to buy the hospital.” “The president is waiting for my call.” An inflated self-esteem that can reach delusional intensity in severe mania. Don’t argue with it or reinforce it. Respond to the feeling and the reality: “You have big plans. Right now, let’s eat this sandwich.”","Distractibility":"Starts a sentence, notices the TV, then a nurse walking by, then the window. Attention can’t filter competing stimuli, so every sound or movement captures it. This is why stimulation is kept low and why redirection works: distractibility can be used to steer the patient away from conflict.","Poor judgment and risk-taking":"Gave away a paycheck, sexually provocative behavior, reckless driving, spending sprees. The brain’s brake on impulses (prefrontal control) is overridden by the reward drive. Protect the patient from consequences they wouldn’t choose when well: lock up valuables, set limits on sexual behavior, and assign staff thoughtfully.","Psychomotor agitation":"Pacing, unable to sit, constant movement, may skip meals and fluids entirely. Excessive energy plus distractibility means the body burns calories and fluid much faster than they are replaced. Risk for dehydration, weight loss and exhaustion. Track intake and output, daily weights and vital signs.","High urine specific gravity (above 1.030)":"Concentrated, dark urine; dry lips and mucous membranes. The kidneys conserve water when intake is low and losses (sweat, activity) are high. Objective evidence of dehydration. It also matters for lithium: dehydration raises the lithium level, so this patient is at risk once lithium is restarted.","Tachycardia and rising BP":"HR 112, BP 148/90 while pacing. Sympathetic overdrive from constant activity, with less circulating volume from dehydration. Trend these. In the textbook case, physical stability (VS, intake, sleep) is the first outcome, before insight.","Medication nonadherence":"“I stopped it. I feel amazing; I don’t need it.” Mania feels good at first, and lithium can blunt that high. Thirst, tremor and weight gain also push people to stop. The most common cause of relapse. Teaching later focuses on the early signs of relapse and why to continue lithium after the episode ends."},"questions":[{"id":"q-bp-2","sourceId":"q-bp-2","q":"Which teaching points are correct for a patient starting lithium? Select all that apply.","o":["Keep sodium intake consistent","Drink 1.5–3 L of fluid a day","Ibuprofen is a safe choice for headaches","Call the provider if you have vomiting, diarrhea or heavy sweating","Stop lithium once your mood is stable"],"a":[0,1,3],"r":["Supported. Lithium follows sodium and water: keep both steady; fluid loss raises the level. NSAIDs raise lithium levels. It is continued after the episode to prevent relapse.","Supported. Lithium follows sodium and water: keep both steady; fluid loss raises the level. NSAIDs raise lithium levels. It is continued after the episode to prevent relapse.","Not supported for this question. Lithium follows sodium and water: keep both steady; fluid loss raises the level. NSAIDs raise lithium levels. It is continued after the episode to prevent relapse.","Supported. Lithium follows sodium and water: keep both steady; fluid loss raises the level. NSAIDs raise lithium levels. It is continued after the episode to prevent relapse.","Not supported for this question. Lithium follows sodium and water: keep both steady; fluid loss raises the level. NSAIDs raise lithium levels. It is continued after the episode to prevent relapse."],"phys":"Lithium follows sodium and water: keep both steady; fluid loss raises the level. NSAIDs raise lithium levels. It is continued after the episode to prevent relapse.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-bp-3","sourceId":"q-bp-3","q":"A patient with acute mania has not eaten in 2 days and is pacing constantly. Which intervention is best?","o":["Ask her to sit down for a full meal in the dining room","Offer high-calorie finger foods and fluids she can take while moving","Wait until she is hungry","Schedule her for a nutrition group"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Distractibility and hyperactivity keep her from sitting through a meal: finger foods and drinks “on the run.”","Supported. Distractibility and hyperactivity keep her from sitting through a meal: finger foods and drinks “on the run.”","Not supported for this question. Distractibility and hyperactivity keep her from sitting through a meal: finger foods and drinks “on the run.”","Not supported for this question. Distractibility and hyperactivity keep her from sitting through a meal: finger foods and drinks “on the run.”"],"phys":"Distractibility and hyperactivity keep her from sitting through a meal: finger foods and drinks “on the run.”","ex":[]},{"id":"q-bp-4","sourceId":"q-bp-4","q":"Which activity is most appropriate for a patient in acute mania?","o":["A competitive volleyball game","Walking with a staff member in a quiet hallway","Leading a community meeting","Watching an action movie in the dayroom"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Solitary, noncompetitive physical activity in a low-stimulation area burns energy without escalating.","Supported. Solitary, noncompetitive physical activity in a low-stimulation area burns energy without escalating.","Not supported for this question. Solitary, noncompetitive physical activity in a low-stimulation area burns energy without escalating.","Not supported for this question. Solitary, noncompetitive physical activity in a low-stimulation area burns energy without escalating."],"phys":"Solitary, noncompetitive physical activity in a low-stimulation area burns energy without escalating.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-bp-5","sourceId":"q-bp-5","q":"A patient taking valproate needs which monitoring? Select all that apply.","o":["Liver function tests","Platelet count","Pregnancy status","Serum lithium level","Signs of pancreatitis"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Valproate: hepatotoxicity, thrombocytopenia, pancreatitis and teratogenicity (neural tube defects). A lithium level isn’t relevant.","Supported. Valproate: hepatotoxicity, thrombocytopenia, pancreatitis and teratogenicity (neural tube defects). A lithium level isn’t relevant.","Supported. Valproate: hepatotoxicity, thrombocytopenia, pancreatitis and teratogenicity (neural tube defects). A lithium level isn’t relevant.","Not supported for this question. Valproate: hepatotoxicity, thrombocytopenia, pancreatitis and teratogenicity (neural tube defects). A lithium level isn’t relevant.","Supported. Valproate: hepatotoxicity, thrombocytopenia, pancreatitis and teratogenicity (neural tube defects). A lithium level isn’t relevant."],"phys":"Valproate: hepatotoxicity, thrombocytopenia, pancreatitis and teratogenicity (neural tube defects). A lithium level isn’t relevant.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-bp-6","sourceId":"q-bp-6","q":"What distinguishes hypomania from mania?","o":["Hypomania lasts longer","Hypomania does not cause marked impairment or psychosis","Hypomania involves depression","Only mania involves elevated mood"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Hypomania (4+ days) is a noticeable change without marked impairment or psychosis; mania (1+ week) causes marked impairment and can include psychosis.","Supported. Hypomania (4+ days) is a noticeable change without marked impairment or psychosis; mania (1+ week) causes marked impairment and can include psychosis.","Not supported for this question. Hypomania (4+ days) is a noticeable change without marked impairment or psychosis; mania (1+ week) causes marked impairment and can include psychosis.","Not supported for this question. Hypomania (4+ days) is a noticeable change without marked impairment or psychosis; mania (1+ week) causes marked impairment and can include psychosis."],"phys":"Hypomania (4+ days) is a noticeable change without marked impairment or psychosis; mania (1+ week) causes marked impairment and can include psychosis.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-bipolar"},{"id":"c-mdd","ch":"14","chapters":["14"],"title":"Major depressive disorder","patient":"52-year-old admitted after 6 weeks of low mood since being laid off. Wakes at 3 a.m. every night, has lost 5 kg, stopped showering, speaks slowly in a monotone. Says “my family would be better off without me.” Sertraline was started 10 days ago.","notes":["c-mdd"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 14; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: The cues fit major depressive disorder, with suicidal ideation, access to lethal means (rifle), male sex, middle age, living alone and recent loss, all of which raise the risk. Note the timing: he is 10 days into an SSRI, when energy may return before mood improves.","Priority: The first priority is suicide risk: keep him safe and observed and remove the means. Next come the physical needs (nutrition, fluids, sleep, hygiene, elimination), then hopelessness and social isolation, then teaching about the medication and follow-up.","Act: Start suicide precautions per the risk level, remove harmful objects, and ask about the plan, means and intent directly. Spend short, frequent times with him; sit in silence if he can’t talk; make observations rather than asking many questions; avoid platitudes. Meet the physical needs step by step. Give antidepressants, teach, and watch for serotonin syndrome, TCA overdose and the MAOI–tyramine reaction. Consider ECT for severe or psychotic depression.","Reassess: Reassess suicide risk at every shift, and especially with a sudden improvement in mood, before passes, before changing the observation level and at discharge. Track sleep, intake and weight, hygiene, attendance at groups and statements of hope. Watch for side effects and serotonin syndrome."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Look for depressed mood or anhedonia most of the day, nearly every day, for at least 2 weeks, plus the vegetative signs: sleep (early waking or hypersomnia), appetite and weight, energy, psychomotor change, concentration, guilt or worthlessness, and thoughts of death. Hear the covert suicide message. The cues fit major depressive disorder, with suicidal ideation, access to lethal means (rifle), male sex, middle age, living alone and recent loss, all of which raise the risk. Note the timing: he is 10 days into an SSRI, when energy may return before mood improves.","The first priority is suicide risk: keep him safe and observed and remove the means. Next come the physical needs (nutrition, fluids, sleep, hygiene, elimination), then hopelessness and social isolation, then teaching about the medication and follow-up. Start suicide precautions per the risk level, remove harmful objects, and ask about the plan, means and intent directly. Spend short, frequent times with him; sit in silence if he can’t talk; make observations rather than asking many questions; avoid platitudes. Meet the physical needs step by step. Give antidepressants, teach, and watch for serotonin syndrome, TCA overdose and the MAOI–tyramine reaction. Consider ECT for severe or psychotic depression.","Reassess suicide risk at every shift, and especially with a sudden improvement in mood, before passes, before changing the observation level and at discharge. Track sleep, intake and weight, hygiene, attendance at groups and statements of hope. Watch for side effects and serotonin syndrome."],"requires":[],"terms":["Anhedonia","Psychomotor retardation","Flat or blunted affect","Early-morning awakening","Worthlessness, guilt, hopelessness","Covert suicide message","The “energy before mood” window","Weight loss more than 5% in a month","sertraline","citalopram","escitalopram","venlafaxine","bupropion","fluoxetine","paroxetine","duloxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Anhedonia":"“Nothing feels good anymore. I used to love fishing; now I don’t care.” Reduced activity in the brain’s reward pathway (dopamine), so activities no longer produce pleasure or motivation. One of the two key symptoms (with depressed mood); at least one of the two is required for the diagnosis.","Psychomotor retardation":"Slow movements, long pauses before answering, a fixed gaze, slumped posture; in severe cases mute and not moving. Depression slows both motor and cognitive processing (anergia: an abnormal lack of energy). Allow time to respond, and use simple words. Watch for complications of immobility: constipation, skin breakdown, poor intake.","Flat or blunted affect":"Almost no facial expression, a monotone voice, doesn’t smile at news that should be good. Affect is the observed outward expression of emotion. Constricted → blunted → flat is a range of decreasing expression. Document it as an objective finding. Note whether affect matches the stated mood (congruent); a sudden bright affect can be a warning sign.","Early-morning awakening":"Wakes at 3–4 a.m. and can’t get back to sleep; mood is worst in the morning (diurnal variation). Depression disrupts sleep architecture: less deep sleep, with REM sleep coming early. A hallmark vegetative sign. Track sleep hours; improvement in sleep is often one of the first signs that treatment is working.","Worthlessness, guilt, hopelessness":"“I’m a burden.” “It’s all my fault.” “It will never get better.” Negative thinking about self, world and future (Beck’s cognitive triad) is a core feature of depression. Hopelessness is the feeling most linked to suicide. It is your cue to ask directly about suicidal thoughts.","Covert suicide message":"“My family would be better off without me.” “I won’t be a problem much longer.” Many people hint before they state intent directly; ambivalence leaves room to be rescued. Always follow up with a direct question: “Are you thinking about killing yourself?” Asking does not plant the idea.","The “energy before mood” window":"In the 2nd–4th week of an antidepressant, the patient is more active and talkative but still hopeless. Sleep, appetite and energy often improve before the depressed thinking lifts. A patient who was too slowed to act may now have the energy to carry out a plan. Watch closely in the first weeks of treatment and after any dose change. This is why antidepressants carry a black box warning about suicidality in patients under 25.","Weight loss more than 5% in a month":"Loose clothing, 5 kg down, leaving most of the meal tray. Appetite loss is a vegetative sign; anhedonia removes the pleasure of eating. Weigh weekly, offer small frequent high-calorie meals and favorite foods, and check labs if intake is very poor."},"questions":[{"id":"q-mdd-2","sourceId":"q-mdd-2","q":"A patient taking phenelzine should avoid which foods? Select all that apply.","o":["Aged cheddar cheese","Salami","Fresh chicken","Draft (tap) beer","Cottage cheese"],"a":[0,1,3],"r":["Supported. Aged, cured, fermented and tap beer are high in tyramine → hypertensive crisis with an MAOI. Fresh meat and cottage cheese are safe.","Supported. Aged, cured, fermented and tap beer are high in tyramine → hypertensive crisis with an MAOI. Fresh meat and cottage cheese are safe.","Not supported for this question. Aged, cured, fermented and tap beer are high in tyramine → hypertensive crisis with an MAOI. Fresh meat and cottage cheese are safe.","Supported. Aged, cured, fermented and tap beer are high in tyramine → hypertensive crisis with an MAOI. Fresh meat and cottage cheese are safe.","Not supported for this question. Aged, cured, fermented and tap beer are high in tyramine → hypertensive crisis with an MAOI. Fresh meat and cottage cheese are safe."],"phys":"Aged, cured, fermented and tap beer are high in tyramine → hypertensive crisis with an MAOI. Fresh meat and cottage cheese are safe.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-mdd-3","sourceId":"q-mdd-3","q":"A patient on sertraline who was recently given tramadol has a temperature of 39.5 °C, agitation, diaphoresis, tremor and hyperreflexia. The nurse suspects:","o":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Hypertensive crisis","Lithium toxicity"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Two serotonergic drugs + mental status change, autonomic instability and neuromuscular hyperactivity = serotonin syndrome. Stop the drugs; supportive care.","Supported. Two serotonergic drugs + mental status change, autonomic instability and neuromuscular hyperactivity = serotonin syndrome. Stop the drugs; supportive care.","Not supported for this question. Two serotonergic drugs + mental status change, autonomic instability and neuromuscular hyperactivity = serotonin syndrome. Stop the drugs; supportive care.","Not supported for this question. Two serotonergic drugs + mental status change, autonomic instability and neuromuscular hyperactivity = serotonin syndrome. Stop the drugs; supportive care."],"phys":"Two serotonergic drugs + mental status change, autonomic instability and neuromuscular hyperactivity = serotonin syndrome. Stop the drugs; supportive care.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-mdd-4","sourceId":"q-mdd-4","q":"Which antidepressant class is most dangerous in overdose?","o":["SSRIs","Tricyclic antidepressants","SNRIs","Mirtazapine"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. TCAs cause fatal cardiac dysrhythmias in overdose; dispense small amounts to patients at risk for suicide.","Supported. TCAs cause fatal cardiac dysrhythmias in overdose; dispense small amounts to patients at risk for suicide.","Not supported for this question. TCAs cause fatal cardiac dysrhythmias in overdose; dispense small amounts to patients at risk for suicide.","Not supported for this question. TCAs cause fatal cardiac dysrhythmias in overdose; dispense small amounts to patients at risk for suicide."],"phys":"TCAs cause fatal cardiac dysrhythmias in overdose; dispense small amounts to patients at risk for suicide.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-mdd-5","sourceId":"q-mdd-5","q":"A severely withdrawn depressed patient sits silently. Which approach is best?","o":["Ask a series of questions to get him talking","Sit with him, make simple observations and allow time to respond","Leave him alone until he is ready to talk","Encourage him to cheer up"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. With slowed thinking, offer self, make observations and give time. Many questions overwhelm; platitudes minimize.","Supported. With slowed thinking, offer self, make observations and give time. Many questions overwhelm; platitudes minimize.","Not supported for this question. With slowed thinking, offer self, make observations and give time. Many questions overwhelm; platitudes minimize.","Not supported for this question. With slowed thinking, offer self, make observations and give time. Many questions overwhelm; platitudes minimize."],"phys":"With slowed thinking, offer self, make observations and give time. Many questions overwhelm; platitudes minimize.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-mdd-6","sourceId":"q-mdd-6","q":"After ECT, which finding does the nurse expect?","o":["Permanent loss of long-term memory","Temporary confusion and short-term memory loss","Hypertensive crisis","Severe muscle pain from the seizure"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Transient confusion and short-term memory loss are expected; a muscle relaxant prevents injury during the seizure.","Supported. Transient confusion and short-term memory loss are expected; a muscle relaxant prevents injury during the seizure.","Not supported for this question. Transient confusion and short-term memory loss are expected; a muscle relaxant prevents injury during the seizure.","Not supported for this question. Transient confusion and short-term memory loss are expected; a muscle relaxant prevents injury during the seizure."],"phys":"Transient confusion and short-term memory loss are expected; a muscle relaxant prevents injury during the seizure.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-mdd-7","sourceId":"q-mdd-7","q":"Which findings are vegetative signs of depression? Select all that apply.","o":["Early-morning awakening","Loss of appetite and weight","Decreased libido","Flight of ideas","Constipation"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Vegetative signs involve sleep, appetite, sex and elimination. Flight of ideas is a sign of mania.","Supported. Vegetative signs involve sleep, appetite, sex and elimination. Flight of ideas is a sign of mania.","Supported. Vegetative signs involve sleep, appetite, sex and elimination. Flight of ideas is a sign of mania.","Not supported for this question. Vegetative signs involve sleep, appetite, sex and elimination. Flight of ideas is a sign of mania.","Supported. Vegetative signs involve sleep, appetite, sex and elimination. Flight of ideas is a sign of mania."],"phys":"Vegetative signs involve sleep, appetite, sex and elimination. Flight of ideas is a sign of mania.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-mdd"},{"id":"c-suicide","ch":"25","chapters":["25"],"title":"Suicide risk","patient":"24-year-old brought to the ED after friends found a goodbye note. Lost her job and broke up with her boyfriend this month. Drinking heavily. Says, “I just want the pain to stop.” Her boyfriend’s handgun is in her apartment. One prior overdose at age 19.","notes":["c-suicide"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 25; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: She has many risk factors at once: depression, a prior attempt, recent losses, alcohol, a specific plan with a firearm that is accessible. Few protective factors are active right now. That makes her high risk. Tools such as the SAFE-T (with the Columbia scale) put the risk level into a structured form.","Priority: The top priority is risk for suicide: physical safety comes first, with the most restrictive observation that the risk requires. After that: hopelessness, ineffective coping, social isolation and the alcohol use.","Act: Start suicide precautions: constant one-to-one observation for high risk (never out of sight, within arm’s length if needed), a search of the patient and her belongings, and removal of sharps, cords, belts and glass. Mouth checks after medications. Document her mood and words every 15 minutes. Build a relationship and talk about feelings. Involve her family and remove the means. Give medications (SSRIs are safer than TCAs in overdose).","Reassess: Reassess suicide risk every shift, before any change in observation level, before passes, at discharge, and whenever the mood suddenly lifts or drops. Look for reasons to live, use of coping skills, engagement with staff and peers, a completed safety plan and removal of the means. Plan a follow-up appointment within days and a follow-up phone call."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Listen for overt (“I want to die”) and covert (“You won’t have to worry about me much longer”) statements, and watch for behavior changes: giving things away, putting affairs in order, withdrawal, increased drinking, and a sudden calm. Then ask directly about thoughts, plan, means, intent and past attempts. She has many risk factors at once: depression, a prior attempt, recent losses, alcohol, a specific plan with a firearm that is accessible. Few protective factors are active right now. That makes her high risk. Tools such as the SAFE-T (with the Columbia scale) put the risk level into a structured form.","The top priority is risk for suicide: physical safety comes first, with the most restrictive observation that the risk requires. After that: hopelessness, ineffective coping, social isolation and the alcohol use. Start suicide precautions: constant one-to-one observation for high risk (never out of sight, within arm’s length if needed), a search of the patient and her belongings, and removal of sharps, cords, belts and glass. Mouth checks after medications. Document her mood and words every 15 minutes. Build a relationship and talk about feelings. Involve her family and remove the means. Give medications (SSRIs are safer than TCAs in overdose).","Reassess suicide risk every shift, before any change in observation level, before passes, at discharge, and whenever the mood suddenly lifts or drops. Look for reasons to live, use of coping skills, engagement with staff and peers, a completed safety plan and removal of the means. Plan a follow-up appointment within days and a follow-up phone call."],"requires":[],"terms":["Suicidal ideation","Plan, means, intent (lethality)","Hopelessness","Sudden calm or improvement","Alcohol and drug use","clozapine","lithium","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Suicidal ideation":"“I keep thinking everyone would be better off without me.” Thoughts of ending one’s life range from passive (“I wish I wouldn’t wake up”) to active, with a plan. The first thing to clarify: passive or active, how often, how strong, and whether there is a plan.","Plan, means, intent (lethality)":"“I’d use the gun in the apartment, this weekend when he’s gone.” Risk rises steeply with a specific plan, a lethal method (firearm, hanging, jumping), access to it, and a timeline. Specific + lethal + available + soon = highest risk → constant one-to-one observation and removal of the means.","Hopelessness":"“Nothing will ever change. There’s no point.” Hopelessness is the cognitive state most closely tied to suicide; it removes the reason to wait for things to improve. Ask about suicide whenever you hear it, and build hope with small goals and reasons for living.","Sudden calm or improvement":"A tearful, agitated patient is suddenly peaceful and smiling, and gives her necklace to a peer. Having made the decision can bring relief. Returning energy (early antidepressant treatment) can also make acting possible. Reassess immediately; never loosen precautions because the patient “seems better.”","Alcohol and drug use":"Blood alcohol 0.09; drinking every night this month. Alcohol lowers inhibition, worsens depression, and makes an impulsive act more likely and more lethal. A major risk factor; also plan for withdrawal monitoring."},"questions":[{"id":"q-sui-2","sourceId":"q-sui-2","q":"Which interventions are part of suicide precautions with 1:1 observation? Select all that apply.","o":["Keep the patient within sight at all times, including in the bathroom","Remove belts, shoelaces and sharp objects","Check that each medication is swallowed","Have the patient sign a no-suicide contract instead of observation","Keep the patient’s hands visible while sleeping"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. A no-suicide contract is not a safety measure and never replaces observation. A safety plan is used instead.","Supported. A no-suicide contract is not a safety measure and never replaces observation. A safety plan is used instead.","Supported. A no-suicide contract is not a safety measure and never replaces observation. A safety plan is used instead.","Not supported for this question. A no-suicide contract is not a safety measure and never replaces observation. A safety plan is used instead.","Supported. A no-suicide contract is not a safety measure and never replaces observation. A safety plan is used instead."],"phys":"A no-suicide contract is not a safety measure and never replaces observation. A safety plan is used instead.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-sui-3","sourceId":"q-sui-3","q":"A patient who has been severely depressed suddenly appears calm and is giving away her belongings. The nurse should:","o":["Document improvement and reduce observation","Reassess suicide risk immediately and increase observation","Encourage her to share her belongings","Prepare her for discharge"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Sudden calm and giving things away can mean she has decided to die. Reassess and increase observation.","Supported. Sudden calm and giving things away can mean she has decided to die. Reassess and increase observation.","Not supported for this question. Sudden calm and giving things away can mean she has decided to die. Reassess and increase observation.","Not supported for this question. Sudden calm and giving things away can mean she has decided to die. Reassess and increase observation."],"phys":"Sudden calm and giving things away can mean she has decided to die. Reassess and increase observation.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-sui-4","sourceId":"q-sui-4","q":"Which question is the best way to assess suicide risk?","o":["“You’re not thinking of hurting yourself, are you?”","“Are you thinking about killing yourself?”","“Why would you want to hurt yourself?”","“Everyone has bad days. How was yours?”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Ask directly, using the word. Asking doesn’t plant the idea; leading or “why” questions discourage honesty.","Supported. Ask directly, using the word. Asking doesn’t plant the idea; leading or “why” questions discourage honesty.","Not supported for this question. Ask directly, using the word. Asking doesn’t plant the idea; leading or “why” questions discourage honesty.","Not supported for this question. Ask directly, using the word. Asking doesn’t plant the idea; leading or “why” questions discourage honesty."],"phys":"Ask directly, using the word. Asking doesn’t plant the idea; leading or “why” questions discourage honesty.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-suicide"},{"id":"c-nssi","ch":"25","chapters":["25"],"title":"Nonsuicidal self-injury","patient":"16-year-old seen in the school clinic for cuts on her upper arms in different stages of healing, hidden under long sleeves on a hot day. She says, “It’s not like I want to die. It just makes the pressure go away.”","notes":["c-nssi"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 25; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: The cues point to NSSI used to manage emotional pain after a family stressor. Intent to die is denied, but NSSI doubles the later risk of a suicide attempt, so this is still a safety issue. One wound appears infected.","Priority: Priorities: wound care and infection, risk for self-mutilation, and the underlying anxiety, depression or trauma, while screening for suicide risk.","Act: Respond calmly and without judgment. Treat the wounds matter-of-factly, without lecturing, showing shock or giving extra attention. Build trust; explore triggers and the function of the injury; teach replacement coping skills (DBT: distress tolerance and emotion regulation). Make a plan to tell staff when urges rise, and keep the environment safe.","Reassess: Evaluate frequency and severity of injuries over time, whether she reports urges before acting, her use of new coping skills, wound healing, and suicide risk at every contact."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Notice patterned injuries (cuts, burns, scratches) in hidden places, at different stages of healing, long sleeves in warm weather, and statements about relieving tension or feeling something. Ask directly about suicidal intent, because NSSI and suicide can coexist. The cues point to NSSI used to manage emotional pain after a family stressor. Intent to die is denied, but NSSI doubles the later risk of a suicide attempt, so this is still a safety issue. One wound appears infected.","Priorities: wound care and infection, risk for self-mutilation, and the underlying anxiety, depression or trauma, while screening for suicide risk. Respond calmly and without judgment. Treat the wounds matter-of-factly, without lecturing, showing shock or giving extra attention. Build trust; explore triggers and the function of the injury; teach replacement coping skills (DBT: distress tolerance and emotion regulation). Make a plan to tell staff when urges rise, and keep the environment safe.","Evaluate frequency and severity of injuries over time, whether she reports urges before acting, her use of new coping skills, wound healing, and suicide risk at every contact."],"requires":[],"terms":["Nonsuicidal self-injury (NSSI)","“It makes the pressure go away”","Wounds at different stages"],"termDefinitions":{"Nonsuicidal self-injury (NSSI)":"Superficial, parallel cuts on the forearms or thighs; burns; head-banging; scratching. Deliberate damage to one’s own body without intent to die, used to regulate emotion: to escape overwhelming feelings, to end numbness, or as self-punishment. Not a suicide attempt, but a strong risk factor for later suicide attempts. Always assess suicidal intent separately.","“It makes the pressure go away”":"The patient describes rising tension and then relief right after injuring. Tissue damage releases endogenous opioids (endorphins), which give brief relief or even a “high” (pain offset relief). The relief reinforces the behavior. This is why it repeats: it works in the short term. The plan is to replace it with other ways to tolerate distress, not just forbid it.","Wounds at different stages":"Fresh cuts beside healed scars. Shows a repeated pattern rather than one event. Assess frequency and escalation, and check each wound for infection and the need for sutures."},"questions":[{"id":"q-nssi-1","sourceId":"q-nssi-1","q":"A teen with fresh cuts on her forearms says she cuts “to make the pressure go away.” Which nursing response is best?","o":["“Promise me you’ll never do this again.”","“Let me take care of these cuts. What was happening right before you cut?”","“This is attention-seeking behavior.”","“You’ll be restrained if you cut again.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Calm, nonjudgmental wound care plus exploring triggers builds trust. Promises, labels and threats increase shame.","Supported. Calm, nonjudgmental wound care plus exploring triggers builds trust. Promises, labels and threats increase shame.","Not supported for this question. Calm, nonjudgmental wound care plus exploring triggers builds trust. Promises, labels and threats increase shame.","Not supported for this question. Calm, nonjudgmental wound care plus exploring triggers builds trust. Promises, labels and threats increase shame."],"phys":"Calm, nonjudgmental wound care plus exploring triggers builds trust. Promises, labels and threats increase shame.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-nssi-2","sourceId":"q-nssi-2","q":"Which therapy has the strongest evidence for nonsuicidal self-injury?","o":["Electroconvulsive therapy","Dialectical behavior therapy","Aversion therapy","Psychoanalysis"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. DBT teaches distress tolerance, emotion regulation, mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness.","Supported. DBT teaches distress tolerance, emotion regulation, mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness.","Not supported for this question. DBT teaches distress tolerance, emotion regulation, mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness.","Not supported for this question. DBT teaches distress tolerance, emotion regulation, mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness."],"phys":"DBT teaches distress tolerance, emotion regulation, mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-nssi"},{"id":"c-ptsd","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"PTSD and related disorders","patient":"26-year-old veteran seen in clinic 3 months after returning from deployment. Wakes from nightmares drenched in sweat, startles at loud noises, won’t go to crowded stores, drinks “to sleep,” and says he feels “numb” around his wife and kids. Last week he dove behind a car when a truck backfired.","notes":["c-ptsd"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 16; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Combat trauma, plus symptoms in all four clusters lasting more than 1 month, with impairment = PTSD. Complicating risks: alcohol use, emotional withdrawal from family, and a loaded firearm in a man with hyperarousal and possible depression.","Priority: Priorities: safety (suicide risk, firearm access, impulsive anger), then sleep disturbance and substance use, then anxiety, ineffective coping and family processes. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing the trauma, then reconnection.","Act: Use trauma-informed care: safety, trust, choice, collaboration and empowerment, and ask “what happened to you?”, not “what’s wrong with you?”. Stay calm and predictable; don’t push for details of the trauma. Teach grounding and relaxation. Address sleep, alcohol and the firearm. Refer for trauma-focused CBT or EMDR. Give medications as ordered.","Reassess: Track nightmares and sleep hours, startle and irritability, avoidance (is he going places again?), alcohol use, his use of grounding skills, family relationships, and suicide risk at every visit."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Confirm exposure to a traumatic event (actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence: experienced, witnessed, learned about, or repeated exposure to details). Then look for the four symptom clusters: intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in thinking and mood, and changes in arousal and reactivity. Note how long it has lasted. Combat trauma, plus symptoms in all four clusters lasting more than 1 month, with impairment = PTSD. Complicating risks: alcohol use, emotional withdrawal from family, and a loaded firearm in a man with hyperarousal and possible depression.","Priorities: safety (suicide risk, firearm access, impulsive anger), then sleep disturbance and substance use, then anxiety, ineffective coping and family processes. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing the trauma, then reconnection. Use trauma-informed care: safety, trust, choice, collaboration and empowerment, and ask “what happened to you?”, not “what’s wrong with you?”. Stay calm and predictable; don’t push for details of the trauma. Teach grounding and relaxation. Address sleep, alcohol and the firearm. Refer for trauma-focused CBT or EMDR. Give medications as ordered.","Track nightmares and sleep hours, startle and irritability, avoidance (is he going places again?), alcohol use, his use of grounding skills, family relationships, and suicide risk at every visit."],"requires":[],"terms":["Intrusion (re-experiencing)","Avoidance","Negative thoughts and mood; emotional numbing","Hyperarousal and hypervigilance","Self-medicating with alcohol","sertraline","venlafaxine","paroxetine","psychotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{"Intrusion (re-experiencing)":"Nightmares, flashbacks, intrusive memories; panic at reminders. Trauma memories are stored in a fragmented, sensory way (the amygdala tags them as “current danger” and the hippocampus doesn’t file them as past), so reminders bring them back as if happening now. During a flashback, ground him in the present: his name, where he is, the date, that he is safe.","Avoidance":"Won’t go to crowded stores or talk about deployment; avoids news and fireworks. Avoiding reminders lowers anxiety in the moment, which reinforces the avoidance and keeps the fear from fading. Gradual, supported exposure in therapy is what lets the fear fade.","Negative thoughts and mood; emotional numbing":"“I feel nothing for my kids.” Guilt that he survived (survivor’s guilt); “The world is dangerous.” Numbing protects him from overwhelming emotion but also shuts out positive feelings. Screen for depression and suicide; the numbness strains his relationships.","Hyperarousal and hypervigilance":"Exaggerated startle, always scanning exits, irritability or angry outbursts, insomnia, poor concentration. The sympathetic nervous system stays switched on, as if the threat were still present. Approach calmly, announce yourself, avoid touching without warning, and give him a seat with a view of the door if possible.","Self-medicating with alcohol":"Drinks every night “to sleep.” Alcohol briefly dampens arousal, but it disrupts sleep, worsens depression and increases impulsivity. Screen for substance use (very common with PTSD) and for suicide, especially with a firearm at home."},"questions":[{"id":"q-ptsd-2","sourceId":"q-ptsd-2","q":"A patient with PTSD has a flashback in the dayroom. The nurse should first:","o":["Ask the patient to describe the trauma in detail","Use the patient’s name, orient him to where he is and that he is safe, and use grounding","Leave him alone until it passes","Give a PRN and place him in seclusion"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Grounding and orientation bring the patient back to the present. Don’t push for details.","Supported. Grounding and orientation bring the patient back to the present. Don’t push for details.","Not supported for this question. Grounding and orientation bring the patient back to the present. Don’t push for details.","Not supported for this question. Grounding and orientation bring the patient back to the present. Don’t push for details."],"phys":"Grounding and orientation bring the patient back to the present. Don’t push for details.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-ptsd-3","sourceId":"q-ptsd-3","q":"Which medication may be prescribed for PTSD-related nightmares?","o":["Prazosin","Lithium","Lamotrigine","Bupropion"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Prazosin (an alpha-1 blocker) reduces nightmares; watch for orthostatic hypotension.","Not supported for this question. Prazosin (an alpha-1 blocker) reduces nightmares; watch for orthostatic hypotension.","Not supported for this question. Prazosin (an alpha-1 blocker) reduces nightmares; watch for orthostatic hypotension.","Not supported for this question. Prazosin (an alpha-1 blocker) reduces nightmares; watch for orthostatic hypotension."],"phys":"Prazosin (an alpha-1 blocker) reduces nightmares; watch for orthostatic hypotension.","ex":[]},{"id":"q-ptsd-4","sourceId":"q-ptsd-4","q":"Three weeks after a car accident, a patient has intrusive memories and trouble sleeping. Which diagnosis fits best?","o":["PTSD","Acute stress disorder","Dissociative identity disorder","Bipolar II disorder"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Symptoms between 3 days and 1 month after a trauma = acute stress disorder.","Supported. Symptoms between 3 days and 1 month after a trauma = acute stress disorder.","Not supported for this question. Symptoms between 3 days and 1 month after a trauma = acute stress disorder.","Not supported for this question. Symptoms between 3 days and 1 month after a trauma = acute stress disorder."],"phys":"Symptoms between 3 days and 1 month after a trauma = acute stress disorder.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-ptsd"}],"studios":{"version":1,"mapPage":"Exam 4 Map.html","learnPage":"Exam 4 Learn.html","labs":[{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Illness, functioning and recovery answer different questions","cards":[{"title":"Illness","text":"What symptoms and impairment need assessment?"},{"title":"Function","text":"What can the person do, and where is support needed?"},{"title":"Recovery","text":"What matters to this person’s life and goals?"}],"caption":"Ask about all three instead of reducing the person to a label."},"strategy":{"title":"Illness, functioning and recovery answer different questions","task":"Which nursing goal best supports recovery?","cue":"The patient says joining a walking group would help rebuild friendships.","rule":"This connects the person’s chosen goal with a practical action and needed support.","trap":"Look for the person’s own meaningful goal, not a requirement for complete symptom absence."},"id":"t-intro","ch":"1","chapters":["1"],"title":"Mental health and mental illness","notes":["t-intro"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 1; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Start with the person’s experience and functioning. A diagnosis describes an illness; it does not tell you everything about relationships, strengths, culture or ability to cope today.","Stress, biology, experience and access to support interact. Ask what changed, how the person understands it, and what helps. A cultural difference alone is not evidence of a disorder.","Recovery can mean a meaningful, self-directed life while treatment continues. Nursing care combines safety assessment, practical support, respectful language and goals chosen with the person."],"terms":["Mental health continuum","Resilience","Recovery","Stigma","psychotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{"Mental health continuum":"The same person can be thriving, struggling, unwell, then recovering over a lifetime. Health and illness are not two boxes: stress, supports, biology and coping shift where someone sits at any moment. You assess where the patient is now and what moves them toward health, rather than labeling them by a diagnosis.","Resilience":"Someone who goes through a divorce or job loss, grieves, and then adapts and rebuilds. The ability to bounce back from adversity, supported by relationships, optimism, problem-solving and a sense of purpose. A protective factor you can build: point out strengths, connect patients to support, teach coping skills.","Recovery":"A person with schizophrenia who lives on their own, works part-time and manages symptoms. Recovery is a process of change in which people improve their health, live self-directed lives and reach their potential, even if some symptoms remain. The modern goal of care: hope, person-centered choices and peer support, not just symptom control.","Stigma":"“He’s crazy.” Avoiding a coworker after learning about their diagnosis. Not seeking help out of shame. Negative stereotypes about mental illness lead to discrimination (public stigma) and to shame that people turn against themselves (self-stigma). A major barrier to treatment. Use person-first language (“a person with bipolar disorder,” not “a bipolar”)."},"patient":"A patient receiving treatment for depression is back at work and wants to rebuild friendships. Some symptoms remain.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-intro","title":"Mental health and mental illness","blocks":[{"title":"What’s “normal” depends on culture","lines":["Behavior is judged against the norms of a culture: hearing a deceased relative’s voice during grief may be expected in one culture and alarming in another.","Ask about meaning and impact on functioning before deciding a behavior is a symptom."]},{"title":"How common mental illness is","lines":["About 1 in 5 adults has a mental illness in a given year; about 1 in 20 has a serious mental illness.","Half of all lifetime disorders start by age 14, three-quarters by the mid-20s.","Most common: anxiety disorders, then major depression.","Many people never get treatment, and on average there is a long delay between onset and treatment.","Epidemiology is the study of patterns of disease; incidence = new cases in a period; prevalence = all existing cases at a time.","Prevalence tells you how many patients on a med-surg unit are likely to have a co-occurring mental illness: many."]},{"title":"Causes: it’s never just one thing","lines":["Biologic: genetics, neurotransmitters, brain structure, hormones, infection, inflammation.","Psychological: temperament, learned patterns, trauma, coping style.","Social and environmental: poverty, discrimination, isolation, adverse childhood experiences, substance exposure.","The stress-diathesis (vulnerability + stress) model ties these together."]},{"title":"The psychiatric-mental health nurse","lines":["Basic level (RN): assessment, coordination of care, health teaching, milieu therapy, self-care support, psychobiologic interventions (giving and monitoring medication), crisis care, case management.","Advanced practice (PMH-APRN): psychotherapy, prescribing, consultation.","Nurses care for the whole person: physical, emotional, social and spiritual, in every setting."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"The patient says joining a walking group would help rebuild friendships.","task":"Which nursing goal best supports recovery?","hint":"Look for the person’s own meaningful goal, not a requirement for complete symptom absence.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Wait for every symptom to disappear before discussing social goals.","why":"Recovery work can occur alongside ongoing symptoms and treatment.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Explore a manageable first step toward the walking group together.","why":"This connects the person’s chosen goal with a practical action and needed support.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"Replace that goal with the nurse’s preferred hobby.","why":"The nurse would be choosing the person’s priorities rather than collaborating.","correct":false}],"id":"t-intro:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient describes a grief experience that has an accepted meaning in their culture. There is no stated immediate danger.","task":"What information is most useful next?","hint":"Assess the person in context before applying a label.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"The patient’s meaning, distress, functioning and safety.","why":"Context and impact guide assessment; neither cultural unfamiliarity nor reassurance alone settles it.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Whether the nurse has personally had the same experience.","why":"The nurse’s personal experience is not the standard for deciding whether a symptom is present.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"A diagnosis based only on the unusual description.","why":"One unfamiliar description is insufficient for a diagnosis.","correct":false}],"id":"t-intro:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Culture and mental health","cards":[{"title":"Three worldviews (broad patterns, not rules)","text":"Western: the individual is the source of identity; autonomy; mind and body separate; disease has a measurable cause; time is linear; plan for the future. · Eastern: the family is the source of identity; group decisions; mind–body–spirit unity; illness = imbalance (yin–yang, hot–cold); time is circular. · Indigenous: the community and nature; illness = loss of harmony with others, nature or the spirit world; time is the present."},{"title":"Beliefs that differ from Western care","text":"Decisions: made by the patient (Western) vs by the family or its head. · Telling the truth: full disclosure vs protecting the patient from bad news to preserve hope. · Sick role: be independent and get moving vs family cares for the sick person, rest helps recovery."},{"title":"Nonverbal communication varies","text":"Eye contact: respect in the US; rude, challenging or sexual in some cultures. · Personal space: US personal distance about 1.5–4 ft; others stand closer or farther. · Touch: high-touch vs low-touch cultures (touch between men and women may be taboo)."}],"caption":"Worldviews, barriers to care, cultural syndromes, and culturally congruent care."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Who should interpret for a patient with limited English in a psychiatric interview?","cue":"Worldviews, barriers to care, cultural syndromes, and culturally congruent care.","rule":"A professional medical interpreter, never family or friends.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-culture","ch":"5","chapters":["5"],"title":"Culture and mental health","notes":["t-culture"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 5; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Culture decides what counts as normal, how distress is felt and described, who makes decisions, and what treatment people trust. Psychiatric nursing grew out of Western ideas, so the nurse checks their own assumptions, assesses each patient’s beliefs, and builds a plan that fits them: culturally congruent care.","Three worldviews (broad patterns, not rules): Western: the individual is the source of identity; autonomy; mind and body separate; disease has a measurable cause; time is linear; plan for the future.; Eastern: the family is the source of identity; group decisions; mind–body–spirit unity; illness = imbalance (yin–yang, hot–cold); time is circular.; Indigenous: the community and nature; illness = loss of harmony with others, nature or the spirit world; time is the present.","Beliefs that differ from Western care: Decisions: made by the patient (Western) vs by the family or its head.; Telling the truth: full disclosure vs protecting the patient from bad news to preserve hope.; Sick role: be independent and get moving vs family cares for the sick person, rest helps recovery.; Treatment: prescribed medicines and technology vs balance-restoring remedies and traditional healers.; Pain: rated on a scale vs expressed openly or not at all; felt globally."],"terms":["Culture vs ethnicity vs race","Ethnocentrism","Stereotyping","Somatization","Acculturation and assimilation"],"termDefinitions":{"Culture vs ethnicity vs race":"Same race, different cultures: an African American family in the US for generations and a family just arrived from Nigeria. Culture = shared beliefs, values and practices that guide a group. Ethnicity = a shared heritage and history. Race = a social category (now self-identified, often multiple). Don’t assume beliefs from appearance or a census box; ask.","Ethnocentrism":"“I’m sure our treatment will fix this.” “Her family is too involved.” The universal tendency to believe one’s own culture’s way is the only right and natural way. Leads to judging patients and to care they won’t accept. The fix is cultural awareness of your own norms.","Stereotyping":"“All Asian patients are quiet about pain.” Believing every member of a group is the same. Reduced by many cultural encounters: individuals express their culture in their own ways.","Somatization":"A woman grieving her son describes only stomach pain, back pain and fatigue, and denies sadness. Psychological distress is felt and expressed as physical symptoms, common where mind and body are seen as one or where mental illness carries strong stigma. A common cause of missed or wrong diagnoses. Assess for depression behind physical complaints.","Acculturation and assimilation":"Teenagers adopt American norms quickly; grandparents keep traditional ways; conflict at home. Acculturation = learning a new culture’s norms. Assimilation = fully adopting them. Enculturation = learning your own culture growing up. Some people become bicultural; some have culture shock. Children acculturate faster → intergenerational conflict. Assess the stage of acculturation."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-culture","title":"Culture and mental health","blocks":[{"title":"Three worldviews (broad patterns, not rules)","lines":["Western: the individual is the source of identity; autonomy; mind and body separate; disease has a measurable cause; time is linear; plan for the future.","Eastern: the family is the source of identity; group decisions; mind–body–spirit unity; illness = imbalance (yin–yang, hot–cold); time is circular.","Indigenous: the community and nature; illness = loss of harmony with others, nature or the spirit world; time is the present."]},{"title":"Beliefs that differ from Western care","lines":["Decisions: made by the patient (Western) vs by the family or its head.","Telling the truth: full disclosure vs protecting the patient from bad news to preserve hope.","Sick role: be independent and get moving vs family cares for the sick person, rest helps recovery.","Treatment: prescribed medicines and technology vs balance-restoring remedies and traditional healers.","Pain: rated on a scale vs expressed openly or not at all; felt globally."]},{"title":"Nonverbal communication varies","lines":["Eye contact: respect in the US; rude, challenging or sexual in some cultures.","Personal space: US personal distance about 1.5–4 ft; others stand closer or farther.","Touch: high-touch vs low-touch cultures (touch between men and women may be taboo).","Gestures: a thumbs-up or a nod can mean different things."]},{"title":"Barriers to care","lines":["Language: use a professional medical interpreter, free, at all hours, matched in gender and age if possible. Never family or friends (embarrassing topics get filtered, terms get lost), especially in mental health.","Stigma: in family-centered cultures illness may shame the whole family → hiding it, delayed care.","Misdiagnosis: tools validated mostly on people of European origin; somatization; idioms (“feeling blue” may mean nothing literally).","Cultural concepts of distress that don’t fit DSM categories.","Pharmacogenetics: different drug responses; most drug trials enroll mostly White participants."]},{"title":"Cultural concepts of distress (DSM-5)","lines":["Replaced the old term “culture-bound syndromes.” Three kinds: idioms of distress (ways of expressing suffering, like “I’m a nervous wreck”), cultural explanations (perceived causes) and cultural syndromes.","Ataque de nervios (Latin American): sudden trembling, palpitations, shouting, fainting under unbearable stress.","Susto (Latin American): “soul loss” after a fright; like PTSD.","Hwa-byung (Korean): suppressed anger → epigastric pain, palpitations, aches; like depression.","Neurasthenia (Chinese): fatigue, poor sleep and concentration, denies sadness; like depression.","Ghost sickness (Navajo), Jin possession (Somali), wind illness (Chinese, Vietnamese; treated by coining or cupping, which leave marks that are not abuse).","The DSM-5 Cultural Formulation Interview (16 questions) builds culture into the assessment."]},{"title":"Populations at risk","lines":["Immigrants: lost status and jobs, new systems, changed family roles → anxiety, depression.","Refugees: forced to flee; war, torture, loss → high rates of PTSD and depression.","Poverty: people in poverty are 2–3 times more likely to develop mental illness; the higher rates in minority groups are linked to poverty, discrimination and trauma, not to race itself."]},{"title":"Campinha-Bacote: becoming culturally competent","lines":["Cultural awareness: examine your own culture, biases and assumptions.","Cultural knowledge: learn about other groups’ worldviews, communication and health beliefs.","Cultural encounters: direct interactions with diverse patients (reduces stereotyping).","Cultural skill: doing a culturally sensitive assessment.","Cultural desire: genuine wanting to understand and learn from the patient.","A process of becoming, never finished."]},{"title":"The culturally sensitive assessment (Kleinman’s questions)","lines":["“What do you call this illness?” “When did it start, and why then?”","“What do you think caused it?” “What does it do to you?” “How serious is it, and how long will it last?”","“How have you treated it? How do you think it should be treated?”","Indirect version: “What does your family think is wrong?”","Also ask about religion and spiritual practices, diet, healers and herbs, who counts as family, who gets information and how decisions are made."]},{"title":"Planning: preserve, accommodate, repattern","lines":["Preserve practices that help (strong family support, prayer).","Accommodate or negotiate neutral practices (a traditional healer’s visit, a ceremony alongside treatment).","Repattern harmful ones by teaching and negotiating (an herb that interacts with a medication).","Examples of competence: schedule meals around prayer times, help a Hindu patient choose foods from the menu, arrange a chaplain or healer visit."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-culture:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A nurse is caring for a patient with limited English who is being assessed for suicidal thoughts. Who should interpret?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"The patient’s adult son","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A professional medical interpreter. Family and friends may filter sensitive information and lack medical vocabulary."},{"id":"o1","text":"A bilingual housekeeper","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A professional medical interpreter. Family and friends may filter sensitive information and lack medical vocabulary."},{"id":"o2","text":"A professional medical interpreter","correct":true,"why":"Supported. A professional medical interpreter. Family and friends may filter sensitive information and lack medical vocabulary."},{"id":"o3","text":"The patient’s roommate","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A professional medical interpreter. Family and friends may filter sensitive information and lack medical vocabulary."}]},{"id":"t-culture:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Who should interpret for a patient with limited English in a psychiatric interview?","answer":"A professional medical interpreter, never family or friends.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-culture:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"A grieving patient reports only chest and stomach pain. Term?","answer":"Somatization.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Diagnostic tools and assessment","cards":[{"title":"Mental status exam, in order","text":"Appearance: grooming, dress, hygiene, posture, eye contact. · Behavior and motor activity: agitation, retardation, tremors, tics, cooperation. · Speech: rate, volume, quantity (pressured, poverty of speech)."},{"title":"Psychosocial assessment","text":"Reason for seeking care, in the patient’s own words. · Past psychiatric history, hospitalizations, suicide attempts, medications. · Substance use; trauma and abuse history."},{"title":"Rule out medical and substance causes","text":"Labs: CBC, BMP, thyroid (TSH), liver and kidney function, urine drug screen, B12 and folate, glucose; pregnancy test before many medications. · Thyroid disease, delirium, brain injury or tumor, infection, hypoglycemia, and medications (steroids, beta-blockers) can all mimic psychiatric illness."}],"caption":"The DSM-5, the mental status exam, rating scales, and ruling out medical causes."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Who writes the DSM-5?","cue":"The DSM-5, the mental status exam, rating scales, and ruling out medical causes.","rule":"The American Psychiatric Association.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-dx","ch":"1","chapters":["1","13","14","15","16"],"title":"Diagnostic tools and assessment","notes":["t-dx"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 1 and 13–16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Psychiatric diagnosis rests on criteria, not a lab test. The DSM-5 lists the criteria; the mental status exam is the nurse’s systematic snapshot of how the patient looks, thinks and feels right now; rating scales measure severity and track change. Always ask what medical problem or substance could explain the symptoms.","A “new psychiatric illness” that is really hypothyroidism or delirium will be treated wrongly, and the cause will be missed.","Mental status exam, in order: Appearance: grooming, dress, hygiene, posture, eye contact.; Behavior and motor activity: agitation, retardation, tremors, tics, cooperation.; Speech: rate, volume, quantity (pressured, poverty of speech).; Mood and affect.; Thought process (how they think: logical, tangential, flight of ideas, loose) and thought content (what they think: delusions, obsessions, suicidal or homicidal ideation).; Perception: hallucinations (hearing, seeing, smelling, tasting, feeling things that aren’t there), illusions.; Cognition: orientation, attention, memory, abstract thinking.; Insight and judgment.","Psychosocial assessment: Reason for seeking care, in the patient’s own words.; Past psychiatric history, hospitalizations, suicide attempts, medications.; Substance use; trauma and abuse history.; Family history; relationships, supports, work, finances, housing.; Culture, spirituality, strengths and coping.; Use open-ended questions; a calm, private setting; and tell the patient why you’re asking."],"terms":["DSM-5","Mental status exam (MSE)","Mood vs affect","Insight and judgment"],"termDefinitions":{"DSM-5":"“Major depressive disorder: 5 of 9 symptoms for 2 weeks, causing distress or impairment.” The American Psychiatric Association’s manual of diagnostic criteria, so clinicians everywhere mean the same thing by a diagnosis. Gives the medical diagnosis. Codes match the ICD-10-CM for billing. The nurse writes nursing diagnoses (responses to illness), not DSM diagnoses.","Mental status exam (MSE)":"Appearance, behavior, speech, mood and affect, thought process and content, perception, cognition, insight and judgment. Like a physical assessment for the mind: a structured way to describe what you observe and hear. Document specific observations and quotes (“states ‘the TV is talking to me’”) rather than labels (“psychotic”).","Mood vs affect":"Mood: “I feel empty.” Affect: flat face, monotone voice. Mood is the sustained emotion the patient reports; affect is the emotion the nurse observes. Note whether affect is congruent with mood, and its range: full, constricted, blunted, flat; or labile (rapid shifts).","Insight and judgment":"“There’s nothing wrong with me; my family is the problem.” Insight = awareness of one’s illness and its effects. Judgment = ability to make sound decisions (“What would you do if you found a stamped, addressed envelope?”). Poor insight predicts nonadherence; poor judgment predicts safety risks."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-dx","title":"Diagnostic tools and assessment","blocks":[{"title":"Mental status exam, in order","lines":["Appearance: grooming, dress, hygiene, posture, eye contact.","Behavior and motor activity: agitation, retardation, tremors, tics, cooperation.","Speech: rate, volume, quantity (pressured, poverty of speech).","Mood and affect.","Thought process (how they think: logical, tangential, flight of ideas, loose) and thought content (what they think: delusions, obsessions, suicidal or homicidal ideation).","Perception: hallucinations (hearing, seeing, smelling, tasting, feeling things that aren’t there), illusions.","Cognition: orientation, attention, memory, abstract thinking.","Insight and judgment."]},{"title":"Psychosocial assessment","lines":["Reason for seeking care, in the patient’s own words.","Past psychiatric history, hospitalizations, suicide attempts, medications.","Substance use; trauma and abuse history.","Family history; relationships, supports, work, finances, housing.","Culture, spirituality, strengths and coping.","Use open-ended questions; a calm, private setting; and tell the patient why you’re asking."]},{"title":"Rule out medical and substance causes","lines":["Labs: CBC, BMP, thyroid (TSH), liver and kidney function, urine drug screen, B12 and folate, glucose; pregnancy test before many medications.","Thyroid disease, delirium, brain injury or tumor, infection, hypoglycemia, and medications (steroids, beta-blockers) can all mimic psychiatric illness.","A “new psychiatric illness” that is really hypothyroidism or delirium will be treated wrongly, and the cause will be missed."]},{"title":"Rating scales (know what each measures)","lines":["PHQ-9, Beck Depression Inventory, Hamilton: depression severity.","GAD-7: anxiety. Y-BOCS: OCD severity.","Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS) and SAFE-T: suicide risk.","Altman Self-Rating Mania Scale, Mood Disorder Questionnaire: mania and bipolar screening.","Impact of Event Scale, PCL-5: trauma symptoms.","Mini-Cog / MMSE: cognition. AUDIT, CIWA: alcohol use and withdrawal.","Scales give a baseline number you can compare over time to see whether treatment is working."]},{"title":"Nursing diagnoses in mental health","lines":["Written as the patient’s response: risk for suicide, anxiety, ineffective coping, disturbed sleep, social isolation, risk for violence, self-care deficit, hopelessness.","Outcomes are specific and measurable (“will sleep 6 hours by day 3”)."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-dx:case","kind":"choice","scene":"The nurse documents: “Patient states she feels ‘great’ while crying.” This finding is best described as:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Labile mood","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Mood is what she reports (“great”); affect is what you observe (crying). They don’t match: incongruent."},{"id":"o1","text":"Affect incongruent with stated mood","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Mood is what she reports (“great”); affect is what you observe (crying). They don’t match: incongruent."},{"id":"o2","text":"Flight of ideas","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Mood is what she reports (“great”); affect is what you observe (crying). They don’t match: incongruent."},{"id":"o3","text":"Poor insight","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Mood is what she reports (“great”); affect is what you observe (crying). They don’t match: incongruent."}]},{"id":"t-dx:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Who writes the DSM-5?","answer":"The American Psychiatric Association.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-dx:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Mood or affect: “The patient is smiling while describing her father’s death.”","answer":"Affect (observed), and it is incongruent with the topic.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Classification → individual assessment → nursing outcome","cards":[{"title":"Shared language","text":"A diagnostic category organizes a pattern of symptoms."},{"title":"Individual response","text":"Assess sleep, intake, coping, function and risk."},{"title":"Checkable outcome","text":"Name a patient change, its measure and its time frame."}],"caption":"The diagnosis is a starting point for assessment, not a substitute for it."},"strategy":{"title":"Classification → individual assessment → nursing outcome","task":"Which explanation fits?","cue":"Both patients ask why their nursing plans differ.","rule":"Nursing care is individualized even when diagnostic classification is shared.","trap":"Separate the diagnostic category from the person’s current nursing needs."},"id":"t-e4-diagnostics","ch":"1","chapters":["1"],"title":"Diagnostic tools: criteria and nursing care","notes":["t-dx"],"book":"Chapter 1 diagnostic foundations: focused selection from the existing site-authored DSM and nursing-care material. The broader assessment topic remains available separately.","why":["The DSM is the American Psychiatric Association’s diagnostic classification. Shared criteria help clinicians describe disorders consistently. A category is not a laboratory confirmation or a complete care plan.","Nursing assessment identifies this person’s responses and needs: sleep, coping, daily activity, nutrition, relationships and immediate risks. Two people with the same medical diagnosis can need different nursing plans.","Write an outcome that can be checked. Describe the patient’s action or change, a measurable target and a time frame. Evaluate what actually happened, then revise the plan with the patient."],"terms":["DSM-5"],"termDefinitions":{"DSM-5":"The American Psychiatric Association’s manual of diagnostic criteria, so clinicians everywhere mean the same thing by a diagnosis."},"patient":"Two patients have the same diagnostic label. One cannot sleep; the other sleeps adequately but has stopped eating regular meals.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-dx","title":"Diagnostic tools: criteria and nursing care","blocks":[{"title":"What the DSM contributes","lines":["Diagnostic criteria give clinicians a shared framework for naming mental disorders.","A category does not replace the person’s assessment or an individualized nursing plan."]},{"title":"Nursing diagnoses in mental health","lines":["Written as the patient’s response: risk for suicide, anxiety, ineffective coping, disturbed sleep, social isolation, risk for violence, self-care deficit, hopelessness.","Outcomes are specific and measurable (“will sleep 6 hours by day 3”)."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"Both patients ask why their nursing plans differ.","task":"Which explanation fits?","hint":"Separate the diagnostic category from the person’s current nursing needs.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"One diagnosis requires an identical nursing plan for everyone.","why":"A shared diagnosis does not erase different needs, risks and goals.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Nursing plans depend only on which nurse is assigned.","why":"The plan should follow assessed needs and collaborative goals, not staff preference.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Your plans address the different responses and priorities each of you described.","why":"Nursing care is individualized even when diagnostic classification is shared.","correct":true}],"id":"t-e4-diagnostics:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient identifies poor sleep as a priority.","task":"Which draft outcome is easiest to evaluate?","hint":"Distinguish a patient result from a nursing action.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"The patient will feel better soon.","why":"Neither the change nor the time frame is clear enough to evaluate.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"The patient will report at least six hours of sleep by the third morning.","why":"The patient response, amount and time frame are specified; the target still needs to be appropriate and agreed.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"The nurse will explain sleep hygiene.","why":"This describes an intervention, not the patient’s outcome.","correct":false}],"id":"t-e4-diagnostics:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Choose the support that fits the developmental task","cards":[{"title":"School age · industry","text":"Build competence through an achievable task."},{"title":"Adolescence · identity","text":"Allow exploration of values, roles and choices."},{"title":"Later life · integrity","text":"Invite life review and the person’s sense of meaning."}],"caption":"Age suggests a starting question; assessment shows what support is useful."},"strategy":{"title":"Choose the support that fits the developmental task","task":"Which response supports industry?","cue":"The child wants to help organize the bedside art materials.","rule":"A successful task builds a sense of competence without making unrealistic promises.","trap":"Industry is about developing competence."},"id":"t-life","ch":"2","chapters":["2"],"title":"Development across the lifespan","notes":["t-life"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 2; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Developmental theories give you questions to explore; they do not diagnose a person from age alone. Ask about abilities, relationships, experience and what the person is trying to accomplish.","Erikson emphasizes a psychosocial task: trust, autonomy, initiative, industry, identity, intimacy, generativity or integrity. Support a meaningful opportunity to practise the task rather than shaming a difficulty.","Piaget describes forms of thinking, while Kohlberg and Gilligan address moral reasoning from different perspectives. Adapt explanations to demonstrated understanding. The full chapter reference keeps all stages and the additional theories together."],"terms":["psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"A school-age child receiving treatment says, “Everyone else can do their work. I’m bad at everything.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-life","title":"Development across the lifespan","blocks":[{"title":"Erikson’s eight stages","lines":["Infancy (0–1½): trust vs mistrust. Success: trust that needs will be met. Failure: suspicion, withdrawal.","Early childhood (1½–3): autonomy vs shame and doubt. Self-control and willpower vs self-doubt.","Late childhood (3–6): initiative vs guilt. Purpose, starting activities vs fear of punishment, guilt.","School age (6–12): industry vs inferiority. Competence, “I can do things” vs feeling inadequate.","Adolescence (12–20): identity vs role confusion. A clear sense of self vs confusion about who they are.","Early adulthood (20–35): intimacy vs isolation. Close, committed relationships vs loneliness.","Middle adulthood (35–65): generativity vs self-absorption (stagnation). Guiding the next generation and contributing vs self-centeredness.","Later years (65+): integrity vs despair. Accepting one’s life with meaning vs regret, fear of death.","A 70-year-old reviewing life with regret, a teen with no sense of self, a toddler shamed for accidents: each points to a stage and to what the nurse can support."]},{"title":"Piaget: how thinking develops","lines":["Sensorimotor (0–2): learns through senses and movement; object permanence.","Preoperational (2–7): language and symbols, egocentric, magical thinking.","Concrete operational (7–11): logical about concrete things; conservation; rules.","Formal operational (11+): abstract and hypothetical thinking.","Match teaching to the stage: concrete, hands-on explanations for a school-age child; and remember that severe anxiety or psychosis can push adults back to concrete thinking."]},{"title":"Moral development: Kohlberg and Gilligan","lines":["Kohlberg: preconventional (avoid punishment, get rewards) → conventional (be “good,” obey laws and rules) → postconventional (social contract, universal principles of justice).","Gilligan: an ethic of care and relationships, from caring for self → caring for others (self-sacrifice) → caring for both self and others."]},{"title":"Freud’s stages (for context)","lines":["Oral (0–1½) → anal (1½–3) → phallic (3–6) → latency (6–12) → genital (12+).","Unresolved conflicts at a stage can show up later as fixations (e.g., oral: smoking, overeating, dependency)."]},{"title":"Mental health at the ends of life","lines":["Children show distress through behavior: irritability, regression, school problems, physical complaints.","Older adults: depression is underrecognized (blamed on aging), shows as somatic complaints or memory problems; older men have the highest suicide rate; start medications low and slow."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"The child wants to help organize the bedside art materials.","task":"Which response supports industry?","hint":"Industry is about developing competence.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Choose a manageable sorting job and recognize the child’s specific effort.","why":"A successful task builds a sense of competence without making unrealistic promises.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Complete every task for the child to prevent frustration.","why":"Removing all opportunities to practise can reinforce the feeling of being unable.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Tell the child that worrying about ability is childish.","why":"Shaming the concern does not help the developmental task.","correct":false}],"id":"t-life:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A child understands a demonstration with real objects but struggles with an abstract explanation.","task":"Which teaching change best fits the observed thinking?","hint":"Match teaching to thinking ability, not just chronological age.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Use a longer abstract lecture.","why":"More of the same mismatch is unlikely to clarify the idea.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Stop explaining because the child cannot learn.","why":"A difficulty with abstraction does not mean an inability to learn.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Demonstrate with concrete objects and ask the child to show it back.","why":"Concrete examples match demonstrated understanding and allow a check of learning.","correct":true}],"id":"t-life:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"History and treatment settings","cards":[{"title":"Milestones","text":"1950s: chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, makes community living possible. · 1963: Community Mental Health Centers Act → deinstitutionalization. · Unintended results: too few community services, so many people with serious mental illness ended up homeless or incarcerated."},{"title":"Outpatient and community settings","text":"Primary care: where most people first get mental health care. · Community mental health centers: therapy, medication management, case management. · Partial hospitalization (PHP): treatment most of the day, home at night; intensive outpatient (IOP): several hours a few days a week."},{"title":"Inpatient care","text":"For crisis stabilization when outpatient care is not safe: danger to self or others, or gravely disabled. · Short stays focused on safety, stabilization, medication and discharge planning from day one. · State hospitals for longer-term or forensic care."}],"caption":"Deinstitutionalization, the continuum of care, the milieu, and the team."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which law started deinstitutionalization?","cue":"Deinstitutionalization, the continuum of care, the milieu, and the team.","rule":"The Community Mental Health Centers Act of 1963.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-settings","ch":"4","chapters":["4","6"],"title":"History and treatment settings","notes":["t-settings"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 4 and 6; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Care moved from large state hospitals to the community after the first antipsychotics (1950s) and the Community Mental Health Centers Act (1963). Today most people are treated as outpatients; inpatient stays are short and reserved for people who are dangerous to themselves or others or can’t care for themselves. Patients move along a continuum of care to the least restrictive setting that keeps them safe.","Structure lowers anxiety and gives people a safe place to try new behavior.","Milestones: 1950s: chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, makes community living possible.; 1963: Community Mental Health Centers Act → deinstitutionalization.; Unintended results: too few community services, so many people with serious mental illness ended up homeless or incarcerated.; 2008 Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act (in effect 2010) and the Affordable Care Act: insurance must cover mental health and substance use care like physical care.","Outpatient and community settings: Primary care: where most people first get mental health care.; Community mental health centers: therapy, medication management, case management.; Partial hospitalization (PHP): treatment most of the day, home at night; intensive outpatient (IOP): several hours a few days a week.; Assertive community treatment (ACT): a mobile team that brings care to people with serious mental illness wherever they are, on call 24 hours a day.; Psychiatric home care, telehealth, crisis lines (988), peer support, clubhouses, supported housing and employment."],"terms":["psychotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-settings","title":"History and treatment settings","blocks":[{"title":"Milestones","lines":["1950s: chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, makes community living possible.","1963: Community Mental Health Centers Act → deinstitutionalization.","Unintended results: too few community services, so many people with serious mental illness ended up homeless or incarcerated.","2008 Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act (in effect 2010) and the Affordable Care Act: insurance must cover mental health and substance use care like physical care."]},{"title":"Outpatient and community settings","lines":["Primary care: where most people first get mental health care.","Community mental health centers: therapy, medication management, case management.","Partial hospitalization (PHP): treatment most of the day, home at night; intensive outpatient (IOP): several hours a few days a week.","Assertive community treatment (ACT): a mobile team that brings care to people with serious mental illness wherever they are, on call 24 hours a day.","Psychiatric home care, telehealth, crisis lines (988), peer support, clubhouses, supported housing and employment."]},{"title":"Inpatient care","lines":["For crisis stabilization when outpatient care is not safe: danger to self or others, or gravely disabled.","Short stays focused on safety, stabilization, medication and discharge planning from day one.","State hospitals for longer-term or forensic care."]},{"title":"The therapeutic milieu","lines":["The unit itself is part of treatment: a safe, structured environment with predictable routines, clear rules, community meetings and groups.","Patients practice social and coping skills with staff and peers.","The nurse manages the milieu: safety checks, de-escalation, group facilitation, limit setting.","Structure lowers anxiety and gives people a safe place to try new behavior."]},{"title":"The treatment team","lines":["Psychiatric RN: 24-hour care, assessment, the milieu, medication, teaching, coordination.","PMH-APRN and psychiatrist: diagnosis, prescribing, psychotherapy.","Psychologist: testing and therapy. Social worker: family, housing, benefits, discharge.","Occupational and recreational therapists, pharmacist, peer support specialist, case manager."]},{"title":"Safety on behavioral health units","lines":["Screen every patient for suicide risk (a National Patient Safety Goal).","Ligature-resistant rooms, contraband searches, observation levels.","De-escalation before restraint; staff trained in crisis management."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-settings:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which law started deinstitutionalization?","answer":"The Community Mental Health Centers Act of 1963.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-settings:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What is ACT?","answer":"Assertive community treatment: a mobile, 24/7 team that brings care to people with serious mental illness in the community.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Rights + actual risk + lawful action","cards":[{"title":"Assess","text":"What is the person requesting, and is there an immediate risk?"},{"title":"Choose","text":"Use a lawful, effective response with the least restriction."},{"title":"Reassess","text":"Monitor safety, explain the process and document the reason."}],"caption":"A diagnosis or staff disagreement does not supply legal authority by itself."},"strategy":{"title":"Rights + actual risk + lawful action","task":"Which action best respects the discharge request?","cue":"No immediate dangerous behavior has been described.","rule":"This preserves the request while allowing assessment and the legally required process.","trap":"Distinguish a right to request discharge from the specific legal process for release."},"id":"t-legal","ch":"6","chapters":["6"],"title":"Legal and ethical care","notes":["t-legal"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 6; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Begin with rights, current risk and the least restrictive effective response. Admission status does not by itself remove every right. Consent, capacity, emergency authority and discharge processes depend on the applicable law and policy.","Restraint or seclusion is a safety intervention with strict requirements, never a shortcut for convenience, punishment or ordinary disagreement. Assess alternatives, obtain required authorization, monitor and end the restriction as soon as it is no longer needed.","For U.S. hospital restraint or seclusion for violent or self-destructive behavior, federal order-renewal limits distinguish adults and children. These limits are not permission to continue until the clock expires. Nonviolent restraint rules and stricter state requirements must be checked separately. Use the reference for the chapter’s legal terms and ethics comparisons."],"terms":["Least restrictive alternative","Competency vs capacity","Duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff)"],"termDefinitions":{"Least restrictive alternative":"Treating depression as an outpatient rather than in the hospital; verbal de-escalation before medication, and medication before restraint. Patients have a right to treatment that limits their freedom only as much as safety requires. Always try and document less-restrictive measures first, unless the emergency is too acute.","Competency vs capacity":"A judge declares a person incompetent and appoints a guardian; a psychiatrist judges that a patient can’t weigh the risks of ECT today. Competency is a legal status decided by a court; capacity is a clinical judgment about a specific decision, and it can change hour to hour. Patients are presumed competent. Involuntary admission does not by itself make someone incompetent; they keep the right to consent to or refuse treatment.","Duty to warn and protect (Tarasoff)":"A patient says he will kill his ex-girlfriend when he gets out. The Tarasoff rulings (California, 1974 and 1976): when a patient makes a serious threat against an identifiable person, the duty to protect that person outweighs confidentiality. Report the threat to the treatment team and provider immediately; the clinician warns the victim or police per state law."},"patient":"An adult voluntary patient asks to leave. The nurse needs to assess the request, the patient’s condition and the applicable discharge process.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-legal","title":"Legal and ethical care","blocks":[{"title":"Types of admission","lines":["Voluntary: the patient applies in writing; may request discharge, though staff may reevaluate and start an involuntary hold if the patient is dangerous.","Involuntary commitment: court-ordered, without consent, when the person has a mental illness and is a danger to self or others, gravely disabled (can’t meet basic needs for food, clothing, shelter), or needs treatment and the illness prevents them from seeking it.","Emergency (temporary) commitment: a short hold, usually 24–96 hours (often 72), for evaluation, until a court hearing.","Writ of habeas corpus: the patient’s legal request for release when they believe they are held unlawfully."]},{"title":"Discharge types","lines":["Unconditional release: the legal relationship ends.","Conditional release: discharged with required outpatient treatment.","Against medical advice (AMA): a voluntary patient leaves when staff disagree but there are no grounds to commit.","Assisted outpatient treatment: court-ordered outpatient care for people with repeated relapses from nonadherence."]},{"title":"Patients’ rights","lines":["Right to treatment in the least restrictive setting, with dignity and privacy.","Right to refuse treatment, including medication, even if involuntarily committed (except in an emergency, or after a court finds them incompetent).","Right to informed consent (the prescriber obtains it; the nurse educates and witnesses).","Right to communicate (phone, mail, visitors), to an attorney, to keep personal belongings, to manage their money unless found incompetent.","Right to be free from unnecessary restraint and seclusion.","Psychiatric advance directives: written while well, stating treatment preferences for a future crisis."]},{"title":"Restraint and seclusion","lines":["A last resort for imminent danger to self or others after less-restrictive measures fail; never for punishment or staff convenience.","Needs a provider order; never PRN or standing. In an emergency the nurse may initiate and obtain the order promptly.","A face-to-face evaluation by a provider or trained RN within 1 hour.","Order limits: adults 4 hours, ages 9–17: 2 hours, under 9: 1 hour; renewed up to 24 hours, then a new order.","Continuous observation; check circulation, skin, vital signs, fluids, food and toileting every 15–30 minutes per policy; release as soon as safe.","Document the behavior, the alternatives tried, times in and out, and assessments.","Chemical restraint = medication used to control behavior rather than to treat the condition. Side rails used to keep a patient in bed are a restraint too.","Seclusion = alone in a room the patient can’t leave. Time-out is voluntary: the patient can leave.","Restraint and seclusion are dangerous (asphyxia, injury, trauma), so the law treats them as a deprivation of liberty that needs tight time limits and monitoring."]},{"title":"Confidentiality and HIPAA","lines":["Share information only with people directly involved in care, or with the patient’s written consent. Only the patient can waive privilege.","Confirming that someone is a patient is a breach unless they’ve consented.","No patient talk in elevators, cafeterias or on social media; no identifiers in school paperwork.","Confidentiality continues after death.","Exceptions: duty to warn and protect; mandatory reporting of child or elder abuse; a court order."]},{"title":"Torts and liability","lines":["Assault: threatening to touch (“If you don’t take this I’ll hold you down”). Battery: touching without consent (forcing medication outside an emergency).","False imprisonment: confining without legal grounds (locking in a voluntary patient who asks to leave without a hold, or unnecessary restraint).","Defamation: slander (spoken) and libel (written).","Negligence / malpractice: duty + breach + harm caused by the breach (e.g., failing to follow suicide precautions)."]},{"title":"Ethical principles","lines":["Autonomy: respecting the patient’s right to decide (refusing medication).","Beneficence: doing good (spending time calming an anxious patient).","Nonmaleficence: doing no harm (protecting confidential information).","Justice: fair, equal care (equal attention to a patient after a suicide attempt).","Fidelity: keeping promises, loyalty (showing up when you said you would).","Veracity: telling the truth (honest teaching about side effects)."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"42 CFR 482.13: patient rights, restraint and seclusion","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-G/part-482/subpart-B/section-482.13"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"No immediate dangerous behavior has been described.","task":"Which action best respects the discharge request?","hint":"Distinguish a right to request discharge from the specific legal process for release.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Lock the door because staff would prefer another day of treatment.","why":"Staff preference alone is not authority to detain the patient.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Acknowledge the request, assess current risk and promptly follow the required provider and discharge process.","why":"This preserves the request while allowing assessment and the legally required process.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"Promise immediate discharge without checking any requirements.","why":"The nurse should not promise a process or outcome without the required assessment and review.","correct":false}],"id":"t-legal:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A restraint order remains active, but the patient now meets the criteria for safe release.","task":"What governs the next action?","hint":"Ask whether restriction is still necessary, not only whether the order has expired.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Continue solely because unused hours remain.","why":"The order limit is a maximum, not a required duration.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Use restraint to discourage another disagreement.","why":"Punishment and coercion are not acceptable reasons for restraint.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Follow the release criteria and discontinue at the earliest safe time, with reassessment and documentation.","why":"Restriction should end when it is no longer needed for immediate safety; required follow-up still applies.","correct":true}],"id":"t-legal:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"The lens changes the question you ask","cards":[{"title":"Cognitive","text":"What interpretation is driving this response?"},{"title":"Behavioral","text":"What learned pattern or consequence maintains it?"},{"title":"Interpersonal","text":"What is happening in the relationship?"}],"caption":"The same situation can be explored through more than one useful lens."},"strategy":{"title":"The lens changes the question you ask","task":"Which approach is being used most directly?","cue":"The nurse helps the patient examine the evidence for “everyone will reject me.”","rule":"The nurse is helping connect a thought with avoidance and test whether the conclusion fits the evidence.","trap":"Identify what the nurse is doing, not merely the patient’s diagnosis."},"id":"t-theories","ch":"2","chapters":["2"],"title":"Theories and therapies","notes":["t-theories"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 2; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["A theory is a lens for understanding a problem. Psychodynamic approaches explore unconscious patterns; interpersonal approaches examine relationships; behavioral approaches focus on learning; cognitive approaches examine interpretations.","Match the intervention to the lens. Practising a skill or reinforcing a behavior differs from examining the thought that precedes it. DBT combines acceptance and change with skills such as distress tolerance and emotion regulation.","Physiological needs and immediate safety can change what is useful now. A patient who is exhausted or medically unstable needs that problem addressed before a demanding insight exercise. Theories support assessment; they do not replace it."],"terms":["disulfiram"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"A patient avoids a common room because one awkward conversation led to the thought, “Everyone will reject me.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-theories","title":"Theories and therapies","blocks":[{"title":"Freud (psychoanalytic)","lines":["Levels of awareness: conscious, preconscious (can be recalled), unconscious (the largest part, out of awareness, but drives behavior).","Personality: id (instinct, pleasure, “I want it now”), ego (reality, mediates), superego (conscience, morals).","The ego uses defense mechanisms to manage anxiety.","Therapy: psychodynamic (free association, dream analysis, exploring transference)."]},{"title":"Interpersonal: Sullivan and Peplau","lines":["Sullivan: personality forms through relationships; anxiety arises from them; people use “security operations” to reduce it.","Peplau: the nurse–patient relationship is the therapeutic tool. Described the phases (orientation, working, termination), the roles of the nurse, and the levels of anxiety.","Interpersonal therapy (IPT): short-term therapy for depression focused on grief, role disputes, role transitions and social isolation."]},{"title":"Maslow (humanistic)","lines":["Needs in order: physiological → safety → love and belonging → esteem → self-actualization.","Lower needs must be met before higher ones can be addressed.","Rogers: client-centered therapy with empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard.","This is how you prioritize: a manic patient who hasn’t eaten or slept gets food, fluids and rest before insight-oriented groups."]},{"title":"Behavioral theories (learning)","lines":["Pavlov: classical conditioning (a neutral cue becomes linked with a response).","Skinner: operant conditioning: behavior followed by reinforcement increases; by punishment decreases. Positive = adding, negative = removing.","Therapies: modeling (learning by watching), exposure (systematic desensitization = graded, with relaxation; flooding = all at once), aversion therapy (pairing a behavior with something unpleasant, e.g., disulfiram), biofeedback, token economies."]},{"title":"Cognitive theories","lines":["Beck: thoughts drive feelings and behavior; depression comes from automatic negative thoughts and cognitive distortions.","Ellis: rational emotive behavior therapy (REBT), disputing irrational beliefs (A-B-C: activating event, belief, consequence).","CBT: identify distorted thoughts → test the evidence → replace them; plus behavioral experiments and homework. Strong evidence for depression and anxiety.","DBT (dialectical behavior therapy): CBT plus mindfulness; skills in distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness; for self-harm and borderline personality disorder."]},{"title":"Cognitive distortions to recognize","lines":["All-or-nothing: “If I’m not perfect, I’m a failure.”","Overgeneralization: “I always mess up.”","Catastrophizing: “If I fail this exam my life is over.”","Personalization: “It rained at the picnic because of me.”","Mind reading: “She thinks I’m stupid.”","Discounting the positive, emotional reasoning (“I feel it, so it’s true”), “should” statements."]},{"title":"Biological theory and therapies","lines":["Mental illness involves neurotransmitters (serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine, GABA, glutamate), brain structure, genetics and hormones.","Therapies: psychopharmacology and brain stimulation: ECT (induced seizure under anesthesia), rTMS (magnetic pulses, awake, no seizure), vagus nerve stimulation, deep brain stimulation; light therapy."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"The nurse helps the patient examine the evidence for “everyone will reject me.”","task":"Which approach is being used most directly?","hint":"Identify what the nurse is doing, not merely the patient’s diagnosis.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Behavioral reinforcement of a completed task.","why":"Reinforcement changes consequences; this action examines an interpretation.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Cognitive examination of an automatic thought.","why":"The nurse is helping connect a thought with avoidance and test whether the conclusion fits the evidence.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"An interpretation of an unconscious childhood conflict.","why":"The given intervention does not explore an unconscious conflict.","correct":false}],"id":"t-theories:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Before a long therapy group, another patient is dizzy, has eaten little and needs physical assessment.","task":"What changes the immediate plan?","hint":"A useful therapy still has to fit the patient’s condition now.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Meeting physiological needs and assessing stability come before demanding group work.","why":"The patient’s present ability to participate and immediate physical needs set the priority.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"The planned group always takes priority over the current findings.","why":"A schedule does not override an emerging physical concern.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"A theory name is enough to decide that the dizziness is emotional.","why":"The physical finding still requires assessment.","correct":false}],"id":"t-theories:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Receptor action → body effect → nursing response","cards":[{"title":"Signal","text":"What receptor or pathway does this drug affect?"},{"title":"Effect","text":"What expected effect or adverse effect follows?"},{"title":"Response","text":"Assess severity, protect safety and follow the drug-specific plan."}],"caption":"Use the receptor connection to predict what you must assess."},"strategy":{"title":"Receptor action → body effect → nursing response","task":"Which connection best guides the next assessment?","cue":"The patient feels lightheaded after rising from bed.","rule":"This connects a plausible mechanism with assessment and a useful safety action.","trap":"Separate the receptor effects, then act on the patient’s actual symptoms."},"id":"t-neuro","ch":"3","chapters":["3"],"title":"The brain and psych meds","notes":["t-neuro"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 3; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Neurons communicate through chemical signals acting at receptors. Drugs can change signaling in several ways. An agonist activates a receptor; an antagonist blocks its activation. A partial agonist is not the same as a pure blocker.","A receptor’s location helps explain an effect. Blocking histamine H1 can cause sedation; muscarinic blockade can produce dry mouth and constipation; alpha-1 blockade can contribute to orthostatic symptoms. Actual risks vary by drug and dose.","Connect the mechanism to assessment and teaching, then use the specific medication’s precautions. Transmitter charts are simplified learning models, not tests for a diagnosis or proof that one chemical alone causes an illness. The chapter reference retains the brain areas, drug classes, monitoring and interactions."],"terms":["Agonist vs antagonist","Reuptake","Pharmacokinetics vs pharmacodynamics","Narrow therapeutic index","Pharmacogenetics (CYP450)","clozapine","olanzapine","aripiprazole","sertraline","citalopram","venlafaxine","bupropion","lithium","valproate","carbamazepine","lamotrigine","methylphenidate","atomoxetine","donepezil","rivastigmine","memantine","diazepam","lorazepam","clonazepam","alprazolam","haloperidol","fluoxetine","paroxetine","duloxetine","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Agonist vs antagonist":"A benzodiazepine boosts GABA; an antipsychotic blocks dopamine D2 receptors. An agonist binds and stimulates a receptor (a partial agonist stimulates it less). An antagonist binds and blocks it so the natural neurotransmitter can’t act. Tells you the direction of the effect, and so the side effects: blocking a receptor removes everything that receptor normally does.","Reuptake":"SSRIs, SNRIs, TCAs and bupropion are all “reuptake inhibitors.” After signaling, monoamines (serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine) are pulled back into the sending neuron, where monoamine oxidase (MAO) breaks them down. Blocking reuptake, or blocking MAO, leaves more neurotransmitter in the synapse. Why stacking two serotonin-raising drugs (e.g., an SSRI + an MAOI) causes serotonin syndrome.","Pharmacokinetics vs pharmacodynamics":"Lithium rises when the kidneys slow down; an SSRI causes nausea through 5-HT3 receptors. Pharmacokinetics = what the body does to the drug: Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion (ADME). Pharmacodynamics = what the drug does to the body: its action and responses. Kidney or liver disease, age and drug interactions change kinetics, so the level and the toxicity risk change.","Narrow therapeutic index":"Lithium: a therapeutic level and a toxic level are close together. The therapeutic index is the ratio of the lethal (or toxic) dose to the effective dose. Narrow = little room for error. Needs blood level monitoring; anything that changes excretion (dehydration, NSAIDs, thiazides, ACE inhibitors) can push it into toxicity.","Pharmacogenetics (CYP450)":"Two patients on the same dose: one has heavy side effects, the other no benefit. Genetic variations in liver cytochrome P450 enzymes make people poor metabolizers (high levels, more side effects) or rapid metabolizers (low levels, less effect). Consider it when a drug doesn’t work as expected. HLA-B1502 (more common in people of Asian ancestry) → test before carbamazepine (SJS/TEN risk)."},"patient":"After starting a prescribed psychiatric medicine, a patient reports dry mouth and dizziness when standing.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-neuro","title":"The brain and psych meds","blocks":[{"title":"Neurotransmitters and what they do","lines":["Dopamine: movement, reward, thinking. Too much → psychosis, mania. Too little → Parkinson disease, depression.","Norepinephrine: attention, arousal, fight-or-flight. Low → depression. High → mania, anxiety.","Serotonin (5-HT): mood, sleep, appetite, pain, sex, aggression. Low → depression, anxiety.","GABA: the main inhibitory (calming) transmitter. Low → anxiety, mania, seizures.","Glutamate: the main excitatory transmitter; learning and memory. Too much is neurotoxic (Alzheimer disease).","Acetylcholine: memory and learning; the parasympathetic “rest and digest” system. Low → Alzheimer disease.","Histamine: alertness. Blocking it → sedation and weight gain.","Orexin: wakefulness (low in narcolepsy)."]},{"title":"Brain areas","lines":["Brainstem: heart rate, breathing, sleep; the source of the mood pathways.","Limbic system (amygdala = fear and emotion; hippocampus = memory): emotion, drives, memory. The amygdala is overactive in anxiety; the hippocampus shrinks in Alzheimer disease and trauma.","Hypothalamus: hunger, thirst, sex, sleep–wake cycle, hormones (via the pituitary).","Cerebellum: coordination and balance of voluntary movement (why sedatives cause ataxia).","Cerebrum/cortex: thinking, language, voluntary movement. Frontal = reasoning, planning (less active in schizophrenia and depression); parietal = touch; temporal = hearing; occipital = vision.","Basal ganglia: movement. Movement and emotion use the same transmitters, so mood drugs can cause movement side effects."]},{"title":"The HPA axis and stress","lines":["Hypothalamus → CRH → pituitary → ACTH → adrenal cortex → cortisol.","High cortisol in major depression (and suppressed immunity). Low cortisol in PTSD.","Thyroid: hyperthyroidism looks like anxiety; hypothyroidism looks like depression."]},{"title":"Receptor blockade → side effect (the key)","lines":["H1 (histamine) blocked → sedation, weight gain.","M1 (muscarinic, acetylcholine) blocked → anticholinergic: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, tachycardia; confusion in older adults.","α1 (norepinephrine on blood vessels) blocked → vasodilation → orthostatic hypotension, dizziness (and priapism with trazodone).","D2 blocked in the nigrostriatal pathway → EPS (movement); in the tuberoinfundibular pathway → high prolactin (galactorrhea, amenorrhea, gynecomastia, sexual dysfunction); in the mesolimbic pathway → the wanted antipsychotic effect.","5-HT stimulated (SSRIs) → nausea and GI upset (5-HT3), sexual dysfunction (5-HT2).","TCAs and first-generation antipsychotics block H1, M1 and α1 together, which is why both cause sleepiness, dry mouth and dizziness on standing."]},{"title":"Antianxiety and sleep drugs","lines":["Benzodiazepines (lorazepam, alprazolam, clonazepam, diazepam): boost GABA-A; fast; anxiety, sleep, seizures, alcohol withdrawal. Sedation, ataxia, anterograde amnesia; deadly with alcohol or opioids; dependence → taper; falls and delirium in older adults.","Buspirone: 5-HT1A partial agonist; GAD; not controlled, daily (not PRN), weeks to work.","Z-drugs (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone): sleep only; complex sleep behaviors (sleep-driving, sleep-eating); lower zolpidem dose for women.","Ramelteon: melatonin agonist, not controlled. Low-dose doxepin: H1 block for staying asleep. Suvorexant: orexin blocker (not in narcolepsy).","Antidepressants are first line for long-term anxiety, but take 4–8 weeks."]},{"title":"Antidepressants by class","lines":["SSRIs: first line. Fluoxetine most activating, very long half-life; sertraline most GI upset; paroxetine most anticholinergic; fluvoxamine most sedating; citalopram prolongs the QT.","SNRIs (venlafaxine, duloxetine…): sweating, ↑ BP and HR; help nerve pain; venlafaxine has the worst discontinuation syndrome.","Bupropion (NDRI): no sexual dysfunction; seizure risk: avoid in seizure disorders, eating disorders, or abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal.","Mirtazapine: sedating, appetite and weight gain (good for insomnia with weight loss).","Trazodone: sedating (used for sleep), α1 block → orthostasis, priapism.","TCAs: H1/M1/α1 side effects; sodium-channel block on the heart → fatal in overdose.","MAOIs: last line; tyramine and drug interactions (SSRIs, buspirone → serotonin syndrome; stimulants, pseudoephedrine → hypertensive crisis; dextromethorphan).","Esketamine (NMDA antagonist nasal spray; BP and dissociation, watch 2 h) and brexanolone (postpartum depression; 60-h infusion; continuous pulse oximetry).","Why weeks to work: receptors down-regulate slowly, and new synaptic connections grow."]},{"title":"Mood stabilizers","lines":["Lithium: the gold standard; may reduce suicide. Narrow index. Raised by thiazides, ACE inhibitors, NSAIDs, dehydration; lowered by caffeine. Side effects: tremor, polyuria and thirst, hypothyroidism, weight gain, acne.","Valproate: mania, mixed and rapid cycling. LFTs, CBC (platelets), levels; pancreatitis, hyperammonemia; teratogenic.","Carbamazepine: second line; LFTs, CBC, sodium (hyponatremia), ECG; HLA-B1502 test; induces CYP so it lowers other drugs (including birth control pills).","Lamotrigine: maintenance and bipolar depression (not acute mania); slow titration; any rash → report (SJS). Valproate doubles lamotrigine levels, so the lamotrigine dose is halved."]},{"title":"Antipsychotics (more on Exam 5)","lines":["First generation (haloperidol, chlorpromazine): D2 blockers; treat positive symptoms; high EPS: acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism (days to months) and tardive dyskinesia (months to years, may be permanent; screen with the AIMS).","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome: rigidity, high fever, altered mental status, unstable BP and HR: an emergency.","Second generation (block D2 + 5-HT2A): less EPS; treat positive and negative symptoms; metabolic syndrome (weight, glucose, lipids). Highest risk with clozapine and olanzapine.","Clozapine: for treatment-resistant schizophrenia; lowers suicide; neutropenia → ANC weekly for 6 months, then every 2 weeks, then monthly; lowers the seizure threshold; smoking lowers its level.","Ziprasidone and lurasidone must be taken with food; ziprasidone and iloperidone prolong the QT; aripiprazole is a partial agonist (low prolactin, rare compulsive behaviors)."]},{"title":"ADHD and Alzheimer drugs","lines":["Stimulants (methylphenidate, amphetamines): raise dopamine and norepinephrine. Insomnia (give early in the day), ↑ BP and HR, appetite loss, growth suppression; controlled.","Nonstimulants: atomoxetine (watch BP, liver); guanfacine and clonidine (hypotension, bradycardia; don’t stop abruptly: rebound hypertension).","Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine): raise acetylcholine; nausea, diarrhea, bradycardia and syncope.","Memantine: NMDA antagonist that blocks excess glutamate; moderate to severe disease."]},{"title":"Herbals and brain imaging","lines":["Herbals aren’t FDA regulated, may not contain what the label says, and interact (e.g., St. John’s wort + SSRIs → serotonin syndrome). Ask about them at every medication history.","Structural imaging (CT, MRI) shows anatomy: enlarged ventricles in schizophrenia. Functional imaging (PET, SPECT, fMRI) shows activity: less prefrontal activity in depression; less frontal glucose use in schizophrenia. EEG records electrical activity.","No routine test measures brain neurotransmitters; diagnosis is still based on symptoms."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"DailyMed: haloperidol labeling, NMS recognition and management","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b5a6c54c-fb70-4132-90ae-7d75b5e9c9de"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"The patient feels lightheaded after rising from bed.","task":"Which connection best guides the next assessment?","hint":"Separate the receptor effects, then act on the patient’s actual symptoms.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Dry mouth proves that every symptom has the same receptor cause.","why":"A drug can affect multiple receptors; one effect does not identify every other mechanism.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"A psychiatric medication makes physical assessment unnecessary.","why":"Medication-related symptoms still require physical assessment.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Alpha-1 blockade can contribute to orthostasis; assess position-related symptoms and protect against falls.","why":"This connects a plausible mechanism with assessment and a useful safety action.","correct":true}],"id":"t-neuro:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A classmate says every psychiatric illness can be diagnosed by finding one low transmitter.","task":"Which correction is accurate?","hint":"Keep a useful mechanism separate from a diagnostic claim.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Transmitter models help explain drug effects, but disorders and diagnosis are more complex.","why":"A teaching model does not establish a single cause or replace clinical assessment.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"A drug helping proves that the illness was caused by a deficiency of that drug’s target.","why":"Response to a treatment does not establish a single underlying cause.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Receptors have no relationship to adverse effects.","why":"Receptor effects are useful for anticipating adverse effects, even though they do not prove a diagnosis.","correct":false}],"id":"t-neuro:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"The therapeutic relationship","cards":[{"title":"Peplau’s phases","text":"Preorientation: before meeting. Gather data, and do a self-assessment of your own feelings and biases. · Orientation: introductions; set the contract: purpose, time, place, how often, confidentiality and its limits, and when the relationship ends. Build trust; the patient may test you (lateness, limit testing). · Working: explore thoughts, feelings and stressors; problem-solve; practice new coping; transference and resistance often appear."},{"title":"Factors that help the relationship","text":"Consistency and reliability (be on time, keep promises). · Genuineness, empathy, positive regard (accepting the person even when not accepting the behavior). · Active listening, attention to nonverbal cues, cultural humility."},{"title":"Boundary rules for students and new nurses","text":"Limited self-disclosure: only if it helps the patient, briefly, then return the focus to them. · No gifts (or per policy), no social media contact, no personal phone numbers, no dating, no meeting after discharge. · Don’t promise secrets: “I can’t keep information that affects your safety from the team.”"}],"caption":"Peplau’s phases, boundaries, transference and countertransference."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"In which phase is the termination date set?","cue":"Peplau’s phases, boundaries, transference and countertransference.","rule":"The orientation phase (as part of the contract).","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-relationship","ch":"8","chapters":["8"],"title":"The therapeutic relationship","notes":["t-relationship"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 8; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["A therapeutic relationship exists for the patient’s needs, has a goal and an end, and the nurse uses self-awareness to keep it that way. It differs from a social relationship, where both people’s needs are met and advice and personal stories flow both ways.","Termination starts in orientation: telling the patient the end date up front keeps the ending from feeling like abandonment.","Peplau’s phases: Preorientation: before meeting. Gather data, and do a self-assessment of your own feelings and biases.; Orientation: introductions; set the contract: purpose, time, place, how often, confidentiality and its limits, and when the relationship ends. Build trust; the patient may test you (lateness, limit testing).; Working: explore thoughts, feelings and stressors; problem-solve; practice new coping; transference and resistance often appear.; Termination: review progress and goals, summarize, plan follow-up; talk about feelings of loss. The patient may regress or be angry. The end date was planned from the start.","Factors that help the relationship: Consistency and reliability (be on time, keep promises).; Genuineness, empathy, positive regard (accepting the person even when not accepting the behavior).; Active listening, attention to nonverbal cues, cultural humility.; Self-awareness: the Johari window (open, hidden, blind and unknown parts of the self)."],"terms":["Transference","Countertransference","Boundary crossing","Empathy vs sympathy"],"termDefinitions":{"Transference":"A patient treats a nurse with anger because the nurse “acts just like my mother.” The patient unconsciously moves feelings about someone from their past onto the nurse. Expected. Don’t take it personally; explore it, and keep limits clear.","Countertransference":"A nurse feels unusual irritation with a patient who reminds her of her ex, or tries to rescue a patient who reminds her of her son. The nurse’s own unconscious feelings about someone from their past get moved onto the patient. A nurse’s problem to recognize and manage: reflect, talk to a supervisor, and switch assignments if needed.","Boundary crossing":"Sharing a lot of personal information, accepting gifts, spending far more time with one patient, keeping secrets, meeting outside work. The relationship slowly shifts toward the nurse’s needs, or toward a social relationship. Ask: “Whose needs am I meeting?” Bring it to your supervisor; it is always the nurse’s responsibility to keep boundaries.","Empathy vs sympathy":"Empathy: “It sounds like you feel trapped.” Sympathy: “I feel so sorry for you; I cried when my dad died too.” Empathy is understanding the patient’s feelings from their point of view while staying separate; sympathy is sharing the feelings, which shifts the focus to the nurse. Use empathy: it keeps the focus on the patient and keeps you objective."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-relationship","title":"The therapeutic relationship","blocks":[{"title":"Peplau’s phases","lines":["Preorientation: before meeting. Gather data, and do a self-assessment of your own feelings and biases.","Orientation: introductions; set the contract: purpose, time, place, how often, confidentiality and its limits, and when the relationship ends. Build trust; the patient may test you (lateness, limit testing).","Working: explore thoughts, feelings and stressors; problem-solve; practice new coping; transference and resistance often appear.","Termination: review progress and goals, summarize, plan follow-up; talk about feelings of loss. The patient may regress or be angry. The end date was planned from the start.","Termination starts in orientation: telling the patient the end date up front keeps the ending from feeling like abandonment."]},{"title":"Factors that help the relationship","lines":["Consistency and reliability (be on time, keep promises).","Genuineness, empathy, positive regard (accepting the person even when not accepting the behavior).","Active listening, attention to nonverbal cues, cultural humility.","Self-awareness: the Johari window (open, hidden, blind and unknown parts of the self)."]},{"title":"Boundary rules for students and new nurses","lines":["Limited self-disclosure: only if it helps the patient, briefly, then return the focus to them.","No gifts (or per policy), no social media contact, no personal phone numbers, no dating, no meeting after discharge.","Don’t promise secrets: “I can’t keep information that affects your safety from the team.”","Don’t promise to keep in touch after termination."]},{"title":"Responding to common patient behaviors","lines":["Threatens to hurt self: “I’m concerned about your safety.” Ask directly; tell the team.","Asks you to keep a secret: “I can’t promise that. If it affects your safety, I’ll share it with the team.”","Asks personal questions: answer briefly if appropriate, then refocus: “We’re here to talk about you.”","Flirts or asks for a date: set the limit clearly and kindly: “Our relationship is professional.”","Silence: sit with them; offer self.","Cries: stay, allow it, offer tissues; don’t rush to fix it."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-relationship:case","kind":"choice","scene":"In which phase of the nurse–patient relationship are the terms of the contract, including when the relationship will end, established?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Preorientation","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date."},{"id":"o1","text":"Orientation","correct":true,"why":"Supported. The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date."},{"id":"o2","text":"Working","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date."},{"id":"o3","text":"Termination","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. The orientation phase sets the contract: purpose, time, place, confidentiality and the end date."}]},{"id":"t-relationship:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"In which phase is the termination date set?","answer":"The orientation phase (as part of the contract).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-relationship:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"A nurse feels protective of a patient who looks like her daughter. Term?","answer":"Countertransference.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"timeline","title":"Recognize the phase by its task","cards":[{"title":"Prepare","text":"Review information and examine your assumptions."},{"title":"Orientation","text":"Agree on the purpose, limits, time and ending."},{"title":"Working","text":"Explore concerns and practise toward goals."},{"title":"Termination","text":"Review progress, discuss ending and arrange follow-up."}],"caption":"The task distinguishes the phase more reliably than an isolated word such as “ending.”"},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize the phase by its task","task":"Arrange the relationship tasks.","cue":"Place the main tasks in their usual relationship sequence.","rule":"Preparation supports orientation; the working phase uses that agreement; termination reviews and closes the work.","trap":"Start before the first meeting, then establish the agreement before using it."},"id":"t-e4-peplau","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Peplau’s Model of the Nurse-Patient Relationship","notes":["t-relationship"],"book":"Assigned Chapter 9 section: Peplau’s model. Uses the existing site-authored phase card; complete relationship notes are stored under Chapter 8.","why":["Prepare before meeting: review relevant information and notice your own assumptions. In orientation, introduce the role and agree on purpose, boundaries, time and confidentiality limits. Establish the planned ending early.","Working is the active middle of the relationship. Explore concerns, connect patterns, practise coping and work toward the patient’s goals. The nurse uses a professional relationship to support the patient’s work.","Termination reviews progress, discusses feelings about ending and plans the next support. Mentioning the end date during orientation is preparation for termination, not evidence that the relationship is already ending."],"terms":[],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"You will meet with a patient during this admission, practise a coping skill together and end your work when the placement finishes.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-relationship","title":"Peplau’s Model of the Nurse-Patient Relationship","blocks":[{"title":"Peplau’s phases","lines":["Preorientation: before meeting. Gather data, and do a self-assessment of your own feelings and biases.","Orientation: introductions; set the contract: purpose, time, place, how often, confidentiality and its limits, and when the relationship ends. Build trust; the patient may test you (lateness, limit testing).","Working: explore thoughts, feelings and stressors; problem-solve; practice new coping; transference and resistance often appear.","Termination: review progress and goals, summarize, plan follow-up; talk about feelings of loss. The patient may regress or be angry. The end date was planned from the start."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"Open RN: communication and therapeutic relationships","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK610828/"}],"steps":[{"kind":"order","scene":"Place the main tasks in their usual relationship sequence.","task":"Arrange the relationship tasks.","items":["Review available information and your own assumptions.","Introduce the role and agree on purpose, time and the planned ending.","Explore concerns and practise coping toward goals.","Review progress, discuss the ending and plan follow-up."],"why":"Preparation supports orientation; the working phase uses that agreement; termination reviews and closes the work.","hint":"Start before the first meeting, then establish the agreement before using it.","retryHint":"Start before the first meeting, then establish the agreement before using it.","id":"t-e4-peplau:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"At the first meeting, the nurse says, “We will meet through Friday, when my placement ends.”","task":"What is the nurse doing now?","hint":"Distinguish planning an ending from carrying it out.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Establishing the orientation agreement.","why":"The ending is being clarified at the start so expectations are shared.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Completing termination because Friday was mentioned.","why":"The nurse is planning the ending, not yet reviewing and closing completed work.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Beginning informal friendship after the placement.","why":"The statement defines a professional time boundary; it does not create a social relationship.","correct":false}],"id":"t-e4-peplau:active:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"On the final meeting, the patient says, “I’m worried I won’t manage without these visits.”","task":"Which response fits the phase?","hint":"Support closure and continuity without extending the professional relationship into friendship.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Offer private social visits to avoid a difficult goodbye.","why":"Extending into a social relationship blurs the professional boundary.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Ignore the concern and leave quickly.","why":"The ending and feelings about it need acknowledgement.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Discuss the concern, review coping gains and confirm the next sources of support.","why":"Termination includes feelings, progress and continuity after the relationship ends.","correct":true}],"id":"t-e4-peplau:active:2"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Hear the concern → choose the tool → check its effect","cards":[{"title":"Invite","text":"Give room for the patient’s meaning and concern."},{"title":"Clarify","text":"Check an unclear statement rather than guessing."},{"title":"Protect","text":"Ask directly when a safety cue needs assessment."}],"caption":"Choose a technique for this moment, not a memorized “always use” phrase."},"strategy":{"title":"Hear the concern → choose the tool → check its effect","task":"Which response best keeps the concern central?","cue":"The patient pauses after saying that no one listens.","rule":"This invites the patient’s experience without defending others or promising an outcome.","trap":"Choose the response that explores this person’s experience."},"id":"t-comm","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Communication Skills for Nurses","notes":["t-comm"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 9; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Listen for what the patient is communicating, then choose a response that fits the task. Open invitations, silence, observations, clarification and reflection can help the person explain an experience in their own words.","False reassurance, rapid questioning, advice and judgment can close exploration. Avoid assuming a question is therapeutic just because it begins with an open word; consider its tone, timing and effect.","A safety cue changes the task. A direct question about harm can be more appropriate than a broad invitation when risk needs clarification. With a delusion or hallucination, acknowledge distress without endorsing the belief, and assess the actual safety concern."],"terms":["psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"A patient looks down and says, “No one really listens to me.” There is no stated immediate danger.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-comm","title":"Therapeutic communication","blocks":[{"title":"Techniques that help","lines":["Silence: gives time to think.","Offering self: “I’ll sit with you for a while.”","Giving broad openings / general leads: “What would you like to talk about?” “Go on.”","Making observations: “You seem tense.” “I notice you’re wringing your hands.”","Restating: “You can’t sleep at night?”","Reflecting: returning feelings or questions: “You feel angry when this happens?” “What do you think you should do?”","Focusing: “Let’s go back to what you said about your job.”","Exploring: “Tell me more about that.”","Seeking clarification: “I’m not sure I follow. Can you give an example?”","Presenting reality: “I don’t hear any voices; I’m the only one here.”","Voicing doubt: “That’s hard to believe.”","Verbalizing the implied and translating into feelings: “I’m dead inside” → “Are you saying you feel lifeless?”","Placing events in sequence, encouraging comparison, suggesting collaboration, summarizing, giving information, giving recognition (“You combed your hair today”)."]},{"title":"Blocks to avoid","lines":["False reassurance: “Everything will be fine.” (Belittles the concern.)","Giving advice: “If I were you, I’d…” (Takes away problem-solving.)","Minimizing feelings: “Everyone gets down sometimes.”","Asking “why”: “Why did you stop your medicine?” (Sounds like criticism; makes people defensive.)","Excessive questioning (several at once).","Approving or disapproving: “I’m proud of you.” “You shouldn’t have done that.” (Judges; the patient may try to please you.)","Value judgments, belittling, defending (“The doctor is very good”), changing the subject, literal responses, stereotyped comments (“Hang in there”), agreeing with delusions.","All of these shift the focus off the patient’s feelings or close the conversation."]},{"title":"Nonverbal communication","lines":["Up to most of a message is nonverbal: facial expression, eye contact, posture, gestures, tone, touch, distance.","When words and nonverbal cues don’t match, believe the nonverbal and explore it.","Sit at an angle, at eye level, open posture, lean in slightly. Culture shapes eye contact, touch and personal space; ask, don’t assume."]},{"title":"The clinical interview","lines":["A quiet, private, safe place; sit near the door if safety is a concern (for both of you).","Introduce yourself, state the purpose and how long, and explain confidentiality.","Start with open-ended questions; let the patient lead; take few notes.","Use closed questions only to get specific facts (“Do you have a plan?”)."]},{"title":"How to pick the right answer on the exam","lines":["First: is there a safety issue? Address it directly (“Are you thinking about killing yourself?”).","Then choose the response that reflects feelings or explores the patient’s statement.","Eliminate responses that reassure, advise, judge, ask “why,” defend or change the subject.","With psychosis: don’t argue or agree; present reality and respond to the feeling."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"Open RN: communication and therapeutic relationships","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK610828/"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"The patient pauses after saying that no one listens.","task":"Which response best keeps the concern central?","hint":"Choose the response that explores this person’s experience.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Everyone feels ignored sometimes.”","why":"This generalizes and can minimize the specific experience.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"“Tell me about a time you felt unheard.”","why":"This invites the patient’s experience without defending others or promising an outcome.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"“The staff are very busy and try their best.”","why":"Defending staff shifts attention away from the patient’s concern.","correct":false}],"id":"t-comm:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Later the patient says, “Sometimes I think I might hurt myself tonight.”","task":"Which response fits the new task?","hint":"A safety cue can require a direct assessment rather than a broad opening.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Ask directly about current thoughts, intent, plan and immediate safety, and obtain appropriate support.","why":"The statement creates a specific risk-assessment need; direct questions and protective action are appropriate.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Use only silence until the patient changes the subject.","why":"Silence alone leaves an immediate safety concern unassessed.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Promise secrecy so the patient will keep talking.","why":"The nurse cannot promise secrecy when safety requires additional help.","correct":false}],"id":"t-comm:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Stress, coping and defense mechanisms","cards":[{"title":"Defense mechanisms (with examples)","text":"Denial: refusing to accept reality. “The biopsy is wrong; I don’t have cancer.” · Projection: putting your own unacceptable feelings on someone else. A man attracted to a coworker accuses his wife of cheating. · Displacement: moving feelings to a safer target. Yelled at by the boss, he yells at his kids."},{"title":"Levels of anxiety","text":"Mild: alert, perceptual field heightened, can learn; restless. · Moderate: field narrowed, selective inattention; can problem-solve with help. · Severe: field greatly reduced; can’t problem-solve; physical symptoms; needs direction."},{"title":"Stress management to teach","text":"Deep (diaphragmatic) breathing; progressive muscle relaxation; guided imagery; meditation and mindfulness; biofeedback. · Exercise, 7–9 hours of sleep, good nutrition, limiting caffeine and alcohol. · Cognitive reframing, journaling, humor, time management."}],"caption":"Fight-or-flight, Selye’s stages, levels of anxiety, coping and defense mechanisms."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"The only defense mechanism that is conscious?","cue":"Fight-or-flight, Selye’s stages, levels of anxiety, coping and defense mechanisms.","rule":"Suppression.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-stress","ch":"10","chapters":["10","15"],"title":"Stress, coping and defense mechanisms","notes":["t-stress"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 10 and 15; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Stress is the body’s response to any demand. Short-term, the stress response protects us; chronic stress damages the heart, immune system, mood and memory. Coping is what we consciously do to manage stress; defense mechanisms are mostly unconscious ways the mind protects itself from anxiety. Either can be adaptive or harmful.","On the exam, match the quote to the mechanism. Key pairs: suppression is conscious, repression is not; projection blames others, displacement redirects.","Defense mechanisms (with examples): Denial: refusing to accept reality. “The biopsy is wrong; I don’t have cancer.”; Projection: putting your own unacceptable feelings on someone else. A man attracted to a coworker accuses his wife of cheating.; Displacement: moving feelings to a safer target. Yelled at by the boss, he yells at his kids.; Rationalization: a logical-sounding excuse. “I failed because the test was unfair.”; Reaction formation: acting the opposite of what you feel. Being overly sweet to someone you dislike.; Regression: returning to an earlier stage. An adult patient sulks or throws a tantrum; a 4-year-old with a new sibling wets the bed.; Repression: unconscious forgetting of something painful.; Suppression: conscious decision to put it aside for now. “I’ll worry about the rent after my exam.” (The only conscious one, and healthy.); Sublimation: channeling an unacceptable impulse into something constructive. Anger into running or kickboxing. (Always healthy.); Compensation: making up for a weakness by excelling elsewhere.; Intellectualization: using facts and logic to avoid feelings. Reciting survival statistics instead of grieving.; Undoing: an act to cancel out a previous one. Buying flowers after an argument.; Identification: taking on traits of an admired person.; Conversion: anxiety becomes a physical symptom with no organic cause (sudden blindness).; Splitting: seeing people as all good or all bad (common in borderline personality disorder).; Dissociation: disconnecting from memory, identity or surroundings to escape distress.; Altruism: meeting your own needs by helping others.","Levels of anxiety: Mild: alert, perceptual field heightened, can learn; restless.; Moderate: field narrowed, selective inattention; can problem-solve with help.; Severe: field greatly reduced; can’t problem-solve; physical symptoms; needs direction.; Panic: disorganized, can’t attend to the environment, possible loss of reality; needs safety."],"terms":["Fight-or-flight response","General adaptation syndrome (Selye)","Eustress vs distress","Coping"],"termDefinitions":{"Fight-or-flight response":"Racing heart, fast breathing, dilated pupils, sweating, tense muscles, dry mouth. The sympathetic nervous system and the adrenal medulla release epinephrine and norepinephrine, sending blood and glucose to the muscles and brain. The same signs show up with anxiety and panic. Relaxation techniques activate the parasympathetic system and reverse them.","General adaptation syndrome (Selye)":"Alarm at a crisis → coping for weeks → getting sick and worn out. Alarm (fight-or-flight) → resistance (the HPA axis raises cortisol to sustain energy) → exhaustion (resources run out; illness follows). Chronic stress and high cortisol suppress immunity and raise BP and glucose. Ask about stressors in every health history.","Eustress vs distress":"Starting a new job you wanted vs losing one. Both trigger the stress response; eustress is positive and motivating, distress is negative and harmful. Even positive life changes (marriage, a new baby) count as stressors.","Coping":"Making a study plan (problem-focused) or talking to a friend (emotion-focused). Problem-focused coping changes the situation; emotion-focused coping changes how you feel about it. Both are useful at different times. Ask, “What has helped you get through hard times before?” and build on those strengths."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-stress","title":"Stress, coping and defense mechanisms","blocks":[{"title":"Defense mechanisms (with examples)","lines":["Denial: refusing to accept reality. “The biopsy is wrong; I don’t have cancer.”","Projection: putting your own unacceptable feelings on someone else. A man attracted to a coworker accuses his wife of cheating.","Displacement: moving feelings to a safer target. Yelled at by the boss, he yells at his kids.","Rationalization: a logical-sounding excuse. “I failed because the test was unfair.”","Reaction formation: acting the opposite of what you feel. Being overly sweet to someone you dislike.","Regression: returning to an earlier stage. An adult patient sulks or throws a tantrum; a 4-year-old with a new sibling wets the bed.","Repression: unconscious forgetting of something painful.","Suppression: conscious decision to put it aside for now. “I’ll worry about the rent after my exam.” (The only conscious one, and healthy.)","Sublimation: channeling an unacceptable impulse into something constructive. Anger into running or kickboxing. (Always healthy.)","Compensation: making up for a weakness by excelling elsewhere.","Intellectualization: using facts and logic to avoid feelings. Reciting survival statistics instead of grieving.","Undoing: an act to cancel out a previous one. Buying flowers after an argument.","Identification: taking on traits of an admired person.","Conversion: anxiety becomes a physical symptom with no organic cause (sudden blindness).","Splitting: seeing people as all good or all bad (common in borderline personality disorder).","Dissociation: disconnecting from memory, identity or surroundings to escape distress.","Altruism: meeting your own needs by helping others.","On the exam, match the quote to the mechanism. Key pairs: suppression is conscious, repression is not; projection blames others, displacement redirects."]},{"title":"Levels of anxiety","lines":["Mild: alert, perceptual field heightened, can learn; restless.","Moderate: field narrowed, selective inattention; can problem-solve with help.","Severe: field greatly reduced; can’t problem-solve; physical symptoms; needs direction.","Panic: disorganized, can’t attend to the environment, possible loss of reality; needs safety."]},{"title":"Stress management to teach","lines":["Deep (diaphragmatic) breathing; progressive muscle relaxation; guided imagery; meditation and mindfulness; biofeedback.","Exercise, 7–9 hours of sleep, good nutrition, limiting caffeine and alcohol.","Cognitive reframing, journaling, humor, time management.","Social support: the strongest buffer against stress."]},{"title":"Adverse childhood experiences (ACEs)","lines":["Abuse, neglect, or a household with violence, substance use, mental illness, divorce or incarceration, before age 18.","4 or more ACEs sharply raise the adult risk of heart and lung disease, diabetes, substance use, depression and suicide.","Toxic stress changes the developing brain and stress system; safe, stable relationships protect."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-stress:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A student says, “I’m not going to think about my parents’ divorce until after my finals.” This is:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Repression","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Suppression is a conscious choice to postpone dealing with something. Repression is unconscious."},{"id":"o1","text":"Suppression","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Suppression is a conscious choice to postpone dealing with something. Repression is unconscious."},{"id":"o2","text":"Denial","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Suppression is a conscious choice to postpone dealing with something. Repression is unconscious."},{"id":"o3","text":"Displacement","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Suppression is a conscious choice to postpone dealing with something. Repression is unconscious."}]},{"id":"t-stress:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"The only defense mechanism that is conscious?","answer":"Suppression.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-stress:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"“I didn’t get the job because the interviewer was biased.”","answer":"Rationalization.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Trauma and trauma-informed care","cards":[{"title":"Principles of trauma-informed care","text":"Safety: physical and emotional. · Trustworthiness and transparency: explain what you’ll do before doing it. · Peer support."},{"title":"What trauma does","text":"Changes the brain’s alarm system (amygdala), memory (hippocampus) and calming circuits (prefrontal cortex). · Shows up in the body: chronic pain, GI symptoms, headaches, sleep problems. · Leads to hypervigilance, avoidance, numbing, dissociation, substance use and self-harm as ways of coping."},{"title":"Crisis and crisis intervention","text":"A crisis is a temporary state when usual coping fails, typically resolving within 4–6 weeks, for better or worse. · Types: maturational (developmental stages), situational (job loss, illness), adventitious (disasters, violence). · Steps: assess safety first, then the problem, supports and coping; help the person act (directive when needed); follow up."}],"caption":"What trauma does, how to care without retraumatizing, and crisis care."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"The trauma-informed question?","cue":"What trauma does, how to care without retraumatizing, and crisis care.","rule":"“What happened to you?” instead of “What’s wrong with you?”","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-trauma","ch":"10","chapters":["10","16"],"title":"Trauma and trauma-informed care","notes":["t-trauma"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 10 and 16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Trauma is an event, or a series of events, that is physically or emotionally harmful or life-threatening and has lasting effects on functioning and well-being. Trauma-informed care assumes anyone could have a trauma history and changes the question from “What’s wrong with you?” to “What happened to you?” The disorders themselves (PTSD, acute stress, adjustment, dissociative) are under Think it through.","Restraint, being undressed, loud voices or being touched without warning can retraumatize people. Choices and explanations return a sense of control.","Principles of trauma-informed care: Safety: physical and emotional.; Trustworthiness and transparency: explain what you’ll do before doing it.; Peer support.; Collaboration and mutuality: decisions made with the patient.; Empowerment, voice and choice: offer choices whenever possible.; Attention to cultural, historical and gender issues.","What trauma does: Changes the brain’s alarm system (amygdala), memory (hippocampus) and calming circuits (prefrontal cortex).; Shows up in the body: chronic pain, GI symptoms, headaches, sleep problems.; Leads to hypervigilance, avoidance, numbing, dissociation, substance use and self-harm as ways of coping.; Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing, then reconnection."],"terms":[],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-trauma","title":"Trauma and trauma-informed care","blocks":[{"title":"Principles of trauma-informed care","lines":["Safety: physical and emotional.","Trustworthiness and transparency: explain what you’ll do before doing it.","Peer support.","Collaboration and mutuality: decisions made with the patient.","Empowerment, voice and choice: offer choices whenever possible.","Attention to cultural, historical and gender issues.","Restraint, being undressed, loud voices or being touched without warning can retraumatize people. Choices and explanations return a sense of control."]},{"title":"What trauma does","lines":["Changes the brain’s alarm system (amygdala), memory (hippocampus) and calming circuits (prefrontal cortex).","Shows up in the body: chronic pain, GI symptoms, headaches, sleep problems.","Leads to hypervigilance, avoidance, numbing, dissociation, substance use and self-harm as ways of coping.","Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing, then reconnection."]},{"title":"Crisis and crisis intervention","lines":["A crisis is a temporary state when usual coping fails, typically resolving within 4–6 weeks, for better or worse.","Types: maturational (developmental stages), situational (job loss, illness), adventitious (disasters, violence).","Steps: assess safety first, then the problem, supports and coping; help the person act (directive when needed); follow up.","Goal: return to at least the pre-crisis level of functioning."]},{"title":"Grounding for dissociation or flashbacks","lines":["Use the person’s name; orient them to time, place and safety.","5-4-3-2-1: name 5 things you see, 4 you can touch, 3 you hear, 2 you smell, 1 you taste.","Feet firmly on the floor; hold something cold; stomp feet; describe the room aloud."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-trauma:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"The trauma-informed question?","answer":"“What happened to you?” instead of “What’s wrong with you?”","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-trauma:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"How long does a crisis usually last?","answer":"About 4–6 weeks.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Identify the helpful function","cards":[{"title":"Universality","text":"Recognizing a shared experience."},{"title":"Altruism","text":"Contributing in a way that helps another member."},{"title":"Interpersonal learning","text":"Using feedback to understand and change an interaction."}],"caption":"Describe what changed for the member before choosing the factor’s name."},"strategy":{"title":"Identify the helpful function","task":"Which factor is most directly illustrated?","cue":"Another member describes a similar struggle; the new member says, “I feel less alone.”","rule":"The member recognizes that others have a similar experience and feels less isolated.","trap":"Focus on the stated effect: less alone because another person shares the experience."},"id":"t-groups-foundations","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Therapeutic groups: benefits and Yalom’s routes to change","notes":["t-groups"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["A group can offer something a lecture alone cannot: members hear peers, receive feedback and try a new way of relating. Shared time also creates privacy, fit and participation limits that the leader must consider.","Name a therapeutic factor by what the interaction does. Universality reduces the sense of being alone; hope makes improvement seem possible; altruism lets someone benefit through helping; cohesiveness supports belonging.","The other factors describe learning, expression and meaning: information, imitation, social skills, interpersonal learning, family-pattern work, catharsis and existential concerns. Several can occur together. Use the complete reference to compare all eleven."],"terms":["Group","Therapeutic group","Group work","Group psychotherapy","Therapeutic factors"],"termDefinitions":{"Group":"Several people work toward an agreed purpose and affect each other’s experience. Membership creates interdependence; one person’s response can change the next person’s behavior. Assess the relationships as well as each person’s needs.","Therapeutic group":"Members practise a useful skill, gain support or explore a difficulty together. A shared activity becomes therapeutic when it supports psychological development or growth. Choose a purpose and measure a relevant benefit; attendance alone is insufficient.","Group work":"The nurse plans a session, attends to participation and checks what members gained. Useful change depends on both preparation and what happens between members. Plan, facilitate and evaluate instead of merely gathering people in a room.","Group psychotherapy":"A trained clinician helps members examine recurring relationship patterns and psychiatric symptoms. The relationships within the session become part of a specialized treatment. Distinguish psychotherapy from RN teaching or activity facilitation; verify preparation and scope.","Therapeutic factors":"A member gains hope, feels understood or tries a new way of relating. Yalom described several routes through which the group experience can support change. Match the helpful factor to the group’s purpose; one mechanism does not explain every benefit."},"patient":"In a coping group, a new member says, “I thought I was the only person struggling like this.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-groups","title":"Therapeutic groups: benefits and Yalom’s routes to change","blocks":[{"title":"Why use a group, and when does it fit?","lines":["Therapeutic groups expanded when clinicians needed to help many people affected by war. A group can now support learning, coping and relationships in hospitals and community care.","One facilitator can reach several people. Peers contribute experiences the facilitator does not have, offer feedback and create chances to rehearse communication with support.","Trade-offs include divided speaking time, privacy risks, disruptive behavior and pressure to conform. A larger group does not automatically give each person enough attention.","Select for this group’s purpose, readiness and current ability to participate. Limited money, few friends or quiet listening alone are not reasons to exclude someone.","Yalom’s existential approach includes responsibility and freedom, isolation, meaning and mortality. A useful group helps people face these concerns while remaining connected."]},{"title":"Yalom: belonging, hope and contribution","lines":["Universality: discovering a shared struggle can reduce the feeling of being uniquely defective. Recognizing similarity differs from being promised an identical outcome.","Instillation of hope: seeing another member make progress can make change seem possible. Encouragement is grounded in possibility, not guaranteed recovery.","Altruism: contributing something useful can strengthen the helper’s sense of worth. Receiving a lecture is a different mechanism.","Imparting information: members gain practical knowledge from the leader or one another. Check the accuracy of peer advice before it becomes a treatment instruction.","Group cohesiveness: a sense of acceptance links members to each other, the leader and the whole group. Shared trust supports disclosure; forced agreement is not cohesion."]},{"title":"Yalom: trying new relationships and making meaning","lines":["Imitative behavior: a member tries a constructive behavior after watching it. Development of socializing techniques: feedback and practice improve everyday interaction.","Interpersonal learning: members notice their effect on others, test another response and examine the result. This involves more than memorizing communication rules.","Corrective recapitulation of the primary family group: familiar family interaction patterns appear in the session and can be worked through in a safer relationship setting.","Catharsis: expressing emotion can help when the experience is understood and integrated. Emotional intensity by itself does not prove useful treatment; uncontrolled expression can overwhelm the work.","Existential factors: members consider unavoidable limits, choice, loss and personal meaning. The aim is a meaningful response to life, not a promise to remove every source of suffering."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"AGPA: group psychotherapy practice guidance","url":"https://agpa.org/guidelines-ethics/practice-guidelines-for-group-psychotherapy-2/"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"Another member describes a similar struggle; the new member says, “I feel less alone.”","task":"Which factor is most directly illustrated?","hint":"Focus on the stated effect: less alone because another person shares the experience.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Imparting information.","why":"No new factual teaching is the main effect described.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Catharsis.","why":"Emotional expression may occur, but the key change is recognizing a shared experience.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Universality.","why":"The member recognizes that others have a similar experience and feels less isolated.","correct":true}],"id":"t-groups-foundations:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A member notices that interrupting makes peers withdraw, practises waiting and receives different feedback.","task":"Which factor best explains this work?","hint":"Distinguish receiving facts from learning through an interaction.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Interpersonal learning.","why":"Feedback connects the member’s behavior with its effect and supports trying a new response.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Imitation alone.","why":"The scene emphasizes examining one’s own interpersonal effect rather than simply copying another person.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Imparting information alone.","why":"Factual instruction does not capture the feedback-and-practice process described.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-foundations:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Design the frame before leading the discussion","cards":[{"title":"Purpose","text":"What should members learn or practise?"},{"title":"Membership","text":"Who can benefit and participate safely?"},{"title":"Process","text":"How will the interaction support the purpose?"}],"caption":"The discussion topic and the way people interact are separate parts of planning."},"strategy":{"title":"Design the frame before leading the discussion","task":"What is the process issue?","cue":"Members are discussing adverse effects, but only two people get speaking time.","rule":"Who gets to speak and how others respond are group-process observations.","trap":"Content is what; process is how."},"id":"t-groups-design","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Plan the group: process, membership and setting","notes":["t-groups"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Plan the purpose, membership, setting and leader preparation before recruitment. A medication-education group and a psychotherapy group require different tasks and competencies.","Content is the subject being discussed. Process is how members interact while discussing it. Norms, speaking time, alliances and feedback can affect whether the content is useful.","Match structure to the setting. Changing inpatient membership may need brief, clear sessions; a continuing community group may build on earlier work. Open or closed membership and shared or varied member characteristics each bring benefits and limits."],"terms":["Group content","Group process","Group norms","Feedback","Open and closed membership","Homogeneous, heterogeneous and subgroup","Group themes and conflict"],"termDefinitions":{"Group content":"Participants discuss how to ask for help after discharge. Content is the subject matter and spoken material; a transcript captures this layer. Record the topic without mistaking it for an account of the interaction.","Group process":"People interrupt one speaker but listen closely to another discussing the same topic. Process concerns how members relate, including tone, gestures, silence and patterns of participation. Notice who responds to whom and intervene when the interaction blocks the work.","Group norms":"Members expect a turn to speak and arrive before the session begins. Shared expectations can support safety or quietly pressure members to agree. Clarify helpful expectations and address rules that silence disagreement.","Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone.","Open and closed membership":"An admission group welcomes new people; a fixed course keeps its original membership. Open groups admit replacements. Closed groups do not add members when someone leaves. Revisit orientation with newcomers and plan continuity for changing membership.","Homogeneous, heterogeneous and subgroup":"Members may share a central characteristic, have varied backgrounds, or form a small alliance. Similarity and diversity affect fit. A subgroup is a smaller cluster whose loyalty may compete with the whole group. Assess the actual mix and include quieter or less-connected members; these terms do not rank people’s value.","Group themes and conflict":"Different stories keep returning to trust; two members disagree about asking for help. A theme is a recurring meaning. Conflict is an expressed difference that may expose useful work. Name a possible theme tentatively and support respectful resolution rather than suppressing every disagreement."},"patient":"You are planning a short inpatient medication-education group where admissions and discharges change membership.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-groups","title":"Plan the group: process, membership and setting","blocks":[{"title":"Read both the topic and the interaction","lines":["Content answers “What was discussed?” Process asks “How did people respond to one another?” Track tone, posture, timing, interruptions and silence as well as spoken facts.","A repeated concern such as belonging may be a theme across several different stories. Offer the connection tentatively and let members respond.","Make norms visible. Expectations about punctuality, turn-taking and privacy can support trust; an expectation of universal agreement can suppress useful differences.","Help members speak to each other rather than routing every reply through the leader. Explore how an interaction affects participation.","Invite specific feedback and help the group resolve conflict. Cohesion is associated with better outcomes, but warmth alone is not an outcome measure."]},{"title":"Design the group before inviting members","lines":["Begin with a clear name, purpose and realistic outcomes. Decide what evidence will show the group helped, not just whether the session occurred.","Choose a population whose needs fit the purpose. Consider functioning, readiness, interpersonal style and the support available to the leader.","Plan the meeting frequency, session times and treatment duration. Specify what the leader and members will each do, with consistent plans when staff rotate.","Open membership allows new arrivals; closed membership stays fixed despite departures. A group may also be time-limited or ongoing; duration and membership are separate decisions.","Homogeneous members share an important characteristic; heterogeneous members differ. Notice subgroups, including pairs whose separate alliance may leave others outside.","Match the room to the group. Attend to sound, privacy, light and temperature. A circle without a privileged seat supports visibility and equality; crowding or excessive distance can inhibit participation.","Avoid assigning a highly disruptive person to an inexperienced facilitator without support, or pushing a withdrawn person into an intensely probing setting without preparation."]},{"title":"Hospital groups and community groups need different structure","lines":["Outpatient membership is often more stable, allowing preparation and sustained relationships. Functioning may be more similar, but willingness still varies, including when attendance is court-ordered.","Inpatients may have different levels of functioning and may join with little preparation. Admission and discharge change the group, and unit interruptions are common.","Hospital treatment lasts only as long as the admission. Use a clear agenda and active time management; limited continuity can constrain cohesion.","An outpatient group may meet for a fixed course or continue longer, with more room for member direction. Reassess actual needs rather than applying the setting label rigidly."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"AGPA: group psychotherapy practice guidance","url":"https://agpa.org/guidelines-ethics/practice-guidelines-for-group-psychotherapy-2/"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"Members are discussing adverse effects, but only two people get speaking time.","task":"What is the process issue?","hint":"Content is what; process is how.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"The medication topic itself.","why":"That is the content; the process issue concerns participation.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Unequal participation in the interaction.","why":"Who gets to speak and how others respond are group-process observations.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"The printed drug names on the handout.","why":"Those are content details rather than the interaction pattern described.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-design:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A new patient may attend only today’s session.","task":"Which design choice best fits this setting?","hint":"Plan for the actual continuity of membership.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Make today’s learning usable on its own, with a clear purpose and opening.","why":"A changing inpatient group needs a session that new members can enter and benefit from.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Require every member to remember last month’s session.","why":"That assumes continuity that the setting cannot provide.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Skip the purpose because membership changes.","why":"Changing membership increases the need for a clear frame; it does not remove it.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-design:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"timeline","title":"The leader’s task changes as the group develops","cards":[{"title":"Orientation","text":"Clarify purpose, agreements and ways to participate."},{"title":"Working","text":"Use interaction, feedback and supported practice."},{"title":"Termination","text":"Review gains, acknowledge ending and plan transfer."}],"caption":"Do not confuse a difficult working moment with automatic failure."},"strategy":{"title":"The leader’s task changes as the group develops","task":"Place the leader’s tasks in phase order.","cue":"Arrange the main group-phase tasks.","rule":"Orientation establishes the frame; working uses it; termination helps members carry the learning forward.","trap":"Set the frame before working within it, then close the work."},"id":"t-groups-development","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Lead through orientation, working and termination","notes":["t-groups"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["At orientation, members need a purpose, agreements and enough safety to begin participating. Early caution and testing do not mean the group has failed.","In working, members explore issues, receive feedback and practise change. Conflict can be part of development when the leader helps members address it respectfully. Storming, norming and performing describe changes within the group’s development.","Termination makes the ending explicit. Review what changed, discuss feelings about leaving and plan how members will use the learning afterward. An ending is more than a final attendance check."],"terms":["Group phases","Feedback","Group norms"],"termDefinitions":{"Group phases":"A new group needs introductions; a mature group can work through disagreement. A group’s needs change as it forms, works and ends. Conflict can move it back to earlier tasks. Adapt the amount of structure and the leadership task to the phase.","Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone.","Group norms":"Members expect a turn to speak and arrive before the session begins. Shared expectations can support safety or quietly pressure members to agree. Clarify helpful expectations and address rules that silence disagreement."},"patient":"A new group is cautious at first. Later, members disagree over speaking time and then develop a workable shared agreement.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-groups","title":"Lead through orientation, working and termination","blocks":[{"title":"Orientation: build safety and a shared purpose","lines":["Start the first meeting with the leader’s introduction and invite members to introduce themselves. Explain what this group is for and how the meeting will work.","Expect some caution, anxiety, politeness or silence while trust is developing. Offer structure, identify common ground and encourage member-to-member responses.","Discuss respect, confidentiality and its limits before sensitive disclosure. Turn off distracting or recording devices under the group agreement; social media posts and photos can expose other members.","Explaining the purpose during a first session is orientation. Deciding that purpose while designing the service is planning."]},{"title":"Working: turn disagreement into useful work","lines":["Help members solve problems tied to the purpose. Support communication and conduct while allowing members to do more of the work.","Tuckman’s forming aligns with early group formation. Within the working period, storming includes challenges and clashes; norming develops cooperation and agreed roles; performing focuses those roles on the task.","A challenge to the leader is not automatically treatment failure. Maintain safety, examine the disagreement and model constructive conflict resolution.","Cohesion supports doing the work, and work plus feedback can develop insight. Development is not a one-way ladder. A new conflict or membership change can require renewed structure and trust-building before the group can work effectively again."]},{"title":"Termination and follow-up: make the ending useful","lines":["Prepare for the end rather than simply cancelling the next meeting. Ask members to identify accomplishments, new understanding and goals to carry forward.","Invite feedback about what helped and what did not. Loss, disappointment or anger about ending can be discussed directly and respectfully.","Check progress during the group’s life and again at the end. Use member feedback and objective outcome measures; the chapter’s Group Questionnaire example examines relationships, quality and structure.","Plan follow-up or referrals around continuing needs. Completion of a group does not mean all symptoms or risks have disappeared."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"AGPA: group psychotherapy practice guidance","url":"https://agpa.org/guidelines-ethics/practice-guidelines-for-group-psychotherapy-2/"}],"steps":[{"kind":"order","scene":"Arrange the main group-phase tasks.","task":"Place the leader’s tasks in phase order.","items":["Establish the purpose, agreements and participation expectations.","Help members use feedback and practise toward the group’s goals.","Review gains, discuss the ending and plan use beyond the group."],"why":"Orientation establishes the frame; working uses it; termination helps members carry the learning forward.","hint":"Set the frame before working within it, then close the work.","retryHint":"Set the frame before working within it, then close the work.","id":"t-groups-development:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Members disagree over airtime without threats or violence.","task":"What is the most useful leadership response?","hint":"Consider whether conflict can be used safely rather than automatically suppressed.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Declare the group unsuccessful and end it immediately.","why":"Disagreement can be worked with when safety is maintained.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Invite members to describe the effect and develop a fair participation agreement.","why":"This uses the conflict to build workable norms and interpersonal learning.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"Prevent any further expression of disagreement.","why":"Suppressing all differences prevents useful process work.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-development:active:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The final session is tomorrow.","task":"Which task belongs in the ending?","hint":"An ending needs review, feeling and a next step.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Review progress, discuss separation and identify follow-up practice.","why":"Termination connects gains with continued use and acknowledges the ending.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Introduce a major new task with no time for review.","why":"A new unfinished task can displace closure.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Avoid mentioning the ending so no one feels sad.","why":"Avoidance leaves feelings and continuity unaddressed.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-development:active:2"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"What does the contribution do?","cards":[{"title":"Task","text":"Moves the shared work forward."},{"title":"Maintenance","text":"Supports participation and workable relationships."},{"title":"Individual pattern","text":"Displaces the shared work with a personal demand or disruption."}],"caption":"The same member can take different roles at different moments."},"strategy":{"title":"What does the contribution do?","task":"Which role is most directly shown?","cue":"A member says, “We have heard from three people. Would anyone who has not spoken like a turn?”","rule":"The member notices unequal airtime and opens participation.","trap":"Identify the function: opening a route for others to participate."},"id":"t-groups-roles","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Recognize what a member’s behavior does for the group","notes":["t-groups"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Name a role by the function of a contribution, not by labeling the person. Task roles help the group do its work; maintenance roles make working together possible.","An information seeker asks for useful facts; an elaborator develops an idea; an orienter brings the group back to its purpose. A gatekeeper opens participation; an encourager supports contributions; a harmonizer helps members work through tension.","An individual-role pattern can displace shared work through attacking, blocking or repeatedly seeking personal attention. Describe the behavior and effect, explore its context and redirect without humiliation. The chapter reference keeps every named role available."],"terms":["Task roles","Maintenance roles","Individual roles","Feedback"],"termDefinitions":{"Task roles":"A participant asks for evidence or helps the group decide its next step. These behaviors move the group toward its stated work. Reinforce useful clarification, information and goal-directed action.","Maintenance roles":"A member notices an overlooked speaker and invites their contribution. These behaviors sustain inclusion and the relationships needed for the work. Support balanced participation and repair tension without abandoning the purpose.","Individual roles":"One person repeatedly seeks applause while the agreed topic is left behind. Personal needs can displace the shared task. This describes behavior, not a permanent identity. Describe the effect, set respectful limits and explore the underlying need.","Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone."},"patient":"During planning, one member asks for needed details while another notices that a quiet peer has not had a turn.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-groups","title":"Recognize what a member’s behavior does for the group","blocks":[{"title":"Task roles: keep the work moving","lines":["Orienter: points back to the goal when discussion drifts. Energizer: helps the group move from discussion into a decision or action.","Elaborator: supplies an illustration or extends an idea so the group can use it. Information seeker: asks for clarification or dependable facts.","Information giver: contributes relevant knowledge or experience. Opinion giver: offers a viewpoint that may influence the group’s priorities or values.","Recognize the function of the contribution. Talking often is not automatically a task role if the group’s purpose is being displaced."]},{"title":"Maintenance roles: keep participation possible","lines":["Gatekeeper: notices unequal airtime and opens a route for others to speak. Encourager: welcomes contributions and recognizes effort.","Harmonizer: helps members understand and settle a difference. Compromiser: adjusts their own position to preserve a workable relationship.","Standard setter: brings shared expectations into words. Follower: supports the group’s direction without needing to lead it.","Maintenance supports the relationships that let the task continue. It is not the same as preventing all conflict or demanding agreement."]},{"title":"Individual roles: describe behavior without labeling the person","lines":["Aggressor: attacks a member or an idea. Blocker: repeatedly obstructs progress rather than examining the shared problem.","Recognition seeker: draws attention toward personal achievements. Help seeker: repeatedly seeks sympathy in a way that displaces the group’s work.","Self-confessor: brings disclosure beyond the group’s purpose or boundaries. Disclosure is not automatically therapeutic just because it is personal.","Roles can shift. Explore the need, describe the effect and reinforce useful alternatives instead of assigning a fixed identity to a member."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"AGPA: group psychotherapy practice guidance","url":"https://agpa.org/guidelines-ethics/practice-guidelines-for-group-psychotherapy-2/"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A member says, “We have heard from three people. Would anyone who has not spoken like a turn?”","task":"Which role is most directly shown?","hint":"Identify the function: opening a route for others to participate.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Information seeker.","why":"The member is not asking for factual clarification of the task.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Aggressor.","why":"The statement invites participation rather than attacking someone.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Gatekeeper.","why":"The member notices unequal airtime and opens participation.","correct":true}],"id":"t-groups-roles:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A member repeatedly returns the discussion to a personal dispute unrelated to today’s work.","task":"Which response best addresses the behavior?","hint":"Describe behavior, effect and a useful redirection.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Describe the pattern and its effect, then help redirect to the group’s purpose.","why":"This addresses the behavior without defining the person by a negative role label.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Tell the member they are an individual-role person permanently.","why":"A role describes a contribution or pattern, not a fixed identity.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Let the unrelated dispute occupy every remaining turn.","why":"This leaves the shared task and other members’ needs displaced.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-roles:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Purpose → approach → evidence and fit","cards":[{"title":"Education","text":"Teach and check knowledge or a usable skill."},{"title":"Support","text":"Share experience, connection and practical coping."},{"title":"Psychotherapy","text":"Use a therapeutic model within appropriate competence and scope."}],"caption":"Choose by the intended work, not simply the word “group.”"},"strategy":{"title":"Purpose → approach → evidence and fit","task":"Which design most directly fits?","cue":"The main goal is medication teach-back.","rule":"The method and evaluation match the knowledge goal.","trap":"Match the activity and evaluation with the stated purpose."},"id":"t-groups-modalities","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Match group purpose, theory and evidence","notes":["t-groups"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Start with the intended change. An education group teaches knowledge or skills; a support group shares experience; psychotherapy uses a therapeutic approach and requires suitable preparation and scope.","A theoretical framework guides what the leader notices and does. Cognitive-behavioral work examines thoughts and actions; psychodynamic work explores recurring relational patterns; humanistic and other approaches bring different emphases.","Match the program to the person’s needs, available evidence and leader competence. A historical treatment example or a published study protocol does not establish that a specific intervention is effective or suitable today. The reference retains all named frameworks, group types and resources."],"terms":["Therapeutic group","Group psychotherapy","Group work"],"termDefinitions":{"Therapeutic group":"Members practise a useful skill, gain support or explore a difficulty together. A shared activity becomes therapeutic when it supports psychological development or growth. Choose a purpose and measure a relevant benefit; attendance alone is insufficient.","Group psychotherapy":"A trained clinician helps members examine recurring relationship patterns and psychiatric symptoms. The relationships within the session become part of a specialized treatment. Distinguish psychotherapy from RN teaching or activity facilitation; verify preparation and scope.","Group work":"The nurse plans a session, attends to participation and checks what members gained. Useful change depends on both preparation and what happens between members. Plan, facilitate and evaluate instead of merely gathering people in a room."},"patient":"A unit wants patients to demonstrate what their prescribed medicine is for and which adverse effects require help.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-groups","title":"Match group purpose, theory and evidence","blocks":[{"title":"The framework changes what the leader listens for","lines":["Humanism (humanistic approaches) emphasizes the person’s experience and growth. Patient-centered, existential and experiential work use attentive listening and guided Socratic questions rather than imposing an answer.","Cognitive-behavioral work links thoughts and actions to concrete goals. Leaders plan practice, model skills, reinforce change and monitor results.","Psychodynamic approaches, including the chapter’s psychoanalytic and Gestalt examples, explore underlying conflict and insight through interpretation, careful challenge or enactment.","Educational work builds knowledge and coping through organized teaching, demonstrations, discussion and checks of understanding.","Systems approaches examine the person’s relationships and social context. The chapter includes Adlerian, choice/reality, feminist, family and interpersonal traditions, with attention to interaction, equality and environmental change. Leaders examine lifestyle patterns, model useful interaction and plan advocacy or activism when needed."]},{"title":"Choose the group purpose and the leader’s competence","lines":["RN-led educational groups build a specific skill or understanding; handouts, slides or video can support discussion. Medication education may be led with a pharmacist and requires checking peer advice.","Dual-diagnosis groups address a substance use disorder together with a mental disorder. Two psychiatric diagnoses without substance use do not meet that meaning; the leader needs competence in both areas.","Stress-management groups practise coping such as paced breathing, exercise, music or spiritual approaches that fit the person. Mindfulness develops present-moment awareness of thoughts, feelings and bodily responses.","Community meetings address unit information, shared concerns, patient advocacy and governance. The nurse uses current unit assessment and models appropriate interaction.","RNs may also facilitate activity, task and support groups within training and policy. These categories can overlap: an activity may include education or mutual support.","Group psychotherapy aims at symptoms and deeper patterns and requires specialized preparation and appropriate authority. PMH-APRNs and other qualified mental health professionals may provide it; ordinary RN group facilitation is not equivalent preparation."]},{"title":"Support and mutual help extend care","lines":["Match support to the person’s experience: bereavement, loss after suicide, living with cancer, veteran concerns, or patient/family needs. NAMI offers support, education and advocacy. Virtual support can improve access but does not guarantee anonymity.","Peer-led self-help or mutual-help groups share lived experience rather than replacing individualized professional treatment. Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) is the chapter’s prototype for twelve-step approaches.","Other named resources include Narcotics Anonymous (NA), Gamblers Anonymous (GA), Overeaters Anonymous (OA), Adult Children of Alcoholics (ACOA), and Al-Anon for people affected by another person’s alcohol use.","Recovery International offers a mental-health mutual-help approach. The National Mental Health Consumers’ Self-Help Clearinghouse helps people locate resources; verify a program’s current access and fit instead of assuming one group suits everyone."]},{"title":"Research examples: skills, mood and recovery","lines":["The chapter cites group CBT for social anxiety, bipolar disorder and adolescents with ADHD; the ADHD example combines CBT with pharmacotherapy. These examples support selecting an appropriate adjunct, not replacing every other treatment.","It also describes brief CBT for depressive/anxiety distress and an eight-session group interpersonal therapy approach for major depression.","For smoking cessation, the cited group behavior programs were studied against self-help or brief individual support. The comparison matters when interpreting a benefit.","For substance use disorders, the chapter discusses CBT, interpersonal, behavioral marital, modified psychodynamic, interactive, rational emotive, Gestalt and psychodrama approaches. These are treatment models, not proof that every model fits every patient."]},{"title":"Research examples: family, trauma and longer-term support","lines":["For children with marked irritability and ADHD, the chapter describes joint parent–child sessions. For early conduct problems, it cites behavioral or cognitive-behavioral parenting groups with parent and child outcomes.","For people at risk of psychosis or with early psychosis, it lists group/family CBT. The cited work includes pilot or noncontrolled designs; it is not equivalent to a large randomized trial.","Seeking Safety is the chapter’s example for co-occurring PTSD and substance use problems. Reminiscence groups are discussed for neurocognitive disorders, and dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) for borderline personality disorder.","Evidence sources in the chapter range from trials and meta-analyses to reviews and practice guidance. Verify current evidence, the actual population and clinician competence before selecting a program; a historical table is not an automatic treatment order."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"AGPA: group psychotherapy practice guidance","url":"https://agpa.org/guidelines-ethics/practice-guidelines-for-group-psychotherapy-2/"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"The main goal is medication teach-back.","task":"Which design most directly fits?","hint":"Match the activity and evaluation with the stated purpose.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A session devoted only to interpreting unconscious conflicts.","why":"That may serve a different psychotherapy purpose but does not directly meet the stated education goal.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"A structured education session with an opportunity to demonstrate understanding.","why":"The method and evaluation match the knowledge goal.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"Attendance without any check of understanding.","why":"Attendance does not establish that the patient can use the information.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-modalities:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A paper describes a protocol for a planned online group trial.","task":"What can the nurse conclude from the protocol alone?","hint":"Separate a research plan from research findings.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"The intervention has already been proven effective.","why":"A planned method is not a completed result.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Every patient should be enrolled regardless of need.","why":"Fit, evidence, consent and access still require assessment.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"It describes how an intervention will be tested; outcomes still need evidence.","why":"A protocol explains a proposed study rather than demonstrating benefit.","correct":true}],"id":"t-groups-modalities:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Style should serve the work","cards":[{"title":"Directive","text":"Provide needed structure and clear direction."},{"title":"Democratic","text":"Invite shared work while holding the therapeutic frame."},{"title":"Little direction","text":"Leaves more responsibility with members; fit and safety still matter."}],"caption":"A style is useful only when it meets the group’s actual needs."},"strategy":{"title":"Style should serve the work","task":"Which adjustment supports democratic leadership?","cue":"Members are able to participate safely and have ideas to offer.","rule":"The leader supports shared problem solving without abandoning responsibility for the frame.","trap":"Shared problem solving and professional responsibility can occur together."},"id":"t-groups-leadership","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Choose a leadership style and use supervision","notes":["t-groups"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Choose a leadership style for the task, phase and current needs. Democratic leadership invites shared problem solving while maintaining the frame. More directive structure can be needed for a focused task; little direction is a poor fit when containment is needed.","Use active listening, clarification, reflection, respectful challenge and summaries to support members’ work. Keep feedback specific to observed behavior and its effect rather than claiming to know everyone’s feelings.","Supervision and coleadership help examine the leader’s reactions, boundaries and decisions. Agree on roles, debrief differences and notice cultural assumptions and barriers to participation. Inclusion does not require forced personal disclosure."],"terms":["Group work","Feedback","Group norms"],"termDefinitions":{"Group work":"The nurse plans a session, attends to participation and checks what members gained. Useful change depends on both preparation and what happens between members. Plan, facilitate and evaluate instead of merely gathering people in a room.","Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone.","Group norms":"Members expect a turn to speak and arrive before the session begins. Shared expectations can support safety or quietly pressure members to agree. Clarify helpful expectations and address rules that silence disagreement."},"patient":"A group is ready to solve a shared problem, but the leader has begun answering every question before members can respond.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-groups","title":"Choose a leadership style and use supervision","blocks":[{"title":"Leadership style follows the group’s needs","lines":["Autocratic: the leader sets the direction and retains control. This may help a short, tightly structured unit agenda, but limits member-led exchange.","Democratic: the leader invites shared problem solving and substantial interaction while maintaining the therapeutic frame.","Laissez-faire: members have substantial freedom and little direction. A safe creative activity may tolerate this; low structure is a poor answer when containment or skill teaching is needed.","A preferred personal style is not a reason to ignore the task, phase or acuity. Flexible leadership still includes responsibility for safety."]},{"title":"Communication that makes a group workable","lines":["Active listening shows sustained attention; questions invite a useful detail; clarification checks the meaning of an unclear statement.","Giving information supplies accurate facts. In medication teaching, the chapter uses antidepressant benefit taking four weeks or longer as an example; timing varies and the prescribed plan still guides care.","Confrontation respectfully notices a mismatch between words and observed behavior. It is a therapeutic challenge, not accusation or ridicule.","Empathy acknowledges the experience; reflection returns a feeling or observation for exploration; support recognizes a specific effort.","Summarizing links the work already done to what comes next. Keep members’ participation central instead of turning every intervention into a long lecture."]},{"title":"Supervision, coleadership and inclusion","lines":["Use clinical supervision to examine facilitation, group dynamics, boundaries and the leader’s own reactions. Transference and countertransference can occur with groups as well as individual care.","An experienced colleague or qualified APRN supervisor can help a new leader develop skill. Supervisors protect professional confidentiality; leaders who provide psychotherapy also seek supervision.","Coleaders can model different useful responses, support a less experienced facilitator and debrief immediately. They may elicit different member reactions and offer support with strong countertransference.","A poor relationship between coleaders can undermine the group. Agree on roles and discuss differences openly rather than forming competing alliances.","Attend to racial, ethnic, economic, sexual-orientation and other differences. Explore assumptions respectfully and remove barriers that marginalize members.","Support LGBTQ inclusion. Offering pronouns can open participation, without forcing personal disclosure. The chapter’s LGBTQ Responsive Model for Supervision of Group Work helps leaders develop relevant awareness, knowledge and skills."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"AGPA: group psychotherapy practice guidance","url":"https://agpa.org/guidelines-ethics/practice-guidelines-for-group-psychotherapy-2/"},{"title":"NIMH: antidepressant response takes time","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/depression"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"Members are able to participate safely and have ideas to offer.","task":"Which adjustment supports democratic leadership?","hint":"Shared problem solving and professional responsibility can occur together.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Invite member proposals, clarify them and help the group compare options.","why":"The leader supports shared problem solving without abandoning responsibility for the frame.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Stop providing any structure or boundaries.","why":"Democratic leadership does not mean absence of leadership.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Continue making every decision for the members.","why":"This retains control rather than building shared work.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-leadership:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A leader notices a strong urge to rescue one member and overlook an agreed limit.","task":"What is the most useful professional response?","hint":"Examine the reaction through the appropriate professional support.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Hide the reaction and quietly give that member special rules.","why":"Unexamined exceptions can undermine fairness and boundaries.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Use supervision to examine the reaction and plan a consistent response.","why":"Supervision can help the leader understand personal reactions and protect therapeutic boundaries.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"Tell the whole group private details about the leader’s personal history.","why":"Personal disclosure is not a substitute for appropriate supervision and can shift the focus away from members.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-leadership:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Explain limits, prepare access and respond to risk","cards":[{"title":"Consent","text":"Explain privacy limits before sensitive disclosure."},{"title":"Remote plan","text":"Know location, contacts and the backup response."},{"title":"Immediate safety","text":"Pause the agenda when assessment or protection is needed."}],"caption":"A privacy agreement and a safety plan do different jobs."},"strategy":{"title":"Explain limits, prepare access and respond to risk","task":"Which reply is accurate?","cue":"The participant asks, “Can you promise nobody here will repeat this?”","rule":"This supports informed participation without making an impossible promise.","trap":"Distinguish the nurse’s duty from control over other members."},"id":"t-groups-safety","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Protect consent, confidentiality and immediate safety","notes":["t-groups"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Before participation, explain the purpose, expectations and confidentiality limits. The nurse has professional duties; an agreement cannot guarantee that every peer will protect a disclosure.","Online groups need attention to private space, recording, unseen listeners, each member’s location, emergency contacts and a connection-failure plan. A video connection does not remove licensure or local-response requirements.","A safety concern can interrupt the planned group task. Assess a new statement about self-harm, obtain appropriate support and protect members from an immediate threat. Base restrictions on actual behavior and assessed risk, not on quietness, diagnosis or limited resources alone."],"terms":["Group psychotherapy","Group norms","Group process"],"termDefinitions":{"Group psychotherapy":"A trained clinician helps members examine recurring relationship patterns and psychiatric symptoms. The relationships within the session become part of a specialized treatment. Distinguish psychotherapy from RN teaching or activity facilitation; verify preparation and scope.","Group norms":"Members expect a turn to speak and arrive before the session begins. Shared expectations can support safety or quietly pressure members to agree. Clarify helpful expectations and address rules that silence disagreement.","Group process":"People interrupt one speaker but listen closely to another discussing the same topic. Process concerns how members relate, including tone, gestures, silence and patterns of participation. Notice who responds to whom and intervene when the interaction blocks the work."},"patient":"Before an online group, a participant asks whether it is safe to discuss something they do not want other members to repeat.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-groups","title":"Protect consent, confidentiality and immediate safety","blocks":[{"title":"Consent and privacy: a promise has limits","lines":["Before participation, explain the purpose, likely benefits, risks, roles and confidentiality limits. Clarify attendance or leaving expectations and rules for contact outside the group.","The nurse has professional confidentiality duties. Peers are not bound by that professional nursing code, and legal protections vary. Never guarantee that another member will keep a disclosure private.","Discuss exceptions required by law and safety policy before sensitive disclosure. An agreement helps set expectations; it cannot eliminate every risk of a breach.","Protect identities in notes, teaching and any recording. Using a recording to review interaction requires appropriate consent and safeguards; it does not justify members casually filming a session.","Use training, credentialing, scope standards and current evidence to select a suitable approach. Removal is generally a last resort after attempts to address non-dangerous difficulties or repeated agreement violations."]},{"title":"Virtual groups: preserve the frame across the screen","lines":["Online options expanded during COVID-19 and can address isolation, grief, anxiety or other needs. They can improve access without being interchangeable with every in-person group.","A flat image can hide nonverbal cues. Leaders have less control over seating, interruptions and who else is nearby, and may find sustained engagement harder.","Use appropriate privacy/security measures, a suitable platform and HIPAA protections where applicable. Discuss unseen listeners, recording, device use and a private joining space.","Confirm each member’s location and the clinician’s authority to provide care there. Cross-state practice is not automatically permitted simply because the meeting is online.","Agree on emergency contacts, a local response plan and a connection-failure backup. Present remote support as helpful access, not a guarantee of secrecy or safety.","The chapter also mentions a solution-focused brief online group study protocol for adolescent anxiety. A protocol describes a planned test; it does not by itself demonstrate effectiveness."]},{"title":"When group work must give way to immediate safety","lines":["New suicidal thoughts require direct risk assessment and appropriate additional support. Calm or quiet behavior does not establish safety, and group reassurance is not a substitute for assessment.","Active paranoid beliefs, major disorganization or acute mania can make participation harder. Seek experienced support and individualize fit; a manic patient unable to attend to the group needs stabilization and a more suitable setting.","If someone threatens or attacks another member, protect the group, summon help and follow the safety protocol. Temporary removal may be necessary immediately; do not wait to finish the planned exercise.","A raised voice or limited resources alone does not establish dangerousness. Base restrictions on assessed behavior, risk and the needs of this particular group."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"AGPA: group psychotherapy practice guidance","url":"https://agpa.org/guidelines-ethics/practice-guidelines-for-group-psychotherapy-2/"},{"title":"HHS: group teletherapy","url":"https://telehealth.hhs.gov/providers/best-practice-guides/telehealth-for-behavioral-health/group-teletherapy"},{"title":"HHS: licensure for behavioral health across jurisdictions","url":"https://telehealth.hhs.gov/licensure/licensure-for-behavioral-health"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"The participant asks, “Can you promise nobody here will repeat this?”","task":"Which reply is accurate?","hint":"Distinguish the nurse’s duty from control over other members.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Promise absolute secrecy because all members signed an agreement.","why":"An agreement sets expectations but cannot guarantee every peer’s behavior.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Explain professional duties and group agreements, including the limits of what can be guaranteed.","why":"This supports informed participation without making an impossible promise.","correct":true},{"id":"o2","text":"Avoid the question until after the disclosure.","why":"The person needs this information before deciding what to share.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-safety:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"During the session, a participant reports a current intention to harm themselves after the call.","task":"Which action takes priority?","hint":"Use the safety plan when the person’s actual risk changes.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Finish the group agenda before responding.","why":"The agenda cannot take priority over a stated immediate safety concern.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"Assume the group’s reassurance is enough.","why":"Peer support does not replace a direct risk assessment and needed protective help.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"Pause, assess immediate risk and activate the location-appropriate safety response and additional support.","why":"The concern requires direct assessment and the prepared emergency pathway.","correct":true}],"id":"t-groups-safety:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Observe participation → invite a useful change → evaluate","cards":[{"title":"Observe","text":"Describe the behavior and its effect without a motive label."},{"title":"Invite","text":"Share airtime, offer a manageable turn and keep limits."},{"title":"Evaluate","text":"Ask for the skill or understanding the group aimed to build."}],"caption":"Respectful inclusion and clear evaluation work together."},"strategy":{"title":"Observe participation → invite a useful change → evaluate","task":"Which response best supports inclusion?","cue":"The quiet member looks thoughtful and has not declined participation.","rule":"This opens participation while respecting readiness and the time needed to respond.","trap":"Invite participation without treating silence as proof of refusal."},"id":"t-groups-participation","ch":"34","chapters":["34"],"title":"Respond to difficult participation and evaluate benefit","notes":["t-groups"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 34; original site-authored teaching from the supplied chapter. Focused parts share the complete map topic.","why":["Protect room for participation without humiliating a member. When one person dominates, describe the pattern, remind the group of shared time and invite other voices. Explore the need behind the pattern without assuming a motive.","Silence can reflect thought, uncertainty or concerns about safety. Offer a manageable invitation and time to respond; do not force sensitive disclosure. With anger, acknowledge feeling while maintaining behavioral limits.","Evaluate the intended change. Medication education needs demonstrated understanding; therapy goals may concern coping, symptoms or relationships. Attendance and a pleasant session can be useful observations, but they do not establish the goal was met."],"terms":["Feedback","Group process","Individual roles"],"termDefinitions":{"Feedback":"A member explains that an interruption made it difficult to finish a thought. Others can describe an interpersonal effect that the speaker did not notice. Make feedback specific and owned: describe the behavior and its effect without speaking for everyone.","Group process":"People interrupt one speaker but listen closely to another discussing the same topic. Process concerns how members relate, including tone, gestures, silence and patterns of participation. Notice who responds to whom and intervene when the interaction blocks the work.","Individual roles":"One person repeatedly seeks applause while the agreed topic is left behind. Personal needs can displace the shared task. This describes behavior, not a permanent identity. Describe the effect, set respectful limits and explore the underlying need."},"patient":"A medication group includes one member who answers every prompt and another who has not spoken yet.","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-groups","title":"Respond to difficult participation and evaluate benefit","blocks":[{"title":"A member takes nearly every turn","lines":["Begin with a reminder about shared speaking time and invite other contributions. Help members notice participation patterns rather than humiliating one person.","If the pattern persists, describe what you observed and its effect in your own words. Avoid claiming to know how every other member feels.","A private conversation can explore anxiety or another need behind the talkativeness. Agree on a practical limit; the chapter gives two or three contributions as an example, not a universal prescription.","When the group can manage it safely, invite respectful feedback about the pattern so the member can compare intended and actual interpersonal effects."]},{"title":"Anger, disruption and rejected help","lines":["Listen without joining the attack. Describe behavior, maintain limits and consider a private check-in about what is driving the anger.","Matter-of-fact empathy can support connection without excusing harmful conduct. Feeling hurt or vulnerable may sit behind angry self-protection; do not assume a motive without exploring it.","Help members use a specific I-statement about an effect instead of assigning blame. Feelings can be acknowledged while behavior remains subject to limits.","When someone struggles to name emotion, invite attention to bodily sensations, current reactions and how another member may experience the interaction.","For repeated complaints followed by rejection of every suggestion, explore the interaction and the person’s own goals instead of escalating the advice-giving. Use supervision when the pattern exceeds the leader’s skill.","Peer feedback, or an appropriately consented session review, may reveal self-focused patterns. Immediate threats or violence move the priority to the safety response above."]},{"title":"Silence needs curiosity, time and an invitation","lines":["A silent member may be assessing safety, thinking carefully or doubting their ability to contribute. Silence alone does not prove refusal or lack of interest.","Allow time to organize a response and offer to return to the person. A planned round with a manageable prompt can give everyone a predictable opening.","Explore barriers without demanding sensitive disclosure. Participation enables feedback and lets others benefit from the member’s perspective.","Consider the effect on the whole group, including uncertainty about what the quiet member thinks. Pair inclusion with respect for readiness."]},{"title":"Choose and measure outcomes that match the group","lines":["In medication education, ask for demonstrated knowledge: medication purpose, three important adverse effects, the prescribed dose and timing, and significant drug–drug or drug–food interactions.","Teach-back can reveal a gap; attendance, accepting a medication’s price or saying the group was pleasant does not establish medication knowledge. The chapter’s review example includes identifying two interactions.","In therapy, assess the intended change in insight, behavior and symptoms. For alcohol-related treatment, connect use to consequences and evaluate abstinence or reduction in risky use as appropriate to the plan.","Use a consistent symptom scale when symptom change is the goal. Combine objective measures with members’ accounts of helpful and unhelpful features.","Reflect on your own group behavior: which actions advance the task, sustain inclusion or displace the work? Examine norms, preparation and the effect of leadership as part of continued learning."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"AGPA: group psychotherapy practice guidance","url":"https://agpa.org/guidelines-ethics/practice-guidelines-for-group-psychotherapy-2/"}],"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"The quiet member looks thoughtful and has not declined participation.","task":"Which response best supports inclusion?","hint":"Invite participation without treating silence as proof of refusal.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Offer a simple invitation, allow time and ask whether to return to them later.","why":"This opens participation while respecting readiness and the time needed to respond.","correct":true},{"id":"o1","text":"Demand a private trauma story to prove engagement.","why":"Forced disclosure can damage safety and is not required to show participation.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"State that silence proves the group is useless to them.","why":"Silence alone does not establish the person’s experience or benefit.","correct":false}],"id":"t-groups-participation:active:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The group’s goal was safer medication use.","task":"Which result best shows the goal was met?","hint":"Measure the intended skill or knowledge, not only satisfaction or attendance.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"The member says the room was comfortable.","why":"Comfort is relevant to experience but does not demonstrate medication knowledge.","correct":false},{"id":"o1","text":"The member attended every meeting.","why":"Attendance does not show the person can apply the teaching.","correct":false},{"id":"o2","text":"The member explains the prescribed purpose and schedule, important adverse effects and relevant interactions.","why":"Teach-back directly checks the knowledge the group was intended to build.","correct":true}],"id":"t-groups-participation:active:1"}],"caseId":"","active":true,"revision":"mh-active-20261109"},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"First, rule out a physical cause (MI, PE, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia, stimulants, withdrawal). Then place the anxiety on the mild → moderate → severe → panic scale, using her perceptual field, her ability to problem-solve and her physical signs. Look for the pattern: panic attacks, anticipatory worry and avoidance."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"During the attack the priority is safety and physical stability: rule out the dangerous cause, stay with her, and bring her anxiety down from panic. After the attack: ineffective coping, avoidance and sleep. Teaching waits until her anxiety is mild to moderate."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Check vital signs and her ability to follow directions as the attack subsides. Over time: fewer attacks and ED visits, use of breathing skills when an attack begins, return to avoided places (highway driving), better sleep, and safe medication use (no mixing benzodiazepines with alcohol)."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Sudden chest tightness, racing heart and fear of dying with a normal ECG; months of worry.","rule":"During the attack the priority is safety and physical stability: rule out the dangerous cause, stay with her, and bring her anxiety down from panic. After the attack: ineffective coping, avoidance and sleep. Teaching waits until her anxiety is mild to moderate.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-anxiety","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Panic attack and generalized anxiety","notes":["c-anxiety"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 15; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: With the cardiac work-up negative, the cues fit panic disorder (recurrent unexpected attacks plus worry and avoidance) with generalized anxiety between attacks. Hyperventilation explains the tingling. She is at the panic level now and will come down to moderate with calm support.","Priority: During the attack the priority is safety and physical stability: rule out the dangerous cause, stay with her, and bring her anxiety down from panic. After the attack: ineffective coping, avoidance and sleep. Teaching waits until her anxiety is mild to moderate.","Act: Stay with her, keep a calm manner and a low, slow voice, move to a quiet space, and use short, simple statements with repetition. Guide slow breathing. Give a PRN as ordered. Once calmer: name the anxiety, explore triggers, teach relaxation and CBT skills, and explain the medications.","Reassess: Check vital signs and her ability to follow directions as the attack subsides. Over time: fewer attacks and ED visits, use of breathing skills when an attack begins, return to avoided places (highway driving), better sleep, and safe medication use (no mixing benzodiazepines with alcohol)."],"terms":["Panic attack","Hyperventilation and tingling","Narrowed perceptual field","Anticipatory anxiety and avoidance","Excessive worry (GAD)","sertraline","escitalopram","venlafaxine","diazepam","lorazepam","clonazepam","alprazolam","paroxetine","duloxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Panic attack":"Sudden surge of terror that peaks within minutes: pounding heart, chest pain, shortness of breath, choking, trembling, sweating, dizziness, tingling, fear of dying or “going crazy.” A false alarm of the fight-or-flight system: the amygdala fires and the sympathetic nervous system floods the body with adrenaline. Usually peaks in about 10 minutes and resolves in under 30. Stay with her, speak calmly and simply, and guide her breathing. Never assume a panic attack until a cardiac cause is ruled out.","Hyperventilation and tingling":"Fast, shallow breathing; numb, tingling fingers and lips; lightheadedness. Overbreathing blows off CO₂ → respiratory alkalosis. Alkalosis lowers ionized calcium, so nerves fire more easily (tingling) and blood vessels in the brain constrict (dizziness). Coach slow breathing (in through the nose, longer breath out). The symptoms ease as CO₂ normalizes.","Narrowed perceptual field":"She can only focus on her heartbeat and can’t follow your instructions about the ECG. As anxiety rises, attention narrows onto the threat and filters out everything else. Tells you the level. At severe or panic anxiety, she can’t problem-solve or learn, so give short, simple directions and save the teaching for later.","Anticipatory anxiety and avoidance":"Has stopped driving on the highway in case an attack happens there. The fear of the next attack becomes its own problem; avoiding places brings relief, which reinforces the avoidance. A sign of panic disorder, and of agoraphobia if avoidance spreads to places where escape would be hard.","Excessive worry (GAD)":"Worries about many things most days, can’t stop, with muscle tension, fatigue, irritability and poor sleep. Chronic overactivation of the stress response; worry is used (unsuccessfully) to feel in control. Generalized anxiety disorder: worry most days for at least 6 months, with at least 3 physical or cognitive symptoms."},"patient":"31-year-old in the ED for the third time this month with sudden chest tightness, pounding heart, shaking, numb fingers and a sense she is about to die. Troponin and ECG are normal. Between attacks she worries constantly about work, money and her kids, sleeps poorly and has stopped driving on the highway.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-anxiety","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["First, rule out a physical cause (MI, PE, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia, stimulants, withdrawal). Then place the anxiety on the mild → moderate → severe → panic scale, using her perceptual field, her ability to problem-solve and her physical signs. Look for the pattern: panic attacks, anticipatory worry and avoidance."]},{"title":"The four levels of anxiety","lines":["Mild: perceptual field heightened; alert, can learn and solve problems; restless, fidgety. Can be useful.","Moderate: field narrowed (selective inattention); can still solve problems with help; faster heart rate and breathing, voice tremor.","Severe: field greatly reduced, fixed on details; cannot solve problems; headache, nausea, dizziness, trembling; needs direction.","Panic: unable to attend to the environment; disorganized, may feel unreal or have hallucinations; may run, freeze or strike out; danger to self and others.","The level tells you what the patient can take in. At mild to moderate you can teach and problem-solve together; at severe to panic you give safety, calm and simple directions."]},{"title":"Anxiety disorders to know","lines":["Panic disorder: recurrent unexpected attacks plus 1 month of worry about attacks or changed behavior.","Agoraphobia: fear of places where escape is hard (crowds, transit, outside the home).","Generalized anxiety disorder: excessive worry for 6 months or more.","Specific phobia: an irrational fear of an object or situation (heights, needles, blood).","Social anxiety disorder: fear of being judged or embarrassed in social or performance situations.","Separation anxiety disorder: excessive fear of separation from an attachment figure (most common in children)."]},{"title":"Medical causes to rule out","lines":["Cardiac (MI, dysrhythmia), PE, asthma or COPD (hypoxia).","Hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia, pheochromocytoma.","Caffeine, stimulants, decongestants; withdrawal from alcohol or benzodiazepines."]}]},{"id":"c-anxiety","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["With the cardiac work-up negative, the cues fit panic disorder (recurrent unexpected attacks plus worry and avoidance) with generalized anxiety between attacks. Hyperventilation explains the tingling. She is at the panic level now and will come down to moderate with calm support."]},{"title":"Why the body feels like it’s dying","lines":["Adrenaline → tachycardia, palpitations, chest tightness, sweating, tremor.","Hyperventilation → low CO₂ → tingling, dizziness, air hunger.","Blood shunted to the muscles → cold hands, nausea, a “butterflies” feeling.","The brain reads these sensations as danger → more adrenaline (the panic cycle)."]},{"title":"Causes","lines":["Biologic: an overactive amygdala and fear circuit; low GABA and serotonin activity; genetics.","Learned: conditioning (a place becomes linked with an attack).","Cognitive: misreading body sensations as catastrophic (“my heart is racing, I’m having a heart attack”).","Stress and trauma; caffeine and substances."]}]},{"id":"c-anxiety","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["During the attack the priority is safety and physical stability: rule out the dangerous cause, stay with her, and bring her anxiety down from panic. After the attack: ineffective coping, avoidance and sleep. Teaching waits until her anxiety is mild to moderate."]},{"title":"Match the action to the level","lines":["Severe/panic: stay, calm, quiet room, short sentences, breathing, PRN medication; meet physical needs. Don’t teach yet.","Mild/moderate: help her name the anxiety, talk about it, problem-solve, link triggers to feelings, and learn new skills."]}]},{"id":"c-anxiety","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Short term: her anxiety falls to mild or moderate (HR and RR return to normal, she can follow directions). Longer term: she can recognize early signs, uses relaxation or breathing to stop the escalation, gradually re-enters avoided situations, sleeps 6–8 hours, and takes medication safely."]}]},{"id":"c-anxiety","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Stay with her, keep a calm manner and a low, slow voice, move to a quiet space, and use short, simple statements with repetition. Guide slow breathing. Give a PRN as ordered. Once calmer: name the anxiety, explore triggers, teach relaxation and CBT skills, and explain the medications."]},{"title":"Severe to panic: what the nurse does","lines":["Stay with the patient; never leave someone at panic level alone.","Stay calm yourself: anxiety is contagious, and so is calm.","Reduce stimuli: a quiet room, dim lights, fewer people.","Short, simple sentences, repeated: “You’re safe. I’m staying with you. Breathe with me.”","Low-pitched voice, slow pace.","Reinforce reality if she has distortions.","Meet physical needs (fluids, rest); PRN medication; seclusion only if she is dangerous."]},{"title":"Mild to moderate: what the nurse does","lines":["Help her recognize it: “You seem uneasy. Are you comfortable right now?”","Encourage her to talk about feelings and concerns; don’t change the subject or give advice.","Ask what she was thinking right before it started.","Problem-solve together; role-play new responses.","Explore what has helped before.","Offer physical outlets: walking, exercise."]},{"title":"Relaxation skills to teach","lines":["Diaphragmatic breathing: slow inhale, longer exhale.","Progressive muscle relaxation: tense and release each muscle group.","Guided imagery, mindfulness, grounding (5-4-3-2-1).","Limit caffeine, nicotine and alcohol; regular exercise and sleep."]},{"title":"Therapies","lines":["CBT (first line): challenge catastrophic thoughts; interoceptive exposure (bringing on the sensations safely).","Exposure therapy and systematic desensitization for phobias and agoraphobia: graded steps, from least to most frightening, paired with relaxation.","Flooding: rapid, intense exposure (only with a therapist)."]},{"title":"Medications","lines":["SSRIs and SNRIs (escitalopram, sertraline, paroxetine, venlafaxine, duloxetine): first line for long-term control (no misuse risk, and they treat co-occurring depression); antianxiety effect takes 4–8 weeks.","Benzodiazepines (lorazepam, alprazolam, clonazepam, diazepam): fast relief for short-term or acute use. Sedation, falls in older adults, respiratory depression with alcohol or opioids, dependence; never stop abruptly (withdrawal seizures). Antidote for overdose: flumazenil.","Buspirone: for GAD; no sedation or dependence, but takes 2–4 weeks, so it is not PRN.","Hydroxyzine: a nonaddictive antihistamine option. Propranolol for performance anxiety (blocks tremor and tachycardia).","Benzodiazepines enhance GABA, the brain’s main brake, so they work in minutes. That same effect adds to alcohol and opioids and builds tolerance."]}]},{"id":"c-anxiety","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Check vital signs and her ability to follow directions as the attack subsides. Over time: fewer attacks and ED visits, use of breathing skills when an attack begins, return to avoided places (highway driving), better sleep, and safe medication use (no mixing benzodiazepines with alcohol)."]},{"title":"Teaching on discharge","lines":["Panic attacks feel dangerous but are not physically harmful once the heart has been checked.","Use the breathing skill at the first sign.","Take the SSRI daily even when well; it takes weeks.","Benzodiazepines: no alcohol, no driving until she knows the effect, don’t stop suddenly.","Follow up with outpatient CBT."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-anxiety:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with GAD is prescribed buspirone. Which statement shows understanding?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“I’ll take it when I feel a panic attack coming on.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Buspirone takes 2–4 weeks, is not PRN, and has no dependence or sedation, unlike benzodiazepines."},{"id":"o1","text":"“It may take 2 to 4 weeks before I notice it working.”","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Buspirone takes 2–4 weeks, is not PRN, and has no dependence or sedation, unlike benzodiazepines."},{"id":"o2","text":"“I can’t stop it suddenly or I’ll have seizures.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Buspirone takes 2–4 weeks, is not PRN, and has no dependence or sedation, unlike benzodiazepines."},{"id":"o3","text":"“It will make me very sleepy.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Buspirone takes 2–4 weeks, is not PRN, and has no dependence or sedation, unlike benzodiazepines."}]},{"id":"c-anxiety:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"31-year-old in the ED for the third time this month with sudden chest tightness, pounding heart, shaking, numb fingers and a sense she is about to die. Troponin and ECG are normal. Between attacks she worries constantly about work, money and her kids, sleeps poorly and has stopped driving on the highway.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"During the attack the priority is safety and physical stability: rule out the dangerous cause, stay with her, and bring her anxiety down from panic. After the attack: ineffective coping, avoidance and sleep. Teaching waits until her anxiety is mild to moderate.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-anxiety:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"31-year-old in the ED for the third time this month with sudden chest tightness, pounding heart, shaking, numb fingers and a sense she is about to die. Troponin and ECG are normal. Between attacks she worries constantly about work, money and her kids, sleeps poorly and has stopped driving on the highway.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Stay with her, keep a calm manner and a low, slow voice, move to a quiet space, and use short, simple statements with repetition. Guide slow breathing. Give a PRN as ordered. Once calmer: name the anxiety, explore triggers, teach relaxation and CBT skills, and explain the medications. Check vital signs and her ability to follow directions as the attack subsides. Over time: fewer attacks and ED visits, use of breathing skills when an attack begins, return to avoided places (highway driving), better sleep, and safe medication use (no mixing benzodiazepines with alcohol).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Identify obsessions (unwanted, intrusive thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety) and compulsions (repetitive behaviors or mental acts done to reduce that anxiety), how much time they take (more than 1 hour a day), how they impair her function, and the physical damage they cause (skin, nutrition, sleep, elimination)."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priorities: impaired skin integrity and infection risk, then nutrition, sleep and elimination (rituals crowd them out), then anxiety and ineffective coping, then role performance (school, relationships)."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Measure time spent on rituals each day, the condition of her skin, intake and weight, sleep, attendance at therapy and classes, and her anxiety level when rituals are delayed."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Intrusive fear of contamination; washes hands 60 times a day until they crack and bleed.","rule":"Priorities: impaired skin integrity and infection risk, then nutrition, sleep and elimination (rituals crowd them out), then anxiety and ineffective coping, then role performance (school, relationships).","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-ocd","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","notes":["c-ocd"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 15; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: The cues fit OCD with contamination obsessions and washing compulsions: more than an hour a day, causing distress and impairment, with good insight. The washing has caused skin breakdown with possible infection, and the time spent on rituals is crowding out eating, sleep and school.","Priority: Priorities: impaired skin integrity and infection risk, then nutrition, sleep and elimination (rituals crowd them out), then anxiety and ineffective coping, then role performance (school, relationships).","Act: Don’t interrupt rituals abruptly or punish them — at first, build time for them into a structured daily schedule. Then gradually limit them with her agreement. Meet physical needs, protect her skin, use a nonjudgmental approach, teach relaxation, and support exposure and response prevention and the SSRI.","Reassess: Measure time spent on rituals each day, the condition of her skin, intake and weight, sleep, attendance at therapy and classes, and her anxiety level when rituals are delayed."],"terms":["Obsession","Compulsion","Fissured, bleeding skin","Insight","sertraline","fluoxetine","paroxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Obsession":"A constant, unwanted fear that germs on her hands will make her family sick. An intrusive thought the person recognizes as coming from their own mind (unlike a delusion), but can’t stop, and it creates intense anxiety. Usually ego-dystonic: the person knows it is excessive and is distressed by it. Ask about the content without judgment.","Compulsion":"Washing hands in a fixed sequence 60 times a day; checking the stove 20 times. The ritual relieves the anxiety briefly. Relief is negative reinforcement, so the ritual gets stronger each time. Interrupting it abruptly sends anxiety soaring, so you don’t stop the ritual cold. Limits are set gradually, with her involved.","Fissured, bleeding skin":"Cracked, raw hands; redness and warmth over the knuckles. Repeated washing strips the skin’s protective oils, so it cracks, and bacteria can get in. A physical priority: skin integrity and infection. Lotion, gloves at night and a provider assessment for cellulitis.","Insight":"“I know it sounds crazy, but I can’t stop.” Most people with OCD have good or fair insight; some have poor insight or none. Good insight helps her engage in therapy; use it to set goals together."},"patient":"22-year-old college student admitted because handwashing now takes 6 hours a day. Her hands are red, cracked and bleeding. She knows the fear of germs is excessive but says, “If I don’t wash, something terrible will happen to my family.” She has missed her classes for a month and rarely eats in the dining hall.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-ocd","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Identify obsessions (unwanted, intrusive thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety) and compulsions (repetitive behaviors or mental acts done to reduce that anxiety), how much time they take (more than 1 hour a day), how they impair her function, and the physical damage they cause (skin, nutrition, sleep, elimination)."]},{"title":"Common obsession → compulsion pairs","lines":["Contamination → washing, cleaning.","Doubt or harm (“Did I leave the stove on?”) → checking.","Symmetry or order → arranging, counting, repeating.","Unwanted violent, sexual or religious thoughts → praying, reassurance-seeking, mental rituals."]},{"title":"OCD-related disorders","lines":["Body dysmorphic disorder: preoccupation with a perceived flaw in appearance; mirror-checking, seeking cosmetic surgery.","Hoarding disorder: persistent difficulty discarding possessions; clutter makes the home unsafe (fire, falls).","Trichotillomania: recurrent hair pulling.","Excoriation (skin-picking) disorder."]}]},{"id":"c-ocd","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The cues fit OCD with contamination obsessions and washing compulsions: more than an hour a day, causing distress and impairment, with good insight. The washing has caused skin breakdown with possible infection, and the time spent on rituals is crowding out eating, sleep and school."]},{"title":"Why it happens","lines":["Biologic: overactivity in the cortico-striatal-thalamic loop (the brain’s error-detection circuit keeps signaling “something’s wrong”); low serotonin activity (why SSRIs help); genetics.","Learned: the ritual reduces anxiety, which reinforces the ritual.","In children, a sudden onset after a strep infection can be PANDAS."]},{"title":"Defense mechanisms in OCD","lines":["Undoing: a ritual to “cancel out” an unacceptable thought.","Reaction formation and isolation of affect are also described."]}]},{"id":"c-ocd","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priorities: impaired skin integrity and infection risk, then nutrition, sleep and elimination (rituals crowd them out), then anxiety and ineffective coping, then role performance (school, relationships)."]},{"title":"Physical needs come first","lines":["Skin: rituals of washing → cracked skin → infection.","Nutrition: no time to eat, or fear of contaminated food.","Elimination: some patients put off voiding or defecating (UTI, constipation).","Sleep: rituals at bedtime can take hours."]}]},{"id":"c-ocd","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: skin heals without infection; she eats and sleeps on schedule; she reduces time spent on rituals step by step (for example from 6 hours to 4, then less); she uses anxiety-reduction skills; and she returns to classes."]}]},{"id":"c-ocd","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Don’t interrupt rituals abruptly or punish them — at first, build time for them into a structured daily schedule. Then gradually limit them with her agreement. Meet physical needs, protect her skin, use a nonjudgmental approach, teach relaxation, and support exposure and response prevention and the SSRI."]},{"title":"Nursing interventions","lines":["Allow time for rituals at first, and plan the day around them so meals and care still happen.","Gradually limit the time with her (a set number of washes, a timer), and praise each reduction.","Structure: a predictable schedule; fewer decisions.","Skin care: gentle soap, lotion after each wash, cotton gloves at night; watch for infection.","Support her during exposure and response prevention sessions; sit with her through the anxiety.","Encourage her to talk about feelings rather than rituals; avoid giving repeated reassurance (reassurance becomes a compulsion).","Recognize your own frustration: the rituals are not a choice.","Stopping a ritual abruptly without another way to manage the anxiety can push her to severe or panic anxiety."]},{"title":"Exposure and response prevention (ERP)","lines":["First-line behavioral therapy.","She is exposed to the trigger (touching a doorknob) and prevented from doing the ritual, while the anxiety rises, peaks and falls on its own.","Repeated practice teaches the brain that the feared outcome doesn’t happen."]},{"title":"Medications","lines":["SSRIs (fluoxetine, fluvoxamine, sertraline, paroxetine) at higher doses, taking 8–12 weeks for full effect.","Clomipramine (a TCA): effective, but anticholinergic and lethal in overdose.","Antipsychotics as add-ons in resistant cases."]}]},{"id":"c-ocd","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Measure time spent on rituals each day, the condition of her skin, intake and weight, sleep, attendance at therapy and classes, and her anxiety level when rituals are delayed."]},{"title":"Signs care is working","lines":["Washing down from 6 hours to under 2 a day.","Hands healing with no signs of infection.","Eating three meals; sleeping 7 hours.","Can wait a set time before washing and uses breathing to ride out the anxiety."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-ocd:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"22-year-old college student admitted because handwashing now takes 6 hours a day. Her hands are red, cracked and bleeding. She knows the fear of germs is excessive but says, “If I don’t wash, something terrible will happen to my family.” She has missed her classes for a month and rarely eats in the dining hall.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priorities: impaired skin integrity and infection risk, then nutrition, sleep and elimination (rituals crowd them out), then anxiety and ineffective coping, then role performance (school, relationships).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-ocd:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"22-year-old college student admitted because handwashing now takes 6 hours a day. Her hands are red, cracked and bleeding. She knows the fear of germs is excessive but says, “If I don’t wash, something terrible will happen to my family.” She has missed her classes for a month and rarely eats in the dining hall.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Don’t interrupt rituals abruptly or punish them — at first, build time for them into a structured daily schedule. Then gradually limit them with her agreement. Meet physical needs, protect her skin, use a nonjudgmental approach, teach relaxation, and support exposure and response prevention and the SSRI. Measure time spent on rituals each day, the condition of her skin, intake and weight, sleep, attendance at therapy and classes, and her anxiety level when rituals are delayed.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Look for an elevated, expansive or irritable mood lasting at least a week, with increased energy: less need for sleep, pressured speech, flight of ideas, grandiosity, distractibility and risky behavior. Then look at the body: no sleep, no food, dehydration and exhaustion are what make mania dangerous."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"The top priority is injury and physical exhaustion: hydration, nutrition, sleep and protection from her own impulsive or aggressive acts. Then come impaired judgment (money, sexuality, safety of others), then medication adherence. Remember that suicide risk is real in bipolar disorder, especially in mixed states and on the way down from mania."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Reassess: sleep hours, intake and output and weight, urine specific gravity, vital signs, lithium level and any toxicity signs, how long redirection lasts, and incidents of aggression. Ready for discharge when she sleeps, eats, controls her behavior, can name her early warning signs (less sleep, racing thoughts, spending) and has a plan for lithium levels and follow-up."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Days without sleep, racing speech, risky spending; lithium stopped 5 weeks ago.","rule":"The top priority is injury and physical exhaustion: hydration, nutrition, sleep and protection from her own impulsive or aggressive acts. Then come impaired judgment (money, sexuality, safety of others), then medication adherence. Remember that suicide risk is real in bipolar disorder, especially in mixed states and on the way down from mania.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-bipolar","ch":"13","chapters":["13"],"title":"Bipolar I — acute mania","notes":["c-bipolar"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 13; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Connect the cues. This is mania (the mood plus the energy, lasting a week, with impairment), triggered by stopping lithium. The danger comes from the physical cost: no sleep and no intake lead to dehydration, exhaustion and cardiac strain. Rule out what can mimic mania: stimulants, steroids, hyperthyroidism and alcohol.","Priority: The top priority is injury and physical exhaustion: hydration, nutrition, sleep and protection from her own impulsive or aggressive acts. Then come impaired judgment (money, sexuality, safety of others), then medication adherence. Remember that suicide risk is real in bipolar disorder, especially in mixed states and on the way down from mania.","Act: Use a firm, calm, consistent approach and short, concrete statements. Keep stimulation low and redirect energy into solitary physical activity. Give high-calorie finger foods and fluids she can take on the move, and track intake and output and weight. Set clear limits with consequences. Lock up valuables. Give medication as ordered and watch for lithium toxicity. Seclusion or restraint is only a last resort, for imminent danger.","Reassess: Reassess: sleep hours, intake and output and weight, urine specific gravity, vital signs, lithium level and any toxicity signs, how long redirection lasts, and incidents of aggression. Ready for discharge when she sleeps, eats, controls her behavior, can name her early warning signs (less sleep, racing thoughts, spending) and has a plan for lithium levels and follow-up."],"terms":["Decreased need for sleep","Pressured speech","Flight of ideas","Grandiosity","Distractibility","Poor judgment and risk-taking","Psychomotor agitation","High urine specific gravity (above 1.030)","Tachycardia and rising BP","Medication nonadherence","olanzapine","quetiapine","aripiprazole","lithium","valproate","carbamazepine","lamotrigine","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Decreased need for sleep":"Up all night cleaning, calling people or writing plans, and says she feels rested after 2 hours. Mania is a state of overdriven arousal (high dopamine and norepinephrine signaling). The brain no longer sends the “tired” signal, so the body runs without recovery. The single most reliable early sign of a manic relapse. Without sleep, exhaustion, dehydration and cardiovascular collapse can follow, so rest is a nursing priority, not a comfort measure.","Pressured speech":"Loud, rapid, hard to interrupt; she keeps talking over you and doesn’t wait for answers. Thoughts are being generated faster than they can be spoken, so speech is pushed out with urgency. Keep your statements short and concrete, because a long explanation will not be taken in. Document examples word for word.","Flight of ideas":"“I’m going home… home sweet home… home is where the heart is… heart of the matter is I want out.” Rapid jumps from topic to topic, linked by sounds, puns or loose associations; the listener can usually follow the links. A thought-process finding that tells you the severity. Don’t try to follow every topic; redirect to one simple task.","Grandiosity":"“I’m going to buy the hospital.” “The president is waiting for my call.” An inflated self-esteem that can reach delusional intensity in severe mania. Don’t argue with it or reinforce it. Respond to the feeling and the reality: “You have big plans. Right now, let’s eat this sandwich.”","Distractibility":"Starts a sentence, notices the TV, then a nurse walking by, then the window. Attention can’t filter competing stimuli, so every sound or movement captures it. This is why stimulation is kept low and why redirection works: distractibility can be used to steer the patient away from conflict.","Poor judgment and risk-taking":"Gave away a paycheck, sexually provocative behavior, reckless driving, spending sprees. The brain’s brake on impulses (prefrontal control) is overridden by the reward drive. Protect the patient from consequences they wouldn’t choose when well: lock up valuables, set limits on sexual behavior, and assign staff thoughtfully.","Psychomotor agitation":"Pacing, unable to sit, constant movement, may skip meals and fluids entirely. Excessive energy plus distractibility means the body burns calories and fluid much faster than they are replaced. Risk for dehydration, weight loss and exhaustion. Track intake and output, daily weights and vital signs.","High urine specific gravity (above 1.030)":"Concentrated, dark urine; dry lips and mucous membranes. The kidneys conserve water when intake is low and losses (sweat, activity) are high. Objective evidence of dehydration. It also matters for lithium: dehydration raises the lithium level, so this patient is at risk once lithium is restarted.","Tachycardia and rising BP":"HR 112, BP 148/90 while pacing. Sympathetic overdrive from constant activity, with less circulating volume from dehydration. Trend these. In the textbook case, physical stability (VS, intake, sleep) is the first outcome, before insight.","Medication nonadherence":"“I stopped it. I feel amazing; I don’t need it.” Mania feels good at first, and lithium can blunt that high. Thirst, tremor and weight gain also push people to stop. The most common cause of relapse. Teaching later focuses on the early signs of relapse and why to continue lithium after the episode ends."},"patient":"28-year-old brought in by police after weaving on the highway. Hasn’t slept in 4 nights, talking nonstop, says she is “on the way to fame and fortune,” gave her paycheck to strangers. Sister says she stopped her lithium about 5 weeks ago. Weight down 3 kg this week.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-bipolar","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Look for an elevated, expansive or irritable mood lasting at least a week, with increased energy: less need for sleep, pressured speech, flight of ideas, grandiosity, distractibility and risky behavior. Then look at the body: no sleep, no food, dehydration and exhaustion are what make mania dangerous."]},{"title":"Mania vs hypomania","lines":["Mania: at least 1 week (or any length if hospitalized), with marked impairment in work or relationships, or psychosis → bipolar I.","Hypomania: at least 4 days, a clear change that others notice, but no marked impairment and no psychosis → bipolar II when paired with major depression.","Cyclothymia: 2 years (1 in children) of hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full criteria.","The difference is severity and impairment, not the type of symptom. A manic episode makes the diagnosis bipolar I even without a depressive episode."]},{"title":"Speech and thought words to know","lines":["Pressured speech: rapid, loud, urgent, hard to interrupt.","Flight of ideas: rapid topic shifts with links you can follow.","Circumstantial: lots of extra detail, but eventually gets to the point.","Tangential: drifts off and never returns to the point.","Loose associations: ideas only loosely connected and hard to follow.","Clang associations: words chosen for their sound or rhyme (“blue, true, do, shoe”)."]},{"title":"Rapid cycling","lines":["4 or more mood episodes in a year.","More common in women and with antidepressant use.","Responds better to anticonvulsant mood stabilizers than to lithium."]}]},{"id":"c-bipolar","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Connect the cues. This is mania (the mood plus the energy, lasting a week, with impairment), triggered by stopping lithium. The danger comes from the physical cost: no sleep and no intake lead to dehydration, exhaustion and cardiac strain. Rule out what can mimic mania: stimulants, steroids, hyperthyroidism and alcohol."]},{"title":"What else could look like mania?","lines":["Substances: cocaine, amphetamines, steroids, antidepressants given alone in bipolar disorder (can “switch” a patient into mania).","Medical: hyperthyroidism, brain injury or tumor, delirium, MS.","So the work-up includes a urine drug screen, thyroid panel and BMP.","The DSM requires that the episode isn’t explained by a substance or another medical condition, which is why the nurse asks about drugs and checks labs."]},{"title":"Why lithium labs come first","lines":["Baseline: renal function (creatinine, BUN), thyroid (TSH, T4), sodium, ECG as indicated, pregnancy test.","Lithium is cleared by the kidneys, can cause hypothyroidism, and is handled like sodium."]}]},{"id":"c-bipolar","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The top priority is injury and physical exhaustion: hydration, nutrition, sleep and protection from her own impulsive or aggressive acts. Then come impaired judgment (money, sexuality, safety of others), then medication adherence. Remember that suicide risk is real in bipolar disorder, especially in mixed states and on the way down from mania."]},{"title":"Priority problems (textbook order)","lines":["Risk for injury: hyperactivity, exhaustion, dehydration.","Risk for violence (to self or others): irritability, intrusiveness, poor impulse control.","Impaired nutrition and sleep.","Impaired social interaction and family coping.","Nonadherence to medication.","Maslow: physiological needs and safety come before insight and education. A patient in acute mania can’t learn anything yet."]},{"title":"Phases of treatment","lines":["Acute: prevent injury, stabilize sleep, food, fluids and safety; start medication.","Continuation (4–9 months): prevent relapse; adherence and teaching.","Maintenance: prevent new episodes; recognize early signs, keep routines."]}]},{"id":"c-bipolar","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes for the acute phase: she stays well hydrated (balanced intake and output, normal urine specific gravity), gets sleep (at least 4–6 hours), maintains her weight, causes no harm to herself or others, and her lithium level reaches range without toxicity. Later outcomes: she can name early relapse signs and her reasons to stay on medication."]},{"title":"Lithium targets (textbook)","lines":["Acute mania: 12-hour trough of 0.8–1.2 mEq/L (sometimes 1.0–1.2).","Maintenance: 0.6–0.8 mEq/L (0.4–0.6 in some cases).","Draw the level 12 hours after the last dose (a trough), every 2–3 days at the start and after any dose change, then every 3–6 months.","Onset is slow, about 7–14 days, so an antipsychotic or benzodiazepine covers the acute agitation.","The therapeutic window is narrow: early toxicity begins at about 1.5. A level drawn soon after a dose reads falsely high."]}]},{"id":"c-bipolar","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Use a firm, calm, consistent approach and short, concrete statements. Keep stimulation low and redirect energy into solitary physical activity. Give high-calorie finger foods and fluids she can take on the move, and track intake and output and weight. Set clear limits with consequences. Lock up valuables. Give medication as ordered and watch for lithium toxicity. Seclusion or restraint is only a last resort, for imminent danger."]},{"title":"Communication in mania","lines":["Firm, calm, short: “Jasmine, come with me. Drink this.”","Consistent limits from every staff member (prevents manipulation and splitting staff against each other).","Name the expectation and the consequence in concrete words: “Do not touch other patients. If you can’t stop, we will help you stop.”","Listen to legitimate complaints and act on them.","Redirect rather than argue; distractibility is your best tool.","A short attention span means long explanations are lost. Calm, firm structure gives the patient outside control while her inside control is gone."]},{"title":"Milieu: low stimulation","lines":["Away from bright lights, noise and groups. A single room if possible.","Solitary activities: walking, writing, sorting with a staff member. No competitive games (they escalate).","Frequent rest periods. At night: a warm bath, soothing music, no caffeine."]},{"title":"Physical needs","lines":["Finger foods and high-calorie drinks (sandwiches, fruit, shakes) she can eat while moving: “eating on the run.”","Remind her to eat and drink often; she doesn’t notice hunger or thirst.","Intake and output, daily weight, vital signs.","Step-by-step hygiene prompts; appropriate clothing to protect her dignity.","Fiber and fluids; watch for constipation from dehydration."]},{"title":"Lithium teaching","lines":["Keep fluids steady at about 1.5–3 L a day, and keep sodium intake steady (don’t start a low-salt diet).","Hold it and call if there is vomiting, diarrhea or heavy sweating: these dehydrate and raise the level.","Avoid NSAIDs, thiazide diuretics and ACE inhibitors unless the prescriber knows; they raise the level. Caffeine lowers it, so keep caffeine intake steady too.","Take it with meals. It is not addictive. Keep taking it after you feel well.","Kidney and thyroid labs periodically. Avoid in pregnancy.","The kidney handles lithium like sodium. When sodium or water is low, the kidney holds onto sodium and lithium with it, so the level rises."]},{"title":"Lithium side effects vs toxicity","lines":["Expected (<1.5): nausea, thirst, polyuria, fine hand tremor, mild sedation, weight gain. Long term: hypothyroidism, goiter, kidney damage.","Early toxicity (1.5–2.0): vomiting and diarrhea, coarse tremor, confusion, muscle irritability, incoordination → hold the dose and get a level.","Advanced (2.0–2.5): ataxia, blurred vision, clonic movements, large dilute urine output, seizures, stupor, hypotension → hospitalize, stop the drug, possibly whole-bowel irrigation.","Severe (>2.5): convulsions, oliguria, death → hemodialysis.","A fine tremor is an expected side effect; a coarse tremor plus GI symptoms and confusion means the level is climbing."]},{"title":"Other mood stabilizers","lines":["Valproate (Depakote): liver (LFTs), platelets (CBC), pancreatitis, hyperammonemia, blood levels; teratogenic (neural tube defects): pregnancy test and birth control. Good for mixed episodes and rapid cycling.","Carbamazepine (second line): CBC (leukopenia, aplastic anemia), LFTs, sodium (hyponatremia), ECG; SJS/TEN (test for HLA-B1502 in patients of Asian ancestry; don’t start if positive); induces CYP enzymes, so it lowers other drugs’ levels, including birth control pills.","Lamotrigine: maintenance and bipolar depression (not acute mania); titrate slowly; any rash → stop and call (SJS). Valproate raises lamotrigine levels, so its dose is halved when valproate is added.","Second-generation antipsychotics (olanzapine, quetiapine, aripiprazole…): fast control of mania; weight, glucose and lipids."]},{"title":"Seclusion and restraint in mania","lines":["Only for imminent danger when less-restrictive measures (verbal de-escalation, PRN medication) have failed.","Needs a provider order (renewed per policy, for adults every 4 hours up to 24); in an emergency the nurse may initiate and get the order promptly.","Observe continuously; offer food, fluids and toileting and check circulation per protocol.","Never as punishment or for staff convenience."]}]},{"id":"c-bipolar","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Reassess: sleep hours, intake and output and weight, urine specific gravity, vital signs, lithium level and any toxicity signs, how long redirection lasts, and incidents of aggression. Ready for discharge when she sleeps, eats, controls her behavior, can name her early warning signs (less sleep, racing thoughts, spending) and has a plan for lithium levels and follow-up."]},{"title":"Signs care is working","lines":["Sleeping 6 or more hours.","Intake meets output; specific gravity is back to normal; weight is stable.","Speech slower and easier to interrupt; can sit through a meal or a short group.","Lithium trough is in range with no coarse tremor, vomiting or confusion."]},{"title":"Signs it isn’t working","lines":["Rising agitation or threats → PRN medication, more staff, and seclusion only as a last resort.","Vomiting, diarrhea, coarse tremor, confusion → hold lithium, draw a level, notify the provider.","A sudden drop in mood → assess for suicide (mixed or post-manic depression)."]},{"title":"Therapies after the acute phase","lines":["Psychoeducation for the patient and family, and support groups (NAMI, DBSA).","CBT, family-focused therapy, and interpersonal and social rhythm therapy (stable sleep, meals and routine protect against relapse).","ECT for severe or treatment-resistant mania or depression."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-bipolar:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient on lithium has a level of 1.9 mEq/L, vomiting, a coarse hand tremor and confusion. The nurse should first:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Give the next dose with food","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. 1.5–2.0 with GI symptoms, coarse tremor and confusion = early toxicity: hold the dose, get a level, notify the provider."},{"id":"o1","text":"Hold the lithium and notify the provider","correct":true,"why":"Supported. 1.5–2.0 with GI symptoms, coarse tremor and confusion = early toxicity: hold the dose, get a level, notify the provider."},{"id":"o2","text":"Encourage a low-sodium diet","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. 1.5–2.0 with GI symptoms, coarse tremor and confusion = early toxicity: hold the dose, get a level, notify the provider."},{"id":"o3","text":"Reassure the patient that these effects are expected","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. 1.5–2.0 with GI symptoms, coarse tremor and confusion = early toxicity: hold the dose, get a level, notify the provider."}]},{"id":"c-bipolar:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"28-year-old brought in by police after weaving on the highway. Hasn’t slept in 4 nights, talking nonstop, says she is “on the way to fame and fortune,” gave her paycheck to strangers. Sister says she stopped her lithium about 5 weeks ago. Weight down 3 kg this week.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"The top priority is injury and physical exhaustion: hydration, nutrition, sleep and protection from her own impulsive or aggressive acts. Then come impaired judgment (money, sexuality, safety of others), then medication adherence. Remember that suicide risk is real in bipolar disorder, especially in mixed states and on the way down from mania.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-bipolar:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"28-year-old brought in by police after weaving on the highway. Hasn’t slept in 4 nights, talking nonstop, says she is “on the way to fame and fortune,” gave her paycheck to strangers. Sister says she stopped her lithium about 5 weeks ago. Weight down 3 kg this week.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Use a firm, calm, consistent approach and short, concrete statements. Keep stimulation low and redirect energy into solitary physical activity. Give high-calorie finger foods and fluids she can take on the move, and track intake and output and weight. Set clear limits with consequences. Lock up valuables. Give medication as ordered and watch for lithium toxicity. Seclusion or restraint is only a last resort, for imminent danger. Reassess: sleep hours, intake and output and weight, urine specific gravity, vital signs, lithium level and any toxicity signs, how long redirection lasts, and incidents of aggression. Ready for discharge when she sleeps, eats, controls her behavior, can name her early warning signs (less sleep, racing thoughts, spending) and has a plan for lithium levels and follow-up.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Look for depressed mood or anhedonia most of the day, nearly every day, for at least 2 weeks, plus the vegetative signs: sleep (early waking or hypersomnia), appetite and weight, energy, psychomotor change, concentration, guilt or worthlessness, and thoughts of death. Hear the covert suicide message."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"The first priority is suicide risk: keep him safe and observed and remove the means. Next come the physical needs (nutrition, fluids, sleep, hygiene, elimination), then hopelessness and social isolation, then teaching about the medication and follow-up."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Reassess suicide risk at every shift, and especially with a sudden improvement in mood, before passes, before changing the observation level and at discharge. Track sleep, intake and weight, hygiene, attendance at groups and statements of hope. Watch for side effects and serotonin syndrome."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Two weeks of low mood and anhedonia, early waking, weight loss; antidepressant just started.","rule":"The first priority is suicide risk: keep him safe and observed and remove the means. Next come the physical needs (nutrition, fluids, sleep, hygiene, elimination), then hopelessness and social isolation, then teaching about the medication and follow-up.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-mdd","ch":"14","chapters":["14"],"title":"Major depressive disorder","notes":["c-mdd"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 14; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: The cues fit major depressive disorder, with suicidal ideation, access to lethal means (rifle), male sex, middle age, living alone and recent loss, all of which raise the risk. Note the timing: he is 10 days into an SSRI, when energy may return before mood improves.","Priority: The first priority is suicide risk: keep him safe and observed and remove the means. Next come the physical needs (nutrition, fluids, sleep, hygiene, elimination), then hopelessness and social isolation, then teaching about the medication and follow-up.","Act: Start suicide precautions per the risk level, remove harmful objects, and ask about the plan, means and intent directly. Spend short, frequent times with him; sit in silence if he can’t talk; make observations rather than asking many questions; avoid platitudes. Meet the physical needs step by step. Give antidepressants, teach, and watch for serotonin syndrome, TCA overdose and the MAOI–tyramine reaction. Consider ECT for severe or psychotic depression.","Reassess: Reassess suicide risk at every shift, and especially with a sudden improvement in mood, before passes, before changing the observation level and at discharge. Track sleep, intake and weight, hygiene, attendance at groups and statements of hope. Watch for side effects and serotonin syndrome."],"terms":["Anhedonia","Psychomotor retardation","Flat or blunted affect","Early-morning awakening","Worthlessness, guilt, hopelessness","Covert suicide message","The “energy before mood” window","Weight loss more than 5% in a month","sertraline","citalopram","escitalopram","venlafaxine","bupropion","fluoxetine","paroxetine","duloxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Anhedonia":"“Nothing feels good anymore. I used to love fishing; now I don’t care.” Reduced activity in the brain’s reward pathway (dopamine), so activities no longer produce pleasure or motivation. One of the two key symptoms (with depressed mood); at least one of the two is required for the diagnosis.","Psychomotor retardation":"Slow movements, long pauses before answering, a fixed gaze, slumped posture; in severe cases mute and not moving. Depression slows both motor and cognitive processing (anergia: an abnormal lack of energy). Allow time to respond, and use simple words. Watch for complications of immobility: constipation, skin breakdown, poor intake.","Flat or blunted affect":"Almost no facial expression, a monotone voice, doesn’t smile at news that should be good. Affect is the observed outward expression of emotion. Constricted → blunted → flat is a range of decreasing expression. Document it as an objective finding. Note whether affect matches the stated mood (congruent); a sudden bright affect can be a warning sign.","Early-morning awakening":"Wakes at 3–4 a.m. and can’t get back to sleep; mood is worst in the morning (diurnal variation). Depression disrupts sleep architecture: less deep sleep, with REM sleep coming early. A hallmark vegetative sign. Track sleep hours; improvement in sleep is often one of the first signs that treatment is working.","Worthlessness, guilt, hopelessness":"“I’m a burden.” “It’s all my fault.” “It will never get better.” Negative thinking about self, world and future (Beck’s cognitive triad) is a core feature of depression. Hopelessness is the feeling most linked to suicide. It is your cue to ask directly about suicidal thoughts.","Covert suicide message":"“My family would be better off without me.” “I won’t be a problem much longer.” Many people hint before they state intent directly; ambivalence leaves room to be rescued. Always follow up with a direct question: “Are you thinking about killing yourself?” Asking does not plant the idea.","The “energy before mood” window":"In the 2nd–4th week of an antidepressant, the patient is more active and talkative but still hopeless. Sleep, appetite and energy often improve before the depressed thinking lifts. A patient who was too slowed to act may now have the energy to carry out a plan. Watch closely in the first weeks of treatment and after any dose change. This is why antidepressants carry a black box warning about suicidality in patients under 25.","Weight loss more than 5% in a month":"Loose clothing, 5 kg down, leaving most of the meal tray. Appetite loss is a vegetative sign; anhedonia removes the pleasure of eating. Weigh weekly, offer small frequent high-calorie meals and favorite foods, and check labs if intake is very poor."},"patient":"52-year-old admitted after 6 weeks of low mood since being laid off. Wakes at 3 a.m. every night, has lost 5 kg, stopped showering, speaks slowly in a monotone. Says “my family would be better off without me.” Sertraline was started 10 days ago.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-mdd","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Look for depressed mood or anhedonia most of the day, nearly every day, for at least 2 weeks, plus the vegetative signs: sleep (early waking or hypersomnia), appetite and weight, energy, psychomotor change, concentration, guilt or worthlessness, and thoughts of death. Hear the covert suicide message."]},{"title":"Diagnosis: 5 of 9 symptoms for 2 weeks","lines":["Depressed mood or anhedonia (at least one of these).","Weight or appetite change (more than 5% in a month).","Insomnia or hypersomnia.","Psychomotor agitation or retardation (seen by others).","Fatigue or loss of energy.","Worthlessness or excessive guilt.","Poor concentration, indecisiveness.","Recurrent thoughts of death, suicidal ideation, plan or attempt.","The symptoms must cause distress or impairment and not be due to a substance or a medical condition, such as hypothyroidism."]},{"title":"Other depressive disorders","lines":["Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia): depressed mood most days for at least 2 years (1 year in children); milder but chronic.","Premenstrual dysphoric disorder: mood symptoms in the week before menses that improve after menses starts.","Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder: children 6–18 with severe temper outbursts and constant irritability.","Substance- or medication-induced and due to another medical condition (e.g., steroids, beta-blockers, hypothyroidism, Parkinson disease).","Specifiers: with seasonal pattern (winter; light therapy), with peripartum onset, with psychotic features."]},{"title":"Depression in older adults and children","lines":["Older adults: more somatic complaints and memory problems (can look like dementia: “pseudodementia”), less talk of sadness. The highest suicide rates are in older men.","Children and teens: irritability more than sadness, falling grades, withdrawal, behavior problems."]}]},{"id":"c-mdd","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The cues fit major depressive disorder, with suicidal ideation, access to lethal means (rifle), male sex, middle age, living alone and recent loss, all of which raise the risk. Note the timing: he is 10 days into an SSRI, when energy may return before mood improves."]},{"title":"Causes the nurse should know","lines":["Biologic: lower serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine signaling; HPA axis overactivity (high cortisol); genetics (first-degree relatives).","Cognitive (Beck): a negative view of self, world and future, kept going by cognitive distortions.","Stress-diathesis: a vulnerability plus a stressor (loss, abuse, illness).","Medical and drug causes: hypothyroidism, steroids, beta-blockers, alcohol."]},{"title":"Grief vs depression","lines":["Grief: waves of sadness tied to reminders of the loss; self-esteem intact; can still feel moments of joy.","Depression: a constant low mood, anhedonia, worthlessness and self-loathing.","DSM-5 removed the bereavement exclusion: MDD can be diagnosed during grief if the criteria are met."]}]},{"id":"c-mdd","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The first priority is suicide risk: keep him safe and observed and remove the means. Next come the physical needs (nutrition, fluids, sleep, hygiene, elimination), then hopelessness and social isolation, then teaching about the medication and follow-up."]},{"title":"Priority problems","lines":["Risk for suicide.","Imbalanced nutrition, disturbed sleep, self-care deficit, constipation.","Hopelessness, chronic low self-esteem, social isolation.","Ineffective coping.","Safety outranks everything. A patient who is hopeless and has a means comes before the patient who needs a shower."]}]},{"id":"c-mdd","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: he does not harm himself and will tell staff when suicidal thoughts increase. He eats at least 50–75% of meals and stops losing weight, sleeps 6 or more hours, does basic hygiene, attends groups, and can state two reasons to live and two coping strategies. Before discharge: a written safety plan and the rifle removed from the home."]},{"title":"Antidepressant timeline to teach","lines":["Side effects come first, in days.","Sleep, appetite and energy start improving in 1–3 weeks.","Full effect on mood takes 4–6 weeks or more.","Continue for at least 6–12 months after remission; taper rather than stopping abruptly.","Without this timeline, patients stop the drug in week 2 because “it’s not working” and they feel the side effects."]}]},{"id":"c-mdd","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Start suicide precautions per the risk level, remove harmful objects, and ask about the plan, means and intent directly. Spend short, frequent times with him; sit in silence if he can’t talk; make observations rather than asking many questions; avoid platitudes. Meet the physical needs step by step. Give antidepressants, teach, and watch for serotonin syndrome, TCA overdose and the MAOI–tyramine reaction. Consider ECT for severe or psychotic depression."]},{"title":"Talking with a withdrawn, depressed patient","lines":["Make observations instead of asking questions: “You’re wearing your new shoes today.”","Use simple, concrete words and allow time for answers.","Sit with him in silence; your presence communicates worth.","Listen for hidden suicide messages and ask directly.","Avoid platitudes (“Cheer up,” “Things will look up,” “Everyone gets down”); they minimize his pain and increase guilt.","Don’t be overly cheerful; match his pace.","Slowed thinking makes direct questions overwhelming. Platitudes tell him he is failing at something he can’t control."]},{"title":"Cognitive work (when he can engage)","lines":["Help him spot distortions: overgeneralizing (“I always fail”), self-blame, mind reading, and discounting the positive.","Look for evidence and alternative explanations.","Set small, achievable goals (shower, then one group) so he experiences success.","Encourage exercise, support groups and reconnecting with people."]},{"title":"Physical care","lines":["Nutrition: small frequent meals, favorite foods, sit with him at meals; weigh weekly.","Sleep: no daytime naps, a bedtime routine, no caffeine, warm milk or a bath.","Hygiene: step-by-step prompts; praise each step.","Elimination: fluids, fiber, activity (slowed movement plus TCA side effects lead to constipation)."]},{"title":"SSRIs (first line)","lines":["Sertraline, fluoxetine, paroxetine, citalopram, escitalopram.","Block serotonin reuptake. Low overdose lethality, so safer in suicidal patients.","Side effects: nausea, insomnia or agitation, headache, sexual dysfunction, weight change, hyponatremia in older adults, bleeding risk with NSAIDs or warfarin.","Don’t stop abruptly (discontinuation syndrome: dizziness, flu-like symptoms, irritability).","Black box warning: increased suicidal thinking in patients under 25 in the early weeks."]},{"title":"Serotonin syndrome","lines":["Cause: too much serotonin: two serotonergic drugs together (SSRI + MAOI, triptans, tramadol, St. John’s wort, linezolid) or a dose increase.","Signs: agitation or confusion, fever, sweating, tachycardia, high BP, diarrhea, tremor, myoclonus and hyperreflexia → seizures, hyperthermia, shock.","Action: stop the drug, notify the provider, cool, give IV fluids and supportive care; cyproheptadine (serotonin blocker) and benzodiazepines for rigidity.","Wait 2–5 weeks after stopping an SSRI (5 for fluoxetine) and 14 days after stopping an MAOI before switching between them.","Serotonin acts on the brain, the gut, the muscles and the autonomic nervous system, so an excess shows up in all four: mental status, GI, neuromuscular and autonomic signs."]},{"title":"SNRIs, bupropion, mirtazapine, trazodone","lines":["SNRIs (venlafaxine, duloxetine): can raise BP; duloxetine also treats nerve pain.","Bupropion: no sexual side effects or weight gain; lowers the seizure threshold: avoid in seizure disorders, eating disorders, and abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal; also used to quit smoking.","Mirtazapine: sedating, increases appetite and weight; taken at bedtime.","Trazodone: very sedating, used for sleep; priapism."]},{"title":"TCAs (amitriptyline, nortriptyline, imipramine)","lines":["Anticholinergic effects: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, tachycardia; orthostatic hypotension; sedation (give at bedtime).","Lethal in overdose (cardiac dysrhythmias): dispense small amounts to suicidal patients.","Use with caution in heart disease, glaucoma, BPH and older adults."]},{"title":"MAOIs (phenelzine, tranylcypromine, selegiline patch)","lines":["Tyramine + MAOI → hypertensive crisis: severe occipital headache, stiff neck, flushing, sweating, chest pain → stroke.","Avoid: aged cheeses, cured or smoked meats (salami, pepperoni), tap or imported beer, Chianti, soy sauce and fermented soy, fava beans, overripe avocados, banana peels, yeast extracts (Marmite).","Safe: fresh meat and fish, milk, cottage and cream cheese, most fruits and vegetables.","No OTC cold medicines (decongestants), stimulants, other antidepressants or meperidine.","Third line because of these interactions.","Monoamine oxidase in the gut normally destroys dietary tyramine. When it is blocked, tyramine reaches the blood and releases a surge of norepinephrine."]},{"title":"ECT and other brain stimulation","lines":["ECT: for severe, psychotic, catatonic or treatment-resistant depression, or when someone is acutely suicidal and can’t wait weeks. Usually 2–3 times a week, 6–12 treatments.","Before: informed consent, NPO after midnight, void, remove dentures and jewelry; general anesthesia + a muscle relaxant (succinylcholine) + oxygen.","After: recovery position, vital signs, confusion and short-term memory loss are expected and temporary; reorient him.","rTMS: magnetic pulses, no anesthesia, outpatient. VNS and deep brain stimulation for resistant cases.","Light therapy for seasonal pattern: bright light in the morning.","Esketamine nasal spray: rapid effect; monitor BP and dissociation for 2 hours; no driving until the next day."]}]},{"id":"c-mdd","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Reassess suicide risk at every shift, and especially with a sudden improvement in mood, before passes, before changing the observation level and at discharge. Track sleep, intake and weight, hygiene, attendance at groups and statements of hope. Watch for side effects and serotonin syndrome."]},{"title":"Warning: sudden calm","lines":["A previously hopeless patient who is suddenly cheerful and at peace, gives away belongings or says goodbye may have made a decision to die.","Reassess directly and increase observation; don’t loosen precautions because he “looks better.”"]},{"title":"Signs care is working","lines":["Sleeping through the night; eating most of his meals; weight stable.","Showers on his own; goes to groups; talks with peers.","Talks about the future (“When I get home I want to…”).","Has a safety plan with warning signs, coping steps, people and places, crisis lines (988) and means removed."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"DailyMed: haloperidol labeling, NMS recognition and management","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b5a6c54c-fb70-4132-90ae-7d75b5e9c9de"}],"steps":[{"id":"c-mdd:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with depression who started an antidepressant 2 weeks ago has more energy and is more talkative. The nurse should:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Reduce suicide precautions since he is improving","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Energy often returns before mood improves, so risk can rise in the early weeks. Keep monitoring closely."},{"id":"o1","text":"Continue close monitoring for suicide risk","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Energy often returns before mood improves, so risk can rise in the early weeks. Keep monitoring closely."},{"id":"o2","text":"Discontinue the medication","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Energy often returns before mood improves, so risk can rise in the early weeks. Keep monitoring closely."},{"id":"o3","text":"Discharge him to home","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Energy often returns before mood improves, so risk can rise in the early weeks. Keep monitoring closely."}]},{"id":"c-mdd:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"52-year-old admitted after 6 weeks of low mood since being laid off. Wakes at 3 a.m. every night, has lost 5 kg, stopped showering, speaks slowly in a monotone. Says “my family would be better off without me.” Sertraline was started 10 days ago.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"The first priority is suicide risk: keep him safe and observed and remove the means. Next come the physical needs (nutrition, fluids, sleep, hygiene, elimination), then hopelessness and social isolation, then teaching about the medication and follow-up.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-mdd:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"52-year-old admitted after 6 weeks of low mood since being laid off. Wakes at 3 a.m. every night, has lost 5 kg, stopped showering, speaks slowly in a monotone. Says “my family would be better off without me.” Sertraline was started 10 days ago.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Start suicide precautions per the risk level, remove harmful objects, and ask about the plan, means and intent directly. Spend short, frequent times with him; sit in silence if he can’t talk; make observations rather than asking many questions; avoid platitudes. Meet the physical needs step by step. Give antidepressants, teach, and watch for serotonin syndrome, TCA overdose and the MAOI–tyramine reaction. Consider ECT for severe or psychotic depression. Reassess suicide risk at every shift, and especially with a sudden improvement in mood, before passes, before changing the observation level and at discharge. Track sleep, intake and weight, hygiene, attendance at groups and statements of hope. Watch for side effects and serotonin syndrome.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Listen for overt (“I want to die”) and covert (“You won’t have to worry about me much longer”) statements, and watch for behavior changes: giving things away, putting affairs in order, withdrawal, increased drinking, and a sudden calm. Then ask directly about thoughts, plan, means, intent and past attempts."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"The top priority is risk for suicide: physical safety comes first, with the most restrictive observation that the risk requires. After that: hopelessness, ineffective coping, social isolation and the alcohol use."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Reassess suicide risk every shift, before any change in observation level, before passes, at discharge, and whenever the mood suddenly lifts or drops. Look for reasons to live, use of coping skills, engagement with staff and peers, a completed safety plan and removal of the means. Plan a follow-up appointment within days and a follow-up phone call."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Hopeless after a loss, has a plan and the means; the first hours of admission are the riskiest.","rule":"The top priority is risk for suicide: physical safety comes first, with the most restrictive observation that the risk requires. After that: hopelessness, ineffective coping, social isolation and the alcohol use.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-suicide","ch":"25","chapters":["25"],"title":"Suicide risk","notes":["c-suicide"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 25; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: She has many risk factors at once: depression, a prior attempt, recent losses, alcohol, a specific plan with a firearm that is accessible. Few protective factors are active right now. That makes her high risk. Tools such as the SAFE-T (with the Columbia scale) put the risk level into a structured form.","Priority: The top priority is risk for suicide: physical safety comes first, with the most restrictive observation that the risk requires. After that: hopelessness, ineffective coping, social isolation and the alcohol use.","Act: Start suicide precautions: constant one-to-one observation for high risk (never out of sight, within arm’s length if needed), a search of the patient and her belongings, and removal of sharps, cords, belts and glass. Mouth checks after medications. Document her mood and words every 15 minutes. Build a relationship and talk about feelings. Involve her family and remove the means. Give medications (SSRIs are safer than TCAs in overdose).","Reassess: Reassess suicide risk every shift, before any change in observation level, before passes, at discharge, and whenever the mood suddenly lifts or drops. Look for reasons to live, use of coping skills, engagement with staff and peers, a completed safety plan and removal of the means. Plan a follow-up appointment within days and a follow-up phone call."],"terms":["Suicidal ideation","Plan, means, intent (lethality)","Hopelessness","Sudden calm or improvement","Alcohol and drug use","clozapine","lithium","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Suicidal ideation":"“I keep thinking everyone would be better off without me.” Thoughts of ending one’s life range from passive (“I wish I wouldn’t wake up”) to active, with a plan. The first thing to clarify: passive or active, how often, how strong, and whether there is a plan.","Plan, means, intent (lethality)":"“I’d use the gun in the apartment, this weekend when he’s gone.” Risk rises steeply with a specific plan, a lethal method (firearm, hanging, jumping), access to it, and a timeline. Specific + lethal + available + soon = highest risk → constant one-to-one observation and removal of the means.","Hopelessness":"“Nothing will ever change. There’s no point.” Hopelessness is the cognitive state most closely tied to suicide; it removes the reason to wait for things to improve. Ask about suicide whenever you hear it, and build hope with small goals and reasons for living.","Sudden calm or improvement":"A tearful, agitated patient is suddenly peaceful and smiling, and gives her necklace to a peer. Having made the decision can bring relief. Returning energy (early antidepressant treatment) can also make acting possible. Reassess immediately; never loosen precautions because the patient “seems better.”","Alcohol and drug use":"Blood alcohol 0.09; drinking every night this month. Alcohol lowers inhibition, worsens depression, and makes an impulsive act more likely and more lethal. A major risk factor; also plan for withdrawal monitoring."},"patient":"24-year-old brought to the ED after friends found a goodbye note. Lost her job and broke up with her boyfriend this month. Drinking heavily. Says, “I just want the pain to stop.” Her boyfriend’s handgun is in her apartment. One prior overdose at age 19.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-suicide","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Listen for overt (“I want to die”) and covert (“You won’t have to worry about me much longer”) statements, and watch for behavior changes: giving things away, putting affairs in order, withdrawal, increased drinking, and a sudden calm. Then ask directly about thoughts, plan, means, intent and past attempts."]},{"title":"Warning signs (act now)","lines":["Talking or writing about death or suicide.","Saying they are hopeless, trapped or a burden, or have no reason to live.","More alcohol or drug use; reckless behavior.","Withdrawal from people; dramatic mood changes.","Giving away possessions, writing a will, saying goodbye."]},{"title":"Risk factors","lines":["Previous attempt (the strongest predictor).","Mood disorder, psychosis, substance use, personality disorder.","Access to lethal means (firearms).","Family history of suicide; exposure to the suicide of others.","Recent loss (relationship, job, money, legal trouble), trauma or abuse, chronic illness or pain.","Demographics: men die by suicide more often (more lethal methods); women attempt more often. Older white men have the highest rates; suicide is a leading cause of death in ages 10–34."]},{"title":"Protective factors","lines":["Effective mental health care and easy access to it.","Strong connections to family, friends and community; children in the home.","Problem-solving skills; religious or cultural beliefs against suicide.","Ongoing contact with providers (follow-up calls)."]},{"title":"How to ask","lines":["“Are you thinking about killing yourself?” Use the word; don’t say “you’re not thinking of hurting yourself, are you?”","“Do you have a plan? What is it? Do you have what you need to do it? When?”","“Have you ever tried before? What happened?”","Asking does not put the idea in someone’s head; it usually brings relief."]}]},{"id":"c-suicide","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["She has many risk factors at once: depression, a prior attempt, recent losses, alcohol, a specific plan with a firearm that is accessible. Few protective factors are active right now. That makes her high risk. Tools such as the SAFE-T (with the Columbia scale) put the risk level into a structured form."]},{"title":"Putting the risk together","lines":["Low: thoughts of death, no plan, intent or behavior; strong protective factors → outpatient referral and a safety plan.","Moderate: ideation with a plan but no intent or behavior → consider admission; safety plan; remove means.","High: plan with intent, a lethal method, a recent attempt or psychosis → admission and constant observation.","Risk is not one item but how many risk factors stack up, minus how many protective factors are active."]},{"title":"Common terms","lines":["Suicide: a death caused by self-directed injurious behavior with intent to die.","Suicide attempt: a nonfatal self-directed act with intent to die (with or without injury).","Suicidal ideation: thoughts of ending one’s life.","Nonsuicidal self-injury: deliberate self-harm without intent to die."]}]},{"id":"c-suicide","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The top priority is risk for suicide: physical safety comes first, with the most restrictive observation that the risk requires. After that: hopelessness, ineffective coping, social isolation and the alcohol use."]},{"title":"Risk is highest when…","lines":["In the first days of admission.","During staff changes, shift change, weekends and holidays.","With sudden improvement or worsening.","In the weeks after discharge.","Early in antidepressant treatment, when energy returns before mood."]}]},{"id":"c-suicide","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: she remains safe; she tells staff when suicidal thoughts increase; she identifies reasons to live, warning signs and coping strategies; and before discharge she has a written safety plan and the firearm is out of the home."]},{"title":"Safety plan (6 steps)","lines":["1 · My warning signs (thoughts, moods, situations).","2 · Coping strategies I can do alone (walk, music, shower).","3 · People and places that distract me.","4 · People I can ask for help.","5 · Professionals and crisis lines: 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline, 911.","6 · Making the environment safe: remove guns and stockpiled pills.","A no-suicide contract is not a safety measure and doesn’t protect the patient or the nurse. A safety plan gives concrete steps to use in a crisis."]}]},{"id":"c-suicide","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Start suicide precautions: constant one-to-one observation for high risk (never out of sight, within arm’s length if needed), a search of the patient and her belongings, and removal of sharps, cords, belts and glass. Mouth checks after medications. Document her mood and words every 15 minutes. Build a relationship and talk about feelings. Involve her family and remove the means. Give medications (SSRIs are safer than TCAs in overdose)."]},{"title":"Suicide precautions on the unit","lines":["One-to-one: a staff member always within sight (arm’s length when risk is highest), including in the bathroom and while she sleeps.","Hands visible while sleeping (not under the covers).","Search belongings and visitors’ items; remove belts, shoelaces, cords, plastic bags, razors, glass, metal utensils. Paper or plastic trays.","Watch each medication swallowed (no cheeking or hoarding).","Document location, mood, verbatim statements and behavior every 15–30 minutes.","Unit design: breakaway curtain rods, tamper-proof screws, windows that don’t open."]},{"title":"What to say","lines":["“I’m concerned about your safety. I’m going to stay with you.”","“Tell me what has been happening that makes you want to die.”","Acknowledge the pain; don’t argue, judge or promise secrecy.","Don’t say “You have so much to live for” (minimizes) or “Suicide is a sin” (judges)."]},{"title":"Means restriction","lines":["Ask family to remove firearms (or lock them away off-site) and lethal medications before discharge.","Dispense medications in small quantities (especially TCAs and MAOIs).","Restricting access to the means saves lives: suicidal crises are often short, and a delay lets the crisis pass."]},{"title":"After an attempt in the ED","lines":["Medical stabilization first (airway, overdose treatment).","Then a full suicide assessment and constant observation.","Lithium and clozapine reduce suicide in bipolar disorder and schizophrenia. ECT helps when the risk is acute and severe."]}]},{"id":"c-suicide","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Reassess suicide risk every shift, before any change in observation level, before passes, at discharge, and whenever the mood suddenly lifts or drops. Look for reasons to live, use of coping skills, engagement with staff and peers, a completed safety plan and removal of the means. Plan a follow-up appointment within days and a follow-up phone call."]},{"title":"Signs the risk is falling","lines":["Denies a plan or intent on direct questioning, and her behavior matches.","Talks about the future and about specific reasons to live.","Uses coping strategies; asks staff for help.","Family has removed the gun; outpatient appointment scheduled."]},{"title":"Survivors","lines":["Family and friends of someone who died by suicide (“survivors”) have grief, guilt, anger and their own higher suicide risk.","Offer support groups and counseling. Staff need debriefing too."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-suicide:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which patient is at highest risk for suicide?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A 20-year-old woman with occasional thoughts of death and a supportive family","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Older male, widowed, alcohol use, access to a firearm and social withdrawal: many risk factors, few protective ones."},{"id":"o1","text":"A 68-year-old widowed man who drinks daily, has a firearm and recently stopped seeing friends","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Older male, widowed, alcohol use, access to a firearm and social withdrawal: many risk factors, few protective ones."},{"id":"o2","text":"A 35-year-old with NSSI who denies suicidal intent","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Older male, widowed, alcohol use, access to a firearm and social withdrawal: many risk factors, few protective ones."},{"id":"o3","text":"A 45-year-old with anxiety who attends weekly therapy","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Older male, widowed, alcohol use, access to a firearm and social withdrawal: many risk factors, few protective ones."}]},{"id":"c-suicide:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"24-year-old brought to the ED after friends found a goodbye note. Lost her job and broke up with her boyfriend this month. Drinking heavily. Says, “I just want the pain to stop.” Her boyfriend’s handgun is in her apartment. One prior overdose at age 19.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"The top priority is risk for suicide: physical safety comes first, with the most restrictive observation that the risk requires. After that: hopelessness, ineffective coping, social isolation and the alcohol use.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-suicide:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"24-year-old brought to the ED after friends found a goodbye note. Lost her job and broke up with her boyfriend this month. Drinking heavily. Says, “I just want the pain to stop.” Her boyfriend’s handgun is in her apartment. One prior overdose at age 19.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Start suicide precautions: constant one-to-one observation for high risk (never out of sight, within arm’s length if needed), a search of the patient and her belongings, and removal of sharps, cords, belts and glass. Mouth checks after medications. Document her mood and words every 15 minutes. Build a relationship and talk about feelings. Involve her family and remove the means. Give medications (SSRIs are safer than TCAs in overdose). Reassess suicide risk every shift, before any change in observation level, before passes, at discharge, and whenever the mood suddenly lifts or drops. Look for reasons to live, use of coping skills, engagement with staff and peers, a completed safety plan and removal of the means. Plan a follow-up appointment within days and a follow-up phone call.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Notice patterned injuries (cuts, burns, scratches) in hidden places, at different stages of healing, long sleeves in warm weather, and statements about relieving tension or feeling something. Ask directly about suicidal intent, because NSSI and suicide can coexist."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priorities: wound care and infection, risk for self-mutilation, and the underlying anxiety, depression or trauma, while screening for suicide risk."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Evaluate frequency and severity of injuries over time, whether she reports urges before acting, her use of new coping skills, wound healing, and suicide risk at every contact."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Cuts on the forearms to “feel something” or relieve tension, without intent to die.","rule":"Priorities: wound care and infection, risk for self-mutilation, and the underlying anxiety, depression or trauma, while screening for suicide risk.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-nssi","ch":"25","chapters":["25"],"title":"Nonsuicidal self-injury","notes":["c-nssi"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 25; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: The cues point to NSSI used to manage emotional pain after a family stressor. Intent to die is denied, but NSSI doubles the later risk of a suicide attempt, so this is still a safety issue. One wound appears infected.","Priority: Priorities: wound care and infection, risk for self-mutilation, and the underlying anxiety, depression or trauma, while screening for suicide risk.","Act: Respond calmly and without judgment. Treat the wounds matter-of-factly, without lecturing, showing shock or giving extra attention. Build trust; explore triggers and the function of the injury; teach replacement coping skills (DBT: distress tolerance and emotion regulation). Make a plan to tell staff when urges rise, and keep the environment safe.","Reassess: Evaluate frequency and severity of injuries over time, whether she reports urges before acting, her use of new coping skills, wound healing, and suicide risk at every contact."],"terms":["Nonsuicidal self-injury (NSSI)","“It makes the pressure go away”","Wounds at different stages"],"termDefinitions":{"Nonsuicidal self-injury (NSSI)":"Superficial, parallel cuts on the forearms or thighs; burns; head-banging; scratching. Deliberate damage to one’s own body without intent to die, used to regulate emotion: to escape overwhelming feelings, to end numbness, or as self-punishment. Not a suicide attempt, but a strong risk factor for later suicide attempts. Always assess suicidal intent separately.","“It makes the pressure go away”":"The patient describes rising tension and then relief right after injuring. Tissue damage releases endogenous opioids (endorphins), which give brief relief or even a “high” (pain offset relief). The relief reinforces the behavior. This is why it repeats: it works in the short term. The plan is to replace it with other ways to tolerate distress, not just forbid it.","Wounds at different stages":"Fresh cuts beside healed scars. Shows a repeated pattern rather than one event. Assess frequency and escalation, and check each wound for infection and the need for sutures."},"patient":"16-year-old seen in the school clinic for cuts on her upper arms in different stages of healing, hidden under long sleeves on a hot day. She says, “It’s not like I want to die. It just makes the pressure go away.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-nssi","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Notice patterned injuries (cuts, burns, scratches) in hidden places, at different stages of healing, long sleeves in warm weather, and statements about relieving tension or feeling something. Ask directly about suicidal intent, because NSSI and suicide can coexist."]},{"title":"Who and when","lines":["Usually begins in early adolescence (12–14).","About 15% of teens and 17–35% of college students have self-injured.","Common with borderline personality disorder, depression, anxiety, eating disorders, substance use and trauma.","Proposed DSM criteria: injury on 5 or more days in the past year without suicidal intent, causing distress."]}]},{"id":"c-nssi","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The cues point to NSSI used to manage emotional pain after a family stressor. Intent to die is denied, but NSSI doubles the later risk of a suicide attempt, so this is still a safety issue. One wound appears infected."]},{"title":"NSSI vs suicide attempt","lines":["Intent: to feel better and keep living vs to die.","Method: usually low lethality and repeated vs often higher lethality.","After: relief vs often unchanged or worse.","But the two overlap: ask every time."]},{"title":"Why people self-injure","lines":["To relieve tension, anger, anxiety or numbness (the most common reason).","To punish oneself (self-loathing).","To communicate distress or get help when words fail.","Learned from peers or social media (contagion).","Linked to childhood trauma, harsh punishment, poor attachment and bullying."]}]},{"id":"c-nssi","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priorities: wound care and infection, risk for self-mutilation, and the underlying anxiety, depression or trauma, while screening for suicide risk."]},{"title":"Priority problems","lines":["Risk for self-mutilation / self-mutilation.","Impaired tissue integrity; risk for infection.","Anxiety, ineffective coping, low self-esteem."]}]},{"id":"c-nssi","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: no new self-injury; she asks staff for help when the urge rises; she names her triggers and two or more alternative coping skills; the wounds heal without infection; she engages in treatment (DBT, therapy) and takes medications for any comorbid disorder as prescribed."]}]},{"id":"c-nssi","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Respond calmly and without judgment. Treat the wounds matter-of-factly, without lecturing, showing shock or giving extra attention. Build trust; explore triggers and the function of the injury; teach replacement coping skills (DBT: distress tolerance and emotion regulation). Make a plan to tell staff when urges rise, and keep the environment safe."]},{"title":"Nursing interventions","lines":["Wound care in a neutral, caring way; teach signs of infection.","Use a nonjudgmental, curious tone: “What was happening right before you cut?”","Ask her to tell staff when she feels the urge (and praise her when she does).","Teach alternatives: holding ice, squeezing a stress ball, intense exercise, drawing on the skin with a red marker, journaling, calling someone, grounding.","Don’t make promises to keep it secret; involve parents, and explain why.","Check yourself for frustration or countertransference; get supervision.","Shock or punishment increases shame, which is a trigger. A calm, consistent response keeps her talking to you, and that is your window to teach other skills."]},{"title":"Treatment","lines":["Dialectical behavior therapy (DBT): the best evidence. Skills for distress tolerance, emotion regulation, mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness.","CBT; family therapy for teens.","Medications target the comorbid disorder (SSRIs for depression or anxiety)."]}]},{"id":"c-nssi","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Evaluate frequency and severity of injuries over time, whether she reports urges before acting, her use of new coping skills, wound healing, and suicide risk at every contact."]},{"title":"If it worsens","lines":["More frequent or deeper injuries, a change to a more lethal method, or new suicidal intent → full suicide assessment and a higher level of care."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-nssi:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"16-year-old seen in the school clinic for cuts on her upper arms in different stages of healing, hidden under long sleeves on a hot day. She says, “It’s not like I want to die. It just makes the pressure go away.”","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priorities: wound care and infection, risk for self-mutilation, and the underlying anxiety, depression or trauma, while screening for suicide risk.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-nssi:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"16-year-old seen in the school clinic for cuts on her upper arms in different stages of healing, hidden under long sleeves on a hot day. She says, “It’s not like I want to die. It just makes the pressure go away.”","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Respond calmly and without judgment. Treat the wounds matter-of-factly, without lecturing, showing shock or giving extra attention. Build trust; explore triggers and the function of the injury; teach replacement coping skills (DBT: distress tolerance and emotion regulation). Make a plan to tell staff when urges rise, and keep the environment safe. Evaluate frequency and severity of injuries over time, whether she reports urges before acting, her use of new coping skills, wound healing, and suicide risk at every contact.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Confirm exposure to a traumatic event (actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence: experienced, witnessed, learned about, or repeated exposure to details). Then look for the four symptom clusters: intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in thinking and mood, and changes in arousal and reactivity. Note how long it has lasted."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priorities: safety (suicide risk, firearm access, impulsive anger), then sleep disturbance and substance use, then anxiety, ineffective coping and family processes. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing the trauma, then reconnection."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Track nightmares and sleep hours, startle and irritability, avoidance (is he going places again?), alcohol use, his use of grounding skills, family relationships, and suicide risk at every visit."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Nightmares, flashbacks, hypervigilance and avoidance 3 months after combat.","rule":"Priorities: safety (suicide risk, firearm access, impulsive anger), then sleep disturbance and substance use, then anxiety, ineffective coping and family processes. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing the trauma, then reconnection.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-ptsd","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"PTSD and related disorders","notes":["c-ptsd"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 16; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Combat trauma, plus symptoms in all four clusters lasting more than 1 month, with impairment = PTSD. Complicating risks: alcohol use, emotional withdrawal from family, and a loaded firearm in a man with hyperarousal and possible depression.","Priority: Priorities: safety (suicide risk, firearm access, impulsive anger), then sleep disturbance and substance use, then anxiety, ineffective coping and family processes. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing the trauma, then reconnection.","Act: Use trauma-informed care: safety, trust, choice, collaboration and empowerment, and ask “what happened to you?”, not “what’s wrong with you?”. Stay calm and predictable; don’t push for details of the trauma. Teach grounding and relaxation. Address sleep, alcohol and the firearm. Refer for trauma-focused CBT or EMDR. Give medications as ordered.","Reassess: Track nightmares and sleep hours, startle and irritability, avoidance (is he going places again?), alcohol use, his use of grounding skills, family relationships, and suicide risk at every visit."],"terms":["Intrusion (re-experiencing)","Avoidance","Negative thoughts and mood; emotional numbing","Hyperarousal and hypervigilance","Self-medicating with alcohol","sertraline","venlafaxine","paroxetine","psychotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{"Intrusion (re-experiencing)":"Nightmares, flashbacks, intrusive memories; panic at reminders. Trauma memories are stored in a fragmented, sensory way (the amygdala tags them as “current danger” and the hippocampus doesn’t file them as past), so reminders bring them back as if happening now. During a flashback, ground him in the present: his name, where he is, the date, that he is safe.","Avoidance":"Won’t go to crowded stores or talk about deployment; avoids news and fireworks. Avoiding reminders lowers anxiety in the moment, which reinforces the avoidance and keeps the fear from fading. Gradual, supported exposure in therapy is what lets the fear fade.","Negative thoughts and mood; emotional numbing":"“I feel nothing for my kids.” Guilt that he survived (survivor’s guilt); “The world is dangerous.” Numbing protects him from overwhelming emotion but also shuts out positive feelings. Screen for depression and suicide; the numbness strains his relationships.","Hyperarousal and hypervigilance":"Exaggerated startle, always scanning exits, irritability or angry outbursts, insomnia, poor concentration. The sympathetic nervous system stays switched on, as if the threat were still present. Approach calmly, announce yourself, avoid touching without warning, and give him a seat with a view of the door if possible.","Self-medicating with alcohol":"Drinks every night “to sleep.” Alcohol briefly dampens arousal, but it disrupts sleep, worsens depression and increases impulsivity. Screen for substance use (very common with PTSD) and for suicide, especially with a firearm at home."},"patient":"26-year-old veteran seen in clinic 3 months after returning from deployment. Wakes from nightmares drenched in sweat, startles at loud noises, won’t go to crowded stores, drinks “to sleep,” and says he feels “numb” around his wife and kids. Last week he dove behind a car when a truck backfired.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-ptsd","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Confirm exposure to a traumatic event (actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence: experienced, witnessed, learned about, or repeated exposure to details). Then look for the four symptom clusters: intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in thinking and mood, and changes in arousal and reactivity. Note how long it has lasted."]},{"title":"Timing tells the diagnosis","lines":["Acute stress disorder: trauma symptoms from 3 days to 1 month after the event.","PTSD: symptoms lasting more than 1 month (can have a delayed onset, 6 months or more later).","Adjustment disorder: emotional or behavioral symptoms within 3 months of a stressor (not necessarily a trauma: divorce, job loss, a move) that are out of proportion; they resolve within 6 months after the stressor ends.","The same kinds of symptoms can appear in all three; timing and the type of event tell them apart."]},{"title":"Trauma in children","lines":["Young children reenact the trauma in play, have frightening dreams without clear content, and regress (bedwetting, thumb-sucking).","Reactive attachment disorder: withdrawn, rarely seeks comfort, after severe neglect.","Disinhibited social engagement disorder: overly friendly with strangers, after neglect.","ACEs (adverse childhood experiences) raise adult risk of heart disease, lung disease, substance use, depression and suicide."]},{"title":"Dissociative disorders","lines":["Dissociation: a disruption of memory, identity, consciousness or perception; a way the mind escapes unbearable experience.","Depersonalization/derealization: feeling detached from oneself (“watching myself in a movie”) or that the world is unreal; reality testing stays intact.","Dissociative amnesia: inability to recall important personal information, usually about a trauma; may include fugue (sudden travel with confusion about identity).","Dissociative identity disorder (DID): two or more distinct identities (alters) with gaps in memory; almost always linked to severe childhood abuse; high suicide and self-harm risk."]}]},{"id":"c-ptsd","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Combat trauma, plus symptoms in all four clusters lasting more than 1 month, with impairment = PTSD. Complicating risks: alcohol use, emotional withdrawal from family, and a loaded firearm in a man with hyperarousal and possible depression."]},{"title":"What trauma does to the brain and body","lines":["Amygdala (alarm) becomes overactive, so it reacts to reminders.","Hippocampus (filing memories in time and place) shrinks, so memories feel present instead of past.","Prefrontal cortex (calming and reasoning) is less active, so it can’t switch off the alarm.","HPA axis dysregulated: altered cortisol, chronic sympathetic arousal.","Trauma is “stored in the body”: headaches, GI symptoms, chronic pain."]},{"title":"Who is at risk","lines":["Severity, duration and closeness to the trauma; interpersonal trauma (assault, abuse) carries more risk than disasters.","Previous trauma, ACEs, prior mental illness, female sex.","Lack of social support after the event (support is the strongest protective factor)."]}]},{"id":"c-ptsd","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priorities: safety (suicide risk, firearm access, impulsive anger), then sleep disturbance and substance use, then anxiety, ineffective coping and family processes. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing the trauma, then reconnection."]},{"title":"Phase model of trauma treatment","lines":["1 · Safety and stabilization: physical safety, sleep, coping and grounding skills, sobriety.","2 · Processing: working through the trauma memories in therapy (exposure, CPT, EMDR).","3 · Reconnection: rebuilding relationships, roles and meaning.","Processing trauma before the person can manage distress can overwhelm them, so skills come first."]}]},{"id":"c-ptsd","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: he stays safe (the firearm is stored locked and unloaded, and he reports any suicidal thoughts); he sleeps 6 or more hours with fewer nightmares; he uses grounding and relaxation when triggered; he reduces his drinking; he engages in trauma-focused therapy; and he reconnects with his family."]}]},{"id":"c-ptsd","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Use trauma-informed care: safety, trust, choice, collaboration and empowerment, and ask “what happened to you?”, not “what’s wrong with you?”. Stay calm and predictable; don’t push for details of the trauma. Teach grounding and relaxation. Address sleep, alcohol and the firearm. Refer for trauma-focused CBT or EMDR. Give medications as ordered."]},{"title":"Nursing interventions","lines":["Introduce yourself and explain each thing before you do it; ask before you touch.","Let him tell the story at his own pace; don’t force him to relive it.","During a flashback or dissociation: stay calm, use his name, orient him (“You’re at the clinic, it’s 2026, you’re safe”), and use grounding (feet on the floor, cold water, naming 5 things he can see).","Teach sleep hygiene and relaxation; screen and counsel on alcohol.","Involve family; refer to veteran and peer support groups.","Assess for suicide and means."]},{"title":"Therapies","lines":["Trauma-focused CBT: prolonged exposure (gradually approaching memories and avoided situations) and cognitive processing therapy (challenging beliefs such as “It was my fault”).","EMDR (eye movement desensitization and reprocessing): recalling the trauma while doing bilateral eye movements or taps, to reprocess the memory.","Group therapy and peer support; crisis intervention right after a trauma."]},{"title":"Medications","lines":["SSRIs (sertraline and paroxetine are FDA approved) and venlafaxine: first-line medication.","Prazosin (an alpha-1 blocker) for nightmares; watch for orthostatic hypotension.","Avoid benzodiazepines (they don’t treat PTSD, they interfere with therapy, and the risk of dependence is high).","Medication eases symptoms; psychotherapy is the treatment of choice."]},{"title":"Caring for a patient with DID","lines":["Build trust with a consistent approach; keep the environment safe (high risk of self-harm).","Accept the alters without encouraging them, and keep focusing on the whole person.","Teach grounding; help with memory gaps using a journal.","Long-term psychotherapy aims at integration."]}]},{"id":"c-ptsd","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Track nightmares and sleep hours, startle and irritability, avoidance (is he going places again?), alcohol use, his use of grounding skills, family relationships, and suicide risk at every visit."]},{"title":"Signs care is working","lines":["Fewer nightmares and better sleep after prazosin and the SSRI.","Went to a store with his wife; stayed 20 minutes using breathing.","Drinking less; attending therapy weekly.","The firearm is stored locked and unloaded, or out of the home."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-ptsd:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A veteran has nightmares, flashbacks, an exaggerated startle response and avoids crowds 4 months after deployment. Which diagnosis fits the timing?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Acute stress disorder","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Trauma symptoms lasting more than 1 month = PTSD. Acute stress disorder is 3 days to 1 month."},{"id":"o1","text":"Posttraumatic stress disorder","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Trauma symptoms lasting more than 1 month = PTSD. Acute stress disorder is 3 days to 1 month."},{"id":"o2","text":"Adjustment disorder","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Trauma symptoms lasting more than 1 month = PTSD. Acute stress disorder is 3 days to 1 month."},{"id":"o3","text":"Generalized anxiety disorder","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Trauma symptoms lasting more than 1 month = PTSD. Acute stress disorder is 3 days to 1 month."}]},{"id":"c-ptsd:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"26-year-old veteran seen in clinic 3 months after returning from deployment. Wakes from nightmares drenched in sweat, startles at loud noises, won’t go to crowded stores, drinks “to sleep,” and says he feels “numb” around his wife and kids. Last week he dove behind a car when a truck backfired.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priorities: safety (suicide risk, firearm access, impulsive anger), then sleep disturbance and substance use, then anxiety, ineffective coping and family processes. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization first, then processing the trauma, then reconnection.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-ptsd:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"26-year-old veteran seen in clinic 3 months after returning from deployment. Wakes from nightmares drenched in sweat, startles at loud noises, won’t go to crowded stores, drinks “to sleep,” and says he feels “numb” around his wife and kids. Last week he dove behind a car when a truck backfired.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Use trauma-informed care: safety, trust, choice, collaboration and empowerment, and ask “what happened to you?”, not “what’s wrong with you?”. Stay calm and predictable; don’t push for details of the trauma. Teach grounding and relaxation. Address sleep, alcohol and the firearm. Refer for trauma-focused CBT or EMDR. Give medications as ordered. Track nightmares and sleep hours, startle and irritability, avoidance (is he going places again?), alcohol use, his use of grounding skills, family relationships, and suicide risk at every visit.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""}]}};
